export const FNH_P2_UPLOADED_WORKED_REFERENCE=Object.freeze({"2005":{"1":"(a) THREE benefits of eating dietary fibre. (3)\n    Prevents constipation by adding bulk and softness to the faeces.\n    Helps weight control, because fibre is filling and low in calories.\n    Lowers blood cholesterol and helps control blood sugar, and reduces the risk of bowel cancer, piles and\n     diverticular disease.\n\n(b) THREE causes of obesity. (3)\n    Eating more energy (calories) than the body uses, especially from fatty and sugary foods.\n    Lack of physical activity (a sedentary lifestyle).\n    Genetic or family factors, hormonal problems (underactive thyroid), emotional eating, or some medications.\n\n(c)(i) FOUR nutritional reasons why liver and orange juice taken together will \"enrich one's blood\". (4)\n    Liver is one of the richest sources of iron, which is needed to make haemoglobin, the red pigment in blood\n     that carries oxygen.\n    The iron in liver is haem iron, the form the body absorbs most easily.\n    Orange juice is rich in vitamin C, which increases the absorption of iron (especially non-haem iron) from the\n     meal, so more iron reaches the blood.\n    Liver also supplies vitamin B12, folic acid and protein, which are needed to make new red blood cells.\n     Together these help prevent and treat anaemia.\n\n(c)(ii) FOUR nutritional reasons why lacto-vegetarians can have a healthy diet. (4)\n    Milk, cheese and yogurt supply high-quality protein, with all the essential amino acids.\n    Dairy foods supply plenty of calcium, plus vitamin B12 and riboflavin, which are nutrients strict vegans often\n     lack.\n    Combining cereals with legumes (rice and peas) gives complementary proteins, and legumes, nuts and green\n     vegetables supply iron.\n    The diet is high in fibre, vitamins and minerals from fruits, vegetables and wholegrains, and low in saturated\n     fat and cholesterol, reducing the risk of heart disease, obesity and constipation.\n\n(d) TWO symptoms Charles may have with high blood sugar. (2)\n    Frequent urination and excessive thirst\n    Tiredness, blurred vision, constant hunger, or slow-healing sores","2":"(a) Define \"dehydration\" in food preservation. (2)\n    Dehydration is preserving food by removing most of its water, using sun-drying, oven-drying or a dehydrator.\n    Micro-organisms need moisture to grow and enzymes need water to act, so dried food keeps for a long time.\n    Examples: raisins, dried sorrel, and salted fish.\n\n(b) FOUR reasons for preserving food. (4)\n\n\n    To prevent spoilage and waste.\n    To make seasonal foods available all year.\n    To save money (by using surplus or cheap seasonal produce) and to earn income from selling preserves.\n    To add variety to the diet, and to make food easier to store and transport.\n\n(c) TWO reasons for each guideline. (6)\n    (i) Blanch pigeon peas before freezing: (1) to destroy (inactivate) enzymes that would otherwise cause loss of\n     colour, flavour, texture and vitamins during freezer storage; (2) to kill surface micro-organisms and keep the\n     peas a bright green colour. It also shrinks them slightly, so they pack more easily.\n    (ii) Add vitamins A and D to margarine: (1) to make margarine as nutritious as butter, which naturally\n     contains these vitamins (margarine made from vegetable oil has little); (2) to help prevent deficiency diseases\n     in the population, since many people use margarine daily: vitamin A prevents night blindness, and vitamin D\n     prevents rickets.\n    (iii) Add colouring to certain foods: (1) to replace natural colour lost during processing (for example canned\n     peas or strawberry jam); (2) to make foods look more attractive and appetizing, and to keep the colour the\n     same from batch to batch.\n\n(d) TWO factors that may have caused each situation. (4)\n    (i) Whitney's guava jam was thick and sticky: (1) it was boiled for too long (over-boiled) past setting point, so\n     too much water evaporated; (2) too much sugar was used for the amount of fruit, or the fruit had too much\n     pectin and not enough liquid was added.\n    (ii) Mariah's mango jam had fermented: (1) too little sugar was used (below about 60%), so it was not\n     concentrated enough to stop yeasts growing, or the jam was under-boiled; (2) the jars were not sterilized or\n     not sealed airtight, or over-ripe or damaged fruit was used, or the jam was stored in a warm, damp place.","3":"(a) Define (i) herbs and (ii) spices. (4)\n    (i) Herbs: the fresh or dried leaves (and sometimes the soft stems) of certain aromatic plants, used to add\n    flavour to food. Examples: thyme, parsley, basil, mint, chives.\n    (ii) Spices: dried aromatic parts of plants other than the leaves, such as seeds, bark, roots, buds, berries or\n    fruits, used to flavour food. They are usually stronger in flavour. Examples: nutmeg (seed), cinnamon (bark),\n    ginger (root), cloves (bud), black pepper and pimento/allspice (berries).\n\n(b) TWO guidelines for using garnishes. (2)\n    Garnishes must be edible, fresh, and suit (complement) the dish in flavour and colour.\n    Keep them simple, do not overcrowd the dish, and add them just before serving so they stay fresh.\n\n(c)(i) THREE guidelines when making the salad. (3)\n    Wash the vegetables thoroughly under clean running water to remove soil, insects and chemicals, and dry\n     them well (a soggy salad is unappetizing).\n    Prepare the salad just before serving, using a sharp stainless steel knife, and tear leafy vegetables rather than\n     cutting them, to keep vitamin C.\n    Use a variety of colours, shapes and textures (lettuce, tomato, cucumber, carrots, sweet peppers). Keep it\n     crisp and cold, and add the dressing at the last minute.\n\n(c)(ii) THREE guidelines when serving the salad. (3)\n\n\n    Serve it chilled and crisp, in a clean, attractive bowl or on a bed of lettuce.\n    Add the dressing just before serving, or serve it separately, so the salad does not wilt.\n    Garnish attractively (tomato roses, radish flowers, egg slices), and serve it straight away. Do not leave it at\n     room temperature for long.\n\n(d) ONE way to avoid each fault. (4)\n    (i) Cocoa with a raw flavour: blend the cocoa powder with a little cold milk or water to a smooth paste, then\n    add the hot liquid and boil the mixture for 1–2 minutes, stirring. The starch in the cocoa must be cooked\n    (gelatinized) to remove the raw taste.\n    (ii) Bitter tea: use freshly boiled water and infuse (brew) the tea for only 3–5 minutes, then remove the leaves\n    or tea bags, or pour the tea off the leaves. Do not boil the tea or leave it standing on the leaves, because this\n    releases bitter tannins. Use the correct amount of tea.","4":"(a) THREE conditions in which the body needs extra water. (3)\n    Hot weather, and heavy physical activity or exercise (loss through sweating).\n    Fever, diarrhoea or vomiting (illness causing fluid loss).\n    Breastfeeding (lactation) and pregnancy; also accept a high-fibre or high-protein diet.\n\n(b) THREE functions of fat in the diet. (3)\n    A concentrated source of energy (9 kcal per gram).\n    Supplies the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K, and essential fatty acids.\n    Insulates the body (keeping it warm) and protects vital organs. Fat also adds flavour and texture to food, and\n     gives a feeling of fullness (satiety).\n\n(c)(i) THREE ways each nutrient contributes to good health in the elderly. (6)\n    Calcium: (1) helps keep bones strong and slows bone loss, reducing the risk of osteoporosis and fractures\n     from falls; (2) keeps teeth (and gums) healthy; (3) needed for normal muscle contraction (including the\n     heartbeat), nerve function and blood clotting.\n    Vitamin A: (1) maintains good eyesight, especially in dim light (prevents night blindness); (2) keeps the skin\n     and the linings of the throat, lungs and gut healthy; (3) strengthens the immune system, helping resist\n     infections, which the elderly catch more easily.\n\n(c)(ii) A THREE-course meal for an elderly person, rich in calcium and vitamin A. (4)\n    Starter: cream of pumpkin soup made with milk (vitamin A from the pumpkin, calcium from the milk)\n    Main course: baked or steamed fish (or flaked sardines in tomato sauce, soft enough to eat the bones)\n    (calcium), mashed sweet potato (vitamin A), steamed callaloo and carrots (vitamin A, calcium)\n    Dessert: baked egg custard, or mango and yogurt (calcium, vitamin A)\n    Drink: carrot juice made with milk (vitamin A, calcium)\n    All the dishes are soft and easy to chew, and low in salt and fat, which suits an elderly person.","5":"(a) THREE desirable features of each item. (6)\n\n\n    (i) Stainless steel saucepan or pot: (1) a heavy, thick base (often with a copper or aluminium core) for even\n     heat and no burning; (2) a well-fitting lid and strong, heat-resistant, securely attached handles; (3) smooth,\n     rust-proof and easy to clean, with no cracks, and the right size for the family.\n    (ii) Free-standing mixer: (1) a powerful motor with several speeds; (2) a range of attachments (beater, whisk,\n     dough hook) and a large stainless steel bowl; (3) a stable, heavy base, safety features, ease of cleaning, and a\n     good guarantee and after-sales service.\n\n(b)(i) TWO benefits and ONE adjustment with the new upright freezer. (3)\n    Benefit 1: food is easier to see, organize and reach, because it is stored on shelves and in door racks. There is\n     no need to bend over and dig to the bottom as in a chest freezer.\n    Benefit 2: it takes up less floor space, and many upright models are frost-free, so they need less defrosting.\n    Adjustment: cold air falls out each time the door is opened, so the temperature rises faster than in a chest\n     freezer. She must open the door less often and close it quickly. She may also have to get used to smaller\n     storage space for large or bulky items, and higher electricity use.\n\n(b)(ii) FIVE guidelines for storing food in the new freezer. (5)\n    Wrap or pack all food in airtight, moisture-proof freezer bags or containers, removing as much air as possible,\n     to prevent freezer burn.\n    Label every package with its contents and the date frozen, and use the oldest food first (first in, first out).\n    Cool cooked food completely before freezing, and freeze in meal-sized portions.\n    Keep the temperature at −18°C or below, and do not overload the freezer with unfrozen food all at once.\n    Blanch vegetables before freezing, never refreeze food that has thawed (unless it has been cooked), and\n     defrost and clean the freezer regularly.\n\n(c) Explain how to treat the scald from hot tea. (2)\n    Hold the hand under cool running water immediately, for at least 10–20 minutes, to cool the skin, reduce pain\n    and stop the burn going deeper. Remove any rings or watches before swelling starts. Then cover the area\n    loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing or cling film. Do not put butter, oil or toothpaste on it, and do not burst\n    blisters. Seek medical help if the scald is large or the skin is broken.","6":"(a) Define ONE term. (2)\n    (i) Smoke point: the temperature at which a fat or oil begins to break down and give off a continuous thin,\n    bluish smoke when heated. Oils with a high smoke point (groundnut or corn oil) are best for frying.\n    (ii) Rancidity: the spoilage of fats and oils, causing an unpleasant smell and taste. It is caused either by oxygen\n    reacting with the fat (oxidative rancidity, speeded up by light, heat and metals) or by enzymes or moisture\n    breaking down the fat (hydrolytic rancidity).\n\n(b) The MAIN difference between processing dried (powdered) milk and condensed milk. (2)\n    For dried milk, almost all the water is removed (to about 3–5% moisture) by spray-drying or roller-drying,\n    leaving a powder. For condensed milk, only part of the water is evaporated (about 60%) and sugar is added\n    (about 40–45%), leaving a thick, sweet liquid that is preserved by the high sugar content.\n\n(c) TWO disadvantages of buying food in bulk. (2)\n    Perishable foods may spoil before they are used, so money is wasted.\n\n\n    A large sum of money is needed at once, and you need enough storage space. Also accept: the family may get\n     tired of the same food, or overuse it.\n\n(d) Explain how a suitable raising agent works in TWO of the items. (4)\n    (i) Swiss roll: air (whisking). The eggs and sugar are whisked together until thick and pale, trapping many tiny\n    air bubbles. In the oven the air expands with heat, which raises the sponge, and then the egg protein\n    coagulates around the bubbles, setting the light, open texture.\n    (ii) Pancakes: steam (and air). The thin batter contains a large proportion of liquid. When it hits the hot pan,\n    the water turns into steam, which expands about 1,600 times in volume and pushes the batter up, making the\n    pancake light. Air beaten into the batter also helps. (Thick pancakes also use baking powder.)\n    (iii) Chocolate cake: chemical raising agent (baking powder or bicarbonate of soda). Baking powder contains\n    an alkali (bicarbonate of soda) and an acid (cream of tartar). When moistened and heated, they react to give\n    off carbon dioxide gas. The bubbles expand in the oven and raise the cake, and the heat then sets the flour and\n    egg structure. (Bicarbonate of soda reacts with the acid in cocoa or buttermilk in the same way.)\n\n(e) THREE changes during each process. (6)\n    (i) Boiling porridge (for example oats or cornmeal): (1) the starch granules absorb the liquid and swell; (2)\n     the granules burst and release starch, which thickens the mixture into a smooth paste (gelatinization), at\n     about 60–80°C; (3) the cereal softens and becomes easier to digest, and the raw flavour disappears. Some B\n     vitamins pass into the liquid, but they are eaten with it.\n    (ii) Scrambling an egg: (1) the egg proteins (albumin and the yolk proteins) coagulate (set) with heat,\n     changing from liquid to solid; (2) the colour changes: the clear white becomes opaque, and the mixture turns\n     pale yellow; (3) stirring breaks the setting egg into soft, creamy curds. If overcooked, the protein shrinks,\n     becomes rubbery, and squeezes out water (syneresis), so the egg becomes watery and tough.","7":"(a) Explain the principle of (i) pressure cooking and (ii) microwave cooking. (6)\n    (i) Pressure cooking: the pan is sealed with a locking lid and a rubber gasket, so steam cannot escape. As the\n    steam builds up, the pressure inside rises (to about 15 psi above normal). At higher pressure water boils at a\n    higher temperature, about 121°C instead of 100°C. The food therefore cooks much faster (in about a third of\n    the time), and tough fibres soften more quickly. A safety valve controls the pressure.\n    (ii) Microwave cooking: a device called a magnetron produces microwaves (high-frequency electromagnetic\n    waves). These pass into the food (to a depth of about 2–4 cm) and make the water molecules in the food\n    vibrate (rotate) millions of times a second. The friction from this movement produces heat inside the food,\n    which cooks it. Heat then spreads to the centre by conduction. Microwaves pass through glass, china and\n    plastic, but are reflected by metal.\n\n(b)(i) SIX ways, apart from labour-saving equipment, to save time and fuel when preparing a luncheon for 50\nstaff. (6)\n    Plan the menu carefully, choosing dishes that can be cooked in bulk and are not too elaborate.\n    Write a detailed time plan, and dovetail tasks so several jobs happen at once.\n    Do pre-preparation the day before: marinate meat, chop vegetables, make desserts and cakes, and set up\n     tables.\n    Cook several dishes in the oven at the same time, using all the shelves, and cook foods in large batches.\n\n\n   Use lids on pans, match pots to burner size, and turn the heat down once foods boil. Use residual heat by\n    switching off the oven or stove slightly early.\n   Divide tasks among class members according to their skills, collect all ingredients and equipment before\n    starting, and clean as you go.\n\n(b)(ii) FOUR guidelines for efficient buffet service. (4)\n   Place the buffet table where guests can move around it easily, with enough space for a queue. For 50 people,\n    serve from both sides of the table.\n   Arrange the food in a logical order: plates first, then main dishes, starches, vegetables and salads, sauces, and\n    cutlery and napkins last.\n   Provide a serving spoon for each dish, label dishes, and keep hot foods hot (chafing dishes) and cold foods\n    cold.\n   Have helpers replenish dishes, clear used plates, and keep the table tidy, and serve desserts and drinks at a\n    separate station to avoid congestion."},"2006":{"1":"(a) Define (i) undernutrition and (ii) diabetes. (4)\n    (i) Undernutrition: a form of malnutrition that results from eating too little food, or too little of one or more\n    nutrients (especially energy and protein), over a long period. It leads to weight loss, poor growth and deficiency\n    diseases.\n    (ii) Diabetes (diabetes mellitus): a long-term condition in which the level of glucose (sugar) in the blood is too\n    high, because the pancreas does not produce enough insulin (type 1), or the body cannot use its insulin\n    properly (type 2). Insulin is the hormone that lets glucose move from the blood into the cells.\n\n(b) TWO rules for planning meals for a convalescent. (2)\n    Serve light, easily digested food, cooked by steaming, poaching, baking or boiling (not fried, fatty or highly\n     spiced).\n    Include plenty of protein and vitamin C for repair and healing, give small, frequent, attractive meals with\n     plenty of fluids, and follow the doctor's instructions.\n\n(c) ONE symptom and ONE food to treat each condition. (6)\n    (i) Anaemia: symptom: pale skin and inner eyelids, with tiredness and breathlessness. Food: liver (also accept\n     red meat, callaloo, kidney beans).\n    (ii) Goitre: symptom: a swelling at the front of the neck (enlarged thyroid gland). Food: seafood (fish,\n     shellfish, seaweed) or iodized salt.\n    (iii) Osteomalacia: symptom: soft, weak bones with bone pain (especially in the hips, legs and back), and\n     muscle weakness. Food: oily fish such as sardines or salmon (vitamin D and calcium), milk, or fortified\n     margarine (also accept egg yolk and sunlight).\n\n(d) TWO health benefits and TWO difficulties of a strict vegetarian (vegan) diet. (4)\n    Benefits: (1) low in saturated fat and cholesterol, which lowers the risk of heart disease and high blood\n     pressure; (2) high in fibre, which prevents constipation and helps weight control.\n    Difficulties: (1) it is hard to get enough vitamin B12 (found only in animal foods), iron, calcium and vitamin D,\n     so there is a risk of anaemia and weak bones; (2) meals need careful planning to combine plant proteins so\n     they supply all the essential amino acids, and it can be hard to eat out or find suitable foods at social events.","2":"(a) FOUR reasons for using additives in food processing. (4)\n    To preserve food and extend its shelf life (preservatives, antioxidants).\n    To improve or restore colour (colourings).\n    To improve flavour (flavourings and flavour enhancers such as MSG, and sweeteners).\n    To improve texture and consistency (emulsifiers, stabilizers, thickeners), and to improve nutritive value\n     (fortification with vitamins and minerals).\n\n(b) TWO chemical preservatives. (2)\n    Sodium benzoate (also accept benzoic acid)\n\n\n    Sodium metabisulphite, sulphur dioxide, potassium sorbate, or sodium nitrite (also accept vinegar and salt)\n\n(c) Describe how to prepare fresh gungo (pigeon) peas for freezing. (6)\n    1. Select young, fresh, green, plump pods. Shell (remove) the peas, discard any that are discoloured or\n    damaged, and wash them in cold water.\n    2. Blanch the peas by plunging them into a large pot of rapidly boiling water for about 2 minutes (start timing\n    when the water returns to the boil). This destroys enzymes and surface bacteria and keeps the bright green\n    colour.\n    3. Cool them immediately in iced water for the same length of time, to stop the cooking.\n    4. Drain thoroughly and pat dry, so ice crystals do not form on the surface. (They can be open-frozen on a tray\n    first, so they stay free-flowing.)\n    5. Pack into airtight freezer bags or containers in family-sized portions, pressing out as much air as possible,\n    and seal tightly.\n    6. Label with the name and date, and freeze quickly at −18°C or below. Use within about 9–12 months.\n\n(d) THREE criteria for judging the quality of a finished bottle of pineapple jam. (3)\n    Appearance: bright, clear colour typical of the fruit, evenly distributed fruit pieces, and no crystals, mould or\n     bubbles.\n    Set (consistency): it should be firm enough to hold its shape when spooned, but spreadable (not runny,\n     rubbery or stiff).\n    Flavour: a good, fresh, fruity pineapple taste, not burnt or fermented. The jar should be clean, well sealed,\n     filled to the top and labelled.\n\n(e) ONE way Christine can remedy jam that has not set. (1)\n    Return it to the pan, add lime or lemon juice (acid) or commercial pectin, and boil rapidly again until it reaches\n    setting point (test with the wrinkle test, or 105°C on a thermometer).","3":"(a) Define \"conservative cooking\". (2)\n    Conservative cooking is a method of cooking vegetables in a very small amount of boiling water, in a pan with a\n    tight-fitting lid, for the shortest possible time. The cooking water is then used in soups or gravies. It conserves\n    (saves) the water-soluble vitamins (B and C), minerals, colour and flavour.\n\n(b) FOUR advantages of pressure cooking. (4)\n    It cooks food very quickly (about a third of the normal time).\n    It saves fuel, because of the shorter cooking time and because several foods can be cooked at once.\n    It tenderizes tough, cheaper cuts of meat and dried peas and beans.\n    It keeps more nutrients and flavour (less water and shorter cooking), and keeps the kitchen cooler and free of\n     steam.\n\n(c) FOUR factors that may have made the meat pies tough. (4)\n    Too much water was added to the pastry, which developed the gluten.\n    The dough was handled or kneaded too much, or rolled heavily.\n    The fat was not rubbed in enough, or the ingredients and hands were too warm, so the fat melted.\n\n\n    The pastry was stretched when rolling or covering the pies, or re-rolled too often, or baked at too low an oven\n     temperature, or too much flour was used for dusting.\n\n(d) Explain why fresh pineapple stops the gelatine mixture setting. (2)\n    Fresh pineapple contains the enzyme bromelain, which breaks down proteins. Gelatine is a protein, so the\n    bromelain digests it into smaller pieces that cannot form the network needed for a gel, and the mixture stays\n    liquid. Canned or cooked pineapple can be used instead, because heating destroys the enzyme.\n\n(e) FOUR qualities of a well-made cream of pumpkin soup. (4)\n    A smooth, creamy texture, with no lumps or fibres.\n    The correct consistency: like thin cream, coating the back of a spoon (not too thick or too watery).\n    A bright, attractive golden-orange colour, with a suitable garnish (cream swirl, croutons, chopped parsley).\n    A good, well-seasoned pumpkin flavour (not starchy, burnt or too salty), served piping hot.","4":"(a) THREE guidelines for planning meals for teenage girls. (3)\n    Include plenty of iron-rich foods (liver, red meat, legumes, dark-green vegetables) with vitamin C, to replace\n     iron lost in menstruation and prevent anaemia.\n    Include calcium-rich foods (milk, cheese, yogurt, sardines) for building peak bone mass.\n    Provide enough energy and protein for the growth spurt, but limit fatty, sugary snack foods to prevent\n     obesity and acne. Encourage regular meals, especially breakfast, and discourage crash dieting.\n\n(b) THREE problems that could affect the nutritional status of the elderly. (3)\n    Loss of teeth or poorly fitting dentures, making chewing difficult.\n    Low income, making nutritious food unaffordable.\n    Loneliness and depression, poor mobility (difficulty shopping and cooking), a reduced sense of taste and\n     smell, and illnesses or medications that affect appetite or absorption.\n\n(c) FOUR ways to encourage toddlers to eat nutritious meals. (4)\n    Serve small portions on colourful plates, and make food look fun (shapes, faces, bright colours).\n    Introduce new foods one at a time, alongside foods they like, and offer them several times without forcing.\n    Let toddlers feed themselves with finger foods, and involve them in simple tasks such as washing fruit.\n    Eat together as a family in a relaxed setting, set a good example, give regular meal times, and avoid filling up\n     on sweets and juice between meals.\n\n(d) Describe the changes in a slice of red snapper when it is baked. (6)\n    The protein coagulates. As the fish heats (from about 60°C), the protein in the muscle sets. The flesh changes\n    from translucent and glossy to opaque white, and becomes firm.\n    The connective tissue turns to gelatin, so the flesh flakes. Fish has very little connective tissue (collagen), and\n    it is easily changed into gelatin by heat. This lets the muscle fibres separate into flakes, so the fish becomes\n    tender and cooks quickly. Over-baking makes it dry and break up.\n    Shrinkage, moisture loss and flavour changes. As the protein sets it squeezes out some of the juices (water\n    and soluble nutrients), so the fish shrinks slightly. The surface may brown a little and develop flavour, any\n    micro-organisms are destroyed, and the skin becomes crisp if left on.","5":"(a) The micro-organism that causes each change. (3)\n    (i) Curdling of milk: bacteria (lactic acid bacteria).\n    (ii) Spoilage of bread: moulds.\n    (iii) Fermentation of a mango drink: yeasts.\n\n(b) THREE guidelines for storing food in a refrigerator. (3)\n    Cover all foods to stop them drying out and to stop smells passing between them.\n    Cool hot food before putting it in, and do not overload the fridge, so cold air can circulate.\n    Store raw meat and fish on the bottom shelf (so juices cannot drip on other food), use foods in date order,\n     keep the temperature between 1°C and 5°C, and clean the fridge regularly.\n\n(c)(i) THREE symptoms the affected students may have had. (3)\n    Nausea and vomiting\n    Diarrhoea\n    Stomach cramps (abdominal pain), with fever and headache\n\n(c)(ii) TWO kitchen hygiene and TWO personal hygiene practices the caterers may not have followed. (4)\n    Kitchen hygiene: (1) using separate chopping boards and utensils for raw and cooked chicken (cross-\n     contamination); (2) keeping cooked chicken hot (above 63°C) or refrigerated, not at room temperature for\n     hours. Also accept: cooking the chicken thoroughly, and cleaning work surfaces.\n    Personal hygiene: (1) washing hands properly after the toilet and after handling raw chicken; (2) wearing\n     clean clothes and hair coverings, covering cuts, and not handling food while ill.\n\n(d) THREE guidelines for caterers buying fresh vegetables safely. (3)\n    Buy from clean, reputable suppliers or markets with good hygiene.\n    Choose vegetables that are fresh, firm and crisp, with no bruises, mould, slime, wilting or insect damage.\n    Avoid produce that is dirty, kept on the ground, or exposed to flies and contamination. Wash all vegetables\n     thoroughly in clean water before use, and store them properly.","6":"(a) Define \"impulse buying\". (2)\n    Impulse buying is buying items without planning to, on the spur of the moment, usually because of attractive\n    displays, advertising, packaging or special offers. Often these are things that are not needed.\n\n(b) FOUR ways a family can spend money wisely for good nutrition. (4)\n    Plan meals for the week and shop with a list and a budget.\n    Buy local foods in season, which are cheaper and more nutritious.\n    Use cheaper sources of protein (legumes, eggs, chicken, canned fish) and combine cereals with legumes.\n    Compare prices and unit prices, buy store brands, buy in bulk when cheaper, and avoid convenience foods\n     and junk snacks.\n\n(c) FOUR items of label information for the sweet bun package. (4)\n    Name of the product (\"Sweet Buns\")\n    List of ingredients (in descending order of weight)\n    Net weight or number of buns\n\n\n    Best-before date, plus the name of the producer (school, Food and Nutrition Department) and the price\n\n(d) ONE factor that caused each result. (6)\n    (i) Dark greyish-green ring around the yolk of over-boiled eggs: cooking too long causes the sulphur in the egg\n    white to form hydrogen sulphide gas, which reacts with the iron in the yolk to form iron sulphide, which is\n    greyish-green. This is worse if the eggs are not cooled quickly in cold water after boiling.\n    (ii) Over-baked macaroni pie with a tough, greasy, dry topping: the long, high heat over-coagulated the\n    protein in the cheese (and egg), which became tough and rubbery and squeezed out its fat, so the surface\n    became greasy, while moisture evaporated and the top dried out.\n    (iii) Lumpy roux-based white sauce: the milk was added too quickly, or all at once, without stirring\n    constantly, so the starch grains clumped together and gelatinized in lumps instead of separating evenly. (Also\n    accept: the roux was not cooked, or the sauce was not stirred while coming to the boil.)","7":"(a) Define (i) buffet, (ii) brunch and (iii) cover. (6)\n    (i) Buffet: a style of service in which a variety of foods is set out on a table, and guests help themselves (or are\n    served from it), then eat seated or standing. It is good for serving large numbers quickly.\n    (ii) Brunch: a meal that combines breakfast and lunch, eaten in the late morning, with both breakfast and\n    lunch-type foods.\n    (iii) Cover: the place setting for one person at a table, about 60 cm wide, with all the cutlery, crockery,\n    glassware and napkin needed for the meal.\n\n(b) FOUR guidelines for refurbishing ONE area of the Food Laboratory. (4)\n    (i) Counter-tops:\n    Use a hard, smooth, non-porous, heat-resistant surface such as stainless steel, granite or laminate, which is\n     easy to clean and does not harbour bacteria.\n    Fit them at a comfortable working height (about 90 cm), so students do not have to bend.\n    Make them continuous, with no cracks or gaps where food and germs can collect, and with rounded edges for\n     safety.\n    Provide enough counter space beside the cookers and sinks, with electrical outlets placed away from water.\n    (ii) Floors (alternative): use non-slip, waterproof, hard-wearing material (non-slip ceramic tiles or vinyl); make\n    it easy to clean, with coved (curved) edges where floor meets wall; slope it towards a floor drain; and choose a\n    light colour, so dirt is easy to see.\n\n(c) A dinner menu card for a three-course à la carte or table d'hôte meal, with at least THREE items per course.\n(6)\n    THE BLUE MAHOE RESTAURANT · Table d'Hôte Dinner · $3,500 per person\n    Starters (choose one): cream of pumpkin soup · fresh fruit cocktail · saltfish fritters with a lime dip\n    Main course (choose one), served with rice and peas or garlic mashed potatoes, glazed carrots and a garden\n    salad: roast chicken with thyme gravy · grilled snapper in lemon butter sauce · curried goat\n    Desserts (choose one): sweet potato pudding · chocolate mousse · coconut ice cream with fresh mango\n    Coffee, tea or sorrel\n    (For an à la carte card, each dish would carry its own separate price instead of one price for the meal.)"},"2008":{"1":"(a) Define \"food fallacy\". (2)\n    A food fallacy is a false or mistaken belief about food or nutrition that many people accept as true, although it\n    is not supported by scientific evidence. For example, \"beetroot builds blood\" or \"eating fish and drinking milk\n    together is poisonous\".\n\n(b)(i) ONE nutritional disorder in young children caused by a lack of protein and energy. (1)\n    Kwashiorkor (also accept marasmus; together they are protein-energy malnutrition, PEM).\n\n(b)(ii) THREE signs of a deficiency of protein and energy in young children. (3)\n    Stunted growth and being underweight; in marasmus, severe wasting (\"skin and bones\").\n    Oedema (swollen belly, legs and face) in kwashiorkor.\n    Thin, patchy, discoloured (reddish) hair and dry, peeling skin; also accept: apathy, misery, frequent infections,\n     and an enlarged liver.\n\n(c) FOUR guidelines for preparing meals for diabetic residents. (4)\n    Limit sugar and sugary foods. Use sugar substitutes and fresh fruit for desserts.\n    Use high-fibre complex carbohydrates (wholegrain bread, brown rice, ground provisions, legumes) in\n     controlled portions, instead of refined starches.\n    Reduce fat and salt. Bake, steam or grill instead of frying, because diabetics are at higher risk of heart disease\n     and hypertension.\n    Serve regular meals at set times in suitable portions, and include plenty of non-starchy vegetables, to keep\n     blood sugar steady.\n\n(d) A disease or condition affecting each family member, and the nutrient likely to be lacking. (6)\n    (i) Father cannot see well at night: condition: night blindness. Nutrient lacking: vitamin A.\n    (ii) Mother broke her arm in several places (fragile bones): condition: osteoporosis (also accept\n     osteomalacia). Nutrient lacking: calcium (and/or vitamin D).\n    (iii) Daughter has a swollen gland on her neck: condition: goitre (enlarged thyroid gland). Nutrient lacking:\n     iodine.","2":"(a) Define \"dehydration\" in food preservation. (2)\n    Dehydration (drying) is a method of preserving food by removing most of its water, using the sun, a warm oven\n    or a dehydrator. Without water, micro-organisms cannot grow and enzymes cannot act, so the food keeps for a\n    long time. Examples are raisins, dried herbs and salted fish.\n\n(b) An additive used in each process. (4)\n    (i) Increasing the nutritive value of margarine: vitamins A and D.\n    (ii) Preserving fresh fish: salt (also accept ice, or chemical preservatives such as sodium benzoate or nitrites,\n     or smoke).\n\n\n    (iii) Stabilizing the oil in mayonnaise: an emulsifier, such as lecithin (from egg yolk).\n    (iv) Tenderizing meat before cooking: papain (from green pawpaw), or a commercial meat tenderizer, or\n     bromelain (from pineapple), or vinegar or lime juice.\n\n(c)(i) Explain what caused the bananas and apples to discolour. (2)\n    Enzymic browning. When the fruits were peeled and cut, the cells were damaged and released enzymes\n    (polyphenol oxidase). These reacted with oxygen in the air and with phenolic compounds in the fruit, forming\n    brown pigments.\n\n(c)(ii) How the discolouration could have been avoided. (2)\n    Toss the cut fruit in lemon or lime juice (or pineapple juice). The acid slows the enzyme action.\n    Prepare the fruit just before serving, cut it with a stainless steel knife, and cover it with syrup or keep it\n     submerged so air cannot reach it.\n\n(d) TWO possible causes of each jam fault. (6)\n    (i) The jam had not set: (1) too little pectin or acid in the fruit (over-ripe fruit), or too much water; (2) not\n     boiled long enough to reach setting point (105°C), or too little sugar used (also accept boiling for too long,\n     which destroys pectin).\n    (ii) Sugar crystals were present: (1) too much sugar, or the jam was over-boiled so it became too\n     concentrated; (2) the sugar was not completely dissolved before boiling began, or there was not enough acid\n     to invert some of the sugar.\n    (iii) Signs of fermentation: (1) too little sugar (under about 60%), so yeasts could grow; (2) the jars were not\n     sterilized, or not sealed airtight, or the jam was stored in a warm, damp place, or under-ripe or damaged fruit\n     was used.","3":"(a)(i) Define (a) poach and (b) steep (infuse). (4)\n    a) Poach: to cook food gently in a small amount of liquid (water, milk or stock) kept just below boiling point\n    (about 70–80°C), so the surface barely moves. Used for eggs, fish and fruit, to keep them tender and whole.\n    b) Steep (infuse): to soak a substance in hot (or cold) liquid without boiling, so that its flavour, colour or other\n    qualities pass into the liquid. Examples are steeping tea leaves or sorrel in hot water, or infusing milk with\n    herbs or vanilla.\n\n(a)(ii) TWO disadvantages of grilling. (2)\n    Only tender, good-quality (and more expensive) cuts of meat can be grilled, and the food must be watched\n     constantly or it will burn.\n    Food can dry out if overcooked, fat spitting can cause burns or fires, and the grill pan is difficult to clean.\n\n(b) TWO precautions Rihanna should have taken so the cherries did not sink. (2)\n    Wash the glacé cherries to remove the sticky syrup, dry them thoroughly, cut them in halves or quarters, and\n     toss them in a little of the measured flour before adding them.\n    Make the cake mixture fairly stiff (not too soft or wet), and do not use too much raising agent, so the mixture\n     can hold the fruit in place.\n\n(c)(i) The most appropriate batter for frying saltfish fritters. (1)\n    Fritter batter (a thick batter, sometimes called a coating or drop batter).\n\n\n(c)(ii) TWO garnishes for the fritters. (2)\n    Lime or lemon wedges (butterflies)\n    Sprigs of parsley or chopped chives (also accept tomato roses or cucumber twists)\n\n(c)(iii) TWO ways to make boiled white rice more attractive. (2)\n    Add colour: cooked peas, diced carrots and sweet peppers, or turmeric or saffron for yellow rice.\n    Mould the rice in a cup or ring mould before turning it out, and garnish with chopped parsley. Also accept\n     cooking it in coconut milk or stock for flavour.\n\n(d) THREE ways to use milk in sweet and savoury dishes so Maisha will enjoy it. (3)\n    Sweet: banana or fruit smoothie, or milkshake.\n    Sweet: baked egg custard, rice pudding, or cornmeal porridge.\n    Savoury: white (béchamel) sauce in macaroni and cheese pie, cream soups (cream of pumpkin), or creamed\n     potatoes.","4":"(a) Define \"invisible fat\", with ONE example. (2)\n    Invisible fat is fat hidden inside a food that cannot easily be seen or removed. Examples: the fat in egg yolk,\n    cheese, avocado, nuts, whole milk, or cakes and pastries.\n\n(b) FOUR advantages of breastfeeding. (4)\n    Breast milk contains all the nutrients a baby needs for the first 6 months, in the right amounts, and is easily\n     digested.\n    It contains antibodies that protect the baby from infections, diarrhoea and allergies.\n    It is clean, at the right temperature, always available, and free (no bottles or formula to buy).\n    It builds the bond between mother and baby, helps the mother's womb return to normal size, helps her lose\n     pregnancy weight, and lowers her risk of breast and ovarian cancer.\n\n(c) FOUR preparation practices to conserve the nutritive value of vegetables. (4)\n    Prepare vegetables just before cooking. Wash them before cutting, and do not soak them.\n    Peel thinly (or not at all), and cut into fairly large pieces with a sharp knife.\n    Cook in a small amount of boiling water with the lid on, for the shortest time (conservative method), or steam\n     or stir-fry them. Do not add bicarbonate of soda.\n    Serve immediately, without keeping them hot for a long time, and use the cooking water in soups or gravies.\n\n(d) TWO reasons for each dietary requirement. (6)\n    (i) Lactating mothers need extra protein: (1) to produce breast milk, which contains protein for the baby's\n     growth; (2) to repair the mother's own tissues after childbirth and maintain her health.\n    (ii) Teenage girls need extra iron: (1) to replace the iron lost in blood during monthly menstruation; (2) to\n     support rapid growth during adolescence (more muscle and blood volume), and so prevent anaemia.\n    (iii) Convalescents need extra vitamin C: (1) to help heal wounds and repair tissues (vitamin C is needed to\n     make collagen); (2) to strengthen the immune system and fight infection, and to help absorb the iron needed\n     to replace any blood lost.","5":"(a)(i) THREE factors, other than poor personal hygiene, that contaminate food in the kitchen. (3)\n\n\n    Pests (flies, cockroaches, rodents)\n    Dirty equipment, work surfaces and dish cloths, and cross-contamination between raw and cooked foods\n    Food stored at the wrong temperature or left uncovered, and poor waste disposal (also accept: chemicals\n     such as cleaning agents stored near food)\n\n(a)(ii) THREE ways to prevent falls in the food preparation area. (3)\n    Wipe up spills of water, grease or food immediately.\n    Keep floors clear of objects (boxes, bags, toys) and wear flat, closed, non-slip shoes.\n    Use non-slip flooring, use a sturdy step-ladder (not a chair) to reach high shelves, and have good lighting.\n\n(b) TWO precautions that might have prevented the clogged sink. (2)\n    Scrape food scraps, peelings and bones into the bin (or compost) before washing dishes, and use a sink\n     strainer.\n    Never pour grease or fat down the drain. Let it cool and put it in the bin. Flush the drain regularly with hot\n     water.\n\n(c)(i) FOUR features of a free-standing food mixer that show value for money. (4)\n    A powerful motor with several speeds, and a pulse setting.\n    A range of attachments (beater, whisk, dough hook), plus optional extras such as a mincer or juicer.\n    A large stainless steel bowl, and a stable, heavy base with non-slip feet. It should be durable and easy to\n     clean.\n    Safety features (overload cut-out, head lock), a good guarantee (warranty), and available spare parts and\n     servicing.\n\n(c)(ii) Explain FOUR benefits of owning a freezer. (4)\n    Saves money: you can buy food in bulk or when it is on special, and freeze surplus home-grown fruit and\n     vegetables.\n    Saves time: meals can be cooked in large batches and frozen for later, which helps busy families and\n     unexpected guests.\n    Reduces waste: leftovers and foods near their expiry date can be frozen instead of thrown away.\n    Gives variety all year: seasonal foods (mangoes, sorrel, pigeon peas) can be enjoyed out of season, and fewer\n     shopping trips are needed.","6":"(a) Define \"unit price\", with ONE example. (2)\n    The unit price is the cost of one standard unit (per kilogram, per 100 g, per litre or per item) of a product. It lets\n    shoppers compare the value of different brands and sizes. Example: a 500 g pack of rice costs $250, so the unit\n    price is $250 ÷ 500 g = $0.50 per gram, or $50 per 100 g.\n\n(b) TWO qualities of fresh fish. (2)\n    Bright, clear, bulging eyes and red gills\n    Firm flesh that springs back when pressed, with a fresh sea smell\n\n(c) The difference between processing white flour and wholewheat flour. (2)\n    For wholewheat (wholemeal) flour, the whole wheat grain (bran, germ and endosperm) is ground: a 100%\n    extraction rate. For white flour, the bran and germ are removed during milling, and only the endosperm is\n\n\n    ground (about a 70–72% extraction rate). White flour is often bleached and then enriched with some of the lost\n    B vitamins and iron.\n\n(d)(i) ONE important item of information missing from the front label. (1)\n    The net weight (also accept the best-before or expiry date).\n\n(d)(ii) Directions for making ONE product from the flour mix. (6)\n    Directions for fried dumplings (makes about 12):\n    1. Put 2 cups (250 g) of Ria's Super Flour Mix in a bowl.\n    2. Gradually add about ½ cup (125 ml) of cold water, mixing with your fingers or a fork to form a soft, but not\n    sticky, dough.\n    3. Knead lightly on a floured surface for 1–2 minutes until smooth. Cover and rest for 10 minutes.\n    4. Divide into 12 equal pieces, roll each into a ball, and flatten slightly.\n    5. Heat about 2 cm of oil in a frying pan over medium heat.\n    6. Fry the dumplings for 3–4 minutes on each side, until golden brown, puffed and cooked through.\n    7. Drain on paper towels and serve hot.\n\n(d)(iii) Describe the changes in the starch when the dumplings are cooked. (3)\n    Gelatinization: the starch granules in the flour absorb the water in the dough and, when heated (above about\n    60°C), swell and burst. This sets the dough into a soft, cooked texture inside.\n    Dextrinization: on the surface, the dry heat of the hot oil breaks some of the starch down into shorter chains\n    called dextrins, which brown, giving the dumplings their golden colour, crisp crust and toasted flavour.\n    Cooking also makes the starch easier to digest.","7":"(a) Define ONE term. (2)\n    (i) Plate service: a method of serving in which the food is put onto individual plates in the kitchen (portioned\n    and garnished) and then carried to each diner at the table. It is used in restaurants and for formal meals.\n    (ii) Hors d'oeuvres: small, light, savoury appetizers served at the start of a meal, or with drinks, to stimulate the\n    appetite, for example stuffed eggs or saltfish fritters.\n\n(b) FOUR advantages of a microwave oven. (4)\n    It cooks and reheats food very quickly, saving time.\n    It uses less energy than a conventional oven.\n    It defrosts frozen food quickly.\n    It keeps more nutrients and colour in vegetables (short cooking time, little water). Food can be cooked in the\n     serving dish, so there is less washing up, and the kitchen stays cool.\n\n(c) TWO ways to save fuel when using (i) the stove and (ii) the oven. (4)\n    (i) Stove: cover pots with lids, use pans that match the burner size, and lower the flame once the food boils\n     (for example when boiling the macaroni). Also accept: use a pressure cooker or a tiered steamer.\n    (ii) Oven: cook the baked chicken, macaroni pie and coconut buns together, or one after another while the\n     oven is hot, using all the shelves. Do not open the door unnecessarily, and switch off a few minutes early to\n     use the residual heat.\n\n\n(d) A nutritionally balanced breakfast menu for your uncle, a construction worker. (6)\n  Breakfast for a construction worker (high energy for heavy manual work)\n  Fresh orange juice (vitamin C)\n  Cornmeal porridge made with milk and nutmeg (carbohydrate, calcium)\n  Ackee and saltfish with sautéed onions and sweet peppers (protein, fat, vitamins)\n  Boiled green bananas, yam, and fried dumplings (carbohydrate for energy)\n  Sliced avocado and tomato (vitamins, healthy fat)\n  Hot chocolate tea, made with milk\n  Why it suits him: hard physical labour needs a lot of energy, so the meal is rich in starchy foods for\n  carbohydrate and has enough protein for muscle repair. It includes fruit and vegetables for vitamins and\n  minerals, plus fluids, to keep him working all morning."},"2009":{"1":"(a) THREE factors, other than nutrition knowledge, that influence food choices. (3)\n    Income and the cost of food\n    Culture, religion and family traditions\n    Advertising and the media, peer pressure, availability (foods in season), likes and dislikes, health status\n\n(b) THREE signs or symptoms of diabetes. (3)\n    Frequent urination\n    Excessive thirst\n    Extreme hunger with unexplained weight loss; also accept tiredness, blurred vision, slow-healing wounds.\n\n(c) THREE guidelines when planning meals for patients convalescing from major surgery. (3)\n    Include extra protein (eggs, fish, chicken, milk) to repair tissues and heal the wound.\n    Include foods rich in vitamin C (citrus, guava) and zinc to help wound healing, and iron to replace blood lost.\n    Serve light, easily digested food (steamed, baked, poached; not fried or spicy), in small, frequent, attractive\n     portions, with plenty of fluids. Follow any doctor's instructions.\n\n(d) THREE nutritional guidelines for Merle (her blood clots slowly and her teeth are weakening). (3)\n    These signs suggest a lack of vitamin K (needed for blood clotting), calcium and vitamin D (for strong teeth and\n    bones).\n    Eat more dark-green leafy vegetables (callaloo, spinach, cabbage, broccoli), which supply vitamin K for blood\n     clotting, as well as calcium.\n    Increase calcium-rich foods: milk, cheese, yogurt, and canned fish with bones (sardines, salmon).\n    Get vitamin D from fortified margarine and milk, oily fish and egg yolks, plus sunlight on the skin, so the\n     calcium can be absorbed. Also include vitamin C for healthy gums.\n\n(e) Explain why each statement is incorrect. (4)\n    (i) \"Plantains are good for children who suffer from anaemia\": plantains are mainly a source of carbohydrate\n    (starch) and contain only a small amount of iron. Anaemia is usually caused by a lack of iron, so a child with\n    anaemia needs iron-rich foods such as liver, red meat, dark-green leafy vegetables and legumes, eaten with\n    vitamin C. Plantain alone will not correct it.\n    (ii) \"An excessive intake of sugar causes diabetes\": sugar itself does not directly cause diabetes. Type 1\n    diabetes is caused by the body's immune system destroying the insulin-producing cells of the pancreas. Type 2\n    diabetes is linked mainly to obesity, physical inactivity, family history (genes) and age, which make the body\n    resistant to insulin. Eating a lot of sugar can contribute to weight gain and obesity, which increases the risk, but\n    it is not the direct cause.","2":"(a) Define (i) bottling and (ii) blanching. (4)\n\n\n    (i) Bottling: a method of preserving food by placing it in clean glass jars, sealing them airtight, and heating them\n    to a temperature high enough to destroy micro-organisms and enzymes. The airtight seal (vacuum) prevents\n    recontamination.\n    (ii) Blanching: plunging vegetables or fruits into boiling water (or steam) for a short time (1–3 minutes), then\n    cooling them quickly in iced water. It is done before freezing, to destroy enzymes, kill surface bacteria and keep\n    the colour, flavour and texture.\n\n(b) TWO qualities to consider when selecting fruit for jam. (2)\n    Firm, just-ripe or slightly under-ripe fruit, which has the most pectin and acid.\n    Fresh, sound fruit free from bruises, mould or insect damage.\n\n(c)(i) ONE feature of a pot and ONE feature of a spoon needed for jam-making. (2)\n    Pot: a large, heavy-based (thick) pan made of stainless steel or enamel.\n    Spoon: a long-handled wooden spoon.\n\n(c)(ii) Why each feature matters. (2)\n    A large, heavy, stainless steel pan spreads heat evenly so the jam does not burn, gives room for the jam to\n     boil rapidly without boiling over, and does not react with the fruit acids.\n    A long-handled wooden spoon does not conduct heat, so it protects the hand from burns and splashes of very\n     hot jam. It also does not react with the acid or scratch the pan.\n\n(d) Explain why the pigeon (gungo) peas became soft after thawing. (2)\n    They were probably frozen too slowly, or not blanched. Slow freezing forms large ice crystals, which rupture\n    the cell walls of the peas. When they thaw, water and nutrients leak out of the damaged cells, so the peas\n    become soft and mushy. Without blanching, enzymes also continue to break down the texture during storage.\n    Thawing and refreezing makes it worse.\n\n(e)(i) ONE additive used in each product. (2)\n    a) Margarine: vitamins A and D (also accept colouring such as annatto or beta-carotene, or an emulsifier).\n    b) Mayonnaise: an emulsifier, such as lecithin (from egg yolk); also accept a preservative or stabilizer.\n\n(e)(ii) The function of each additive. (2)\n    Vitamins A and D in margarine: fortification, which improves its nutritive value so it is similar to butter.\n    Emulsifier in mayonnaise: keeps the oil and vinegar mixed together in a stable emulsion, so they do not\n     separate.","3":"(a) Define \"bake blind\". (2)\n    To bake blind is to bake a pastry case (flan or tart shell) without its filling. The pastry is lined with greaseproof\n    paper and weighted with baking beans or rice, so it keeps its shape and the base does not rise or become\n    soggy. The filling is added afterwards.\n\n(b) TWO advantages each of (i) boiling and (ii) grilling. (4)\n    (i) Boiling: (1) it is simple, safe, and needs little attention or equipment; (2) it uses no fat (suitable for low-fat\n     diets) and tenderizes tough foods, making them easy to digest.\n    (ii) Grilling: (1) it is quick, and food is ready in minutes; (2) fat drips out of the food, so it is a healthy, low-fat\n     method, and it gives an attractive browned surface and good flavour.\n\n\n(c) TWO ways eggs are used in food preparation, with ONE example each. (4)\n    Binding: eggs hold ingredients together, for example in fish cakes, meatballs and burgers.\n    Raising (aerating): beaten eggs trap air, which makes mixtures rise, for example in sponge cakes and\n     meringues. (Also accept: coating, as in egg and breadcrumbs for fried chicken; thickening, as in custards;\n     emulsifying, as in mayonnaise; glazing, as in pastry; garnishing, as in sliced boiled egg.)\n\n(d) Choose a method for the cake (1 lb each of flour, sugar and margarine, and 12 eggs) and describe THREE\nsteps. (4)\n    Method: the creaming method. Equal amounts of fat and sugar make a rich cake, like a pound cake.\n    1. Cream the margarine and sugar together with a wooden spoon or electric mixer until the mixture is very\n    light, pale and fluffy. This traps air.\n    2. Add the beaten eggs gradually, a little at a time, beating well after each addition. Add a spoonful of the flour\n    if the mixture begins to curdle.\n    3. Fold in the sifted flour gently with a metal spoon, using a figure-of-eight movement, so the air is not\n    knocked out. Put the mixture into a greased and lined tin, smooth the top, and bake at 160–180°C until golden,\n    firm and a skewer comes out clean.\n\n(e) ONE factor that caused each muffin fault. (2)\n    (i) Large tunnels: the mixture was over-mixed (beaten too much after adding the liquid), which developed the\n     gluten (also accept: the oven was too hot).\n    (ii) Poor flavour: too little salt or cheese was used, or a mild cheese was used, or there was too much baking\n     powder (bitter), or old or stale ingredients were used.","4":"(a) Define \"complementary feeding\". (2)\n    Complementary feeding is the gradual introduction of other (solid and semi-solid) foods and liquids into a\n    baby's diet, usually from about 6 months of age, while breastfeeding continues. Breast milk alone no longer\n    meets all the baby's nutritional needs at this age.\n\n(b) THREE advantages to the infant of continuing breastfeeding. (3)\n    Breast milk keeps supplying high-quality nutrients (protein, fats, vitamins), filling any gaps in the new foods.\n    It contains antibodies that protect against infections, diarrhoea and allergies.\n    It is clean, at the right temperature, easy to digest, and gives the baby comfort and emotional security.\n\n(c) The MAIN function of protein in an infant's diet. (1)\n    Growth: building new body cells and tissues during this period of rapid growth.\n\n(d)(i) TWO foods to combine with potatoes for a nutritionally adequate meal for the baby. (2)\n    (Note: WHO now advises starting solids at about 6 months. The answer assumes the baby is ready for solids.)\n    Pumpkin or carrot (vegetable)\n    Flaked fish (or minced chicken), or milk (food from animals)\n\n(d)(ii) Describe how to prepare and cook the meal. (4)\n    1. Wash hands and all utensils thoroughly.\n    2. Wash, peel and dice the potato and pumpkin. Remove all bones and skin from a small piece of fish.\n\n\n    3. Steam (or boil in a little water) the potato and pumpkin until very soft, about 15 minutes. Steam the fish over\n    the vegetables for the last 8–10 minutes.\n    4. Mash or purée everything together with a little breast milk, formula or cooking water, until smooth and free\n    of lumps. Do not add salt, sugar or spices. Cool to lukewarm, test the temperature, and feed with a clean\n    spoon.\n\n(d)(iii) ONE vitamin in the meal and ONE function of it. (2)\n    Vitamin A (as carotene in the pumpkin or carrot): needed for healthy eyes (vision in dim light) and skin, and for\n    growth and fighting infection. (Also accept vitamin C from the potato, which keeps gums healthy and helps iron\n    absorption.)\n\n(d)(iv) TWO nutritional guidelines for breastfeeding mothers. (2)\n    Eat a balanced diet with extra energy, protein, calcium and fluids. Drink plenty of water and milk, because\n     breast milk is mostly water.\n    Avoid alcohol, smoking, and large amounts of caffeine, and take only medication the doctor approves, as\n     these pass into the breast milk.","5":"(a) Define \"star rating\" (star marking) for a refrigerator. (2)\n    The star rating is a system of stars (★) on the freezer compartment of a refrigerator and on frozen food packets\n    that shows the temperature of the freezer, and therefore how long frozen food can safely be stored in it. ★ =\n    −6°C (1 week), ★★ = −12°C (1 month), ★★★ = −18°C (3 months), ★★★★ = −18°C or below, able to freeze\n    fresh food.\n\n(b) The purpose of each cooker feature. (2)\n    (i) Oven fan: circulates hot air evenly around the oven, so food cooks evenly on all shelves and faster, at a\n     lower temperature.\n    (ii) Thermostat: automatically controls and keeps the oven at the temperature selected.\n\n(c) TWO other factors customers should consider when choosing a cooker. (2)\n    Size, and the space available in the kitchen.\n    The fuel type (gas or electric) and running cost, price, safety features, ease of cleaning, and after-sales service.\n\n(d)(i) TWO dishes each using rice and carrots. (2)\n    Rice: rice pudding, and fried rice (also accept pelau or rice and peas).\n    Carrots: carrot juice, and carrot cake (also accept glazed carrots or carrot soup).\n\n(d)(ii) A suitable small electrical appliance for each dish. (2)\n    Rice pudding or plain rice: an electric rice cooker. Fried rice: an electric skillet or wok.\n    Carrot juice: a blender or juicer. Carrot cake: a food processor (for grating) or an electric hand mixer.\n\n(d)(iii) TWO first aid measures for Joseph's burned hands. (2)\n    Hold the hands under cool running water for at least 10–20 minutes to cool the burn and ease the pain.\n    Remove rings and watches before swelling starts, then cover loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing or cling\n     film. Do not apply butter or ointments or burst blisters, and get medical help for serious burns.\n\n(d)(iv) FOUR kitchen hygiene practices for preparing food. (4)\n\n\n    Wash hands thoroughly before and during food preparation, especially after handling raw meat.\n    Keep work surfaces, chopping boards and utensils clean, and use separate boards for raw and cooked foods.\n    Store food correctly, covered and at the right temperature (cold below 5°C, hot above 63°C).\n    Dispose of waste in covered bins emptied regularly, keep pests out, and wipe up spills immediately.","6":"(a) TWO advantages of buying food in large supermarkets. (2)\n    A wide variety of foods and brands under one roof (one-stop shopping).\n    Competitive prices, special offers and bulk discounts, plus refrigerated storage, clear labels and dates, and\n     services such as parking and card payments.\n\n(b)(i) TWO convenience foods. (2)\n    Canned corned beef (or other canned foods)\n    Instant noodles (also accept frozen vegetables, cake mixes, packet soups)\n\n(b)(ii) ONE advantage of convenience foods. (1)\n    They save time and effort in preparation.\n\n(b)(iii) ONE disadvantage. (1)\n    They are often expensive, and high in salt, fat, sugar and additives.\n\n(c)(i) FOUR shopping guidelines for Carla (large family, limited money). (4)\n    Plan meals in advance and shop with a list and a budget. Avoid impulse buying.\n    Buy local foods in season (ground provisions, fruit, vegetables), which are cheaper and fresher.\n    Use cheaper protein sources: peas and beans, eggs, chicken, canned fish. Combine cereals and legumes.\n    Compare prices and unit prices, buy store brands, and buy non-perishables in bulk when cheaper.\n\n(c)(ii) ONE reason to use bread flour instead of cake flour for bread. (2)\n    Bread flour is a \"strong\" flour made from hard wheat with a high protein (gluten) content. When kneaded, the\n    gluten develops into a strong, elastic network that traps the carbon dioxide produced by the yeast, so the\n    bread rises well and has a good volume and an open, chewy texture. Cake flour is soft and low in gluten, so the\n    bread would be flat and dense.\n\n(c)(iii) Describe FOUR effects of heat on beef when it is stewed. (4)\n    The protein coagulates, so the meat changes from red to brown or grey, and becomes firm.\n    The collagen (connective tissue) is changed into gelatin by the slow, moist heat, so the meat becomes tender\n     and the gravy thickens.\n    The meat shrinks as the protein fibres contract and squeeze out juices (water and fat), which pass into the\n     stewing liquid.\n    Fat melts, flavour develops (extractives dissolve in the gravy), harmful bacteria are destroyed, and some B\n     vitamins are lost into the liquid (they are kept if the gravy is eaten).","7":"(a) Define (i) brunch, (ii) cocktails and (iii) table d'hôte. (6)\n    (i) Brunch: a meal combining breakfast and lunch, eaten in the late morning (about 10 a.m. to 1 p.m.),\n    especially at weekends, with both breakfast and lunch foods.\n\n\n    (ii) Cocktails: a social party where mixed drinks (alcoholic or non-alcoholic) are served with small savoury\n    snacks (finger foods, canapés) that guests eat standing up, usually in the early evening. Also: an appetizer such\n    as fruit or shrimp cocktail.\n    (iii) Table d'hôte: a set menu with a fixed number of courses and limited choice, served at a fixed price, often at\n    a set time.\n\n(b)(i) TWO factors when planning meals for the elderly residents. (2)\n    Their health conditions and special diets (diabetes, hypertension), plus chewing difficulties: they need soft,\n     easily digested food.\n    Their nutritional needs (less energy, but enough protein, calcium, iron, fibre and fluids), the low budget, and\n     their likes and cultural preferences.\n\n(b)(ii) A two-course, low-budget lunch for senior citizens, with a local food in the starchy dish and in the\ndessert. (6)\n    Main course\n    Steamed fish (or stewed chicken back) in a tomato and onion sauce (protein, soft and low in fat)\n    Mashed breadfruit (or crushed sweet potato) (local starchy food)\n    Steamed callaloo and carrots (vitamins A and C, iron, fibre)\n    Dessert\n    Cassava pone (or sweet potato pudding), in a small portion, low in sugar (local food)\n    Drink: lime juice (lightly sweetened) or water\n    This is cheap because it uses local foods in season and cheaper proteins. It is soft and easy to chew, low in salt\n    and fat, and balanced.\n\n(b)(iii) TWO ways to save fuel when preparing and cooking this meal. (2)\n    Steam the fish and vegetables in a tiered steamer over the pot where the breadfruit is boiling, so one burner\n     cooks several items.\n    Use lids, lower the flame once foods boil, and bake the pudding while the oven is already on for another item.\n     Prepare all ingredients before lighting the stove."},"2010":{"1":"(a) Explain the term \"vegan\". (2)\n    A vegan is a strict vegetarian who eats only foods from plants (cereals, legumes, nuts, seeds, fruits and\n    vegetables) and no animal products at all: no meat, fish, poultry, eggs, milk, cheese, or usually honey.\n\n(b) One regional organization that gives information on the benefits of being vegan. (1)\n    CARPHA (Caribbean Public Health Agency), formerly CFNI (Caribbean Food and Nutrition Institute). Also accept\n    PAHO.\n\n(c) THREE effects, other than obesity, of a high-fat diet. (3)\n    High blood cholesterol, leading to blocked arteries (atherosclerosis), heart disease and stroke.\n    High blood pressure (hypertension).\n    Increased risk of type 2 diabetes, gallstones and some cancers (bowel, breast); also accept indigestion.\n\n(d)(i) Explain TWO benefits of adequate fibre. (4)\n    Prevents constipation and bowel disorders. Fibre absorbs water and adds bulk to the faeces, making them soft\n    and easy to pass. This prevents constipation, piles (haemorrhoids) and diverticular disease, and lowers the risk\n    of bowel cancer, because waste moves through the intestine quickly.\n    Helps weight control and controls blood sugar and cholesterol. High-fibre foods are filling and take longer to\n    chew and digest, so Jan feels full on fewer calories, which helps her lose weight. Soluble fibre (in oats and\n    beans) slows the absorption of sugar into the blood and helps lower blood cholesterol.\n\n(d)(ii) Plan a two-course vegan lunch for Jan and her friend. (4)\n    Main course: red bean and vegetable stew (made with coconut milk), brown rice, steamed callaloo, and a\n    tossed salad with tomato, cucumber and a lime-oil dressing\n    Dessert: fresh fruit salad (paw paw, pineapple, watermelon) topped with toasted coconut\n    Drink: fresh sorrel or lime juice, lightly sweetened\n\n(d)(iii) TWO lifestyle practices, other than diet, to reduce body weight. (2)\n    Exercise regularly, for example 30–60 minutes of brisk walking, jogging, swimming or aerobics on most days.\n    Get enough sleep, manage stress, and reduce sitting time (TV, computer); walk or take the stairs instead of\n     driving or using the lift. Also accept: avoid alcohol and smoking.","2":"(a)(i) Two benefits of preserving food. (2)\n    Prevents spoilage and waste.\n    Makes seasonal foods available all year, and saves money (also accept: adds variety).\n\n(a)(ii) Explain \"pickling\". (2)\n    Pickling is a method of preserving food (such as cucumbers, onions or mangoes) in vinegar, usually with salt,\n    sugar and spices. The acid in the vinegar lowers the pH, so micro-organisms cannot grow.\n\n\n(a)(iii) Two preservation methods other than pickling. (2)\n    Freezing\n    Drying (also accept canning, jam-making, salting, smoking)\n\n(a)(iv) Explain why each happened. (6)\n    a) The pickled cucumber became mouldy: the vinegar was too weak (not acidic enough), or the cucumbers\n    were not fully covered by the vinegar, or the jars were not sterilized, or not sealed properly (airtight). This\n    allowed mould spores from the air to land on the surface and grow.\n    b) The string beans became discoloured and soft after leaving the freezer: they were not blanched (or not\n    blanched long enough) before freezing, so enzymes stayed active and caused loss of colour (turning olive-green\n    or brown) and texture. They may also have been frozen slowly, forming large ice crystals that ruptured the cell\n    walls. When thawed, the water leaked out, leaving the beans limp and soft. Poor packaging (freezer burn) or\n    thawing and refreezing would add to the damage.\n\n(b) Explain how removing water and using chemicals can be applied to preserving fish. (4)\n    Removal of water (drying or salting): the fish is split, covered with salt and then dried in the sun or a dryer. Salt\n    draws water out of the fish cells, and out of any bacteria, by osmosis, and drying removes more moisture.\n    Micro-organisms need water to grow, so without it they become inactive, and enzymes slow down too. This is\n    how salted codfish is made.\n    Use of chemicals: chemicals such as salt, vinegar (acid), sugar, or smoke compounds (from smoking) prevent\n    the growth of micro-organisms. For example, fish can be soaked in brine and smoked (smoked herring). The\n    salt and the chemicals in the smoke (phenols) act as preservatives. Fish can also be pickled in vinegar\n    (escoveitch style or soused), where the low pH stops bacterial growth.","3":"(a)(i) Two dry-heat methods of cooking. (2)\n    Baking (also accept roasting)\n    Grilling (also accept frying or toasting)\n\n(a)(ii) Two methods of steaming. (2)\n    Using a tiered steamer or a steamer basket over a pan of boiling water (direct steaming).\n    Placing food on a covered plate over a saucepan of boiling water (plate method), or in a basin covered with\n     foil and stood in boiling water (indirect steaming, as for steamed puddings). Also accept a pressure cooker.\n\n(a)(iii) Two advantages of steaming. (2)\n    Fewer water-soluble nutrients are lost, because the food does not sit in the water.\n    The food is light, moist and easily digested, needs no fat, and several foods can be cooked at once in a tiered\n     steamer, saving fuel.\n\n(b)(i) One item made from yeast dough. (1)\n    Bread rolls (also accept a loaf of bread, cinnamon rolls, pizza base, buns, doughnuts).\n\n(b)(ii) The effect, other than sweetness, of too much sugar in the yeast dough. (2)\n    Too much sugar draws water out of the yeast cells by osmosis, which slows down or kills the yeast. The dough\n    rises slowly and poorly, so the product is small, heavy and dense. The extra sugar also makes the crust brown\n    too quickly or burn.\n\n\n(b)(iii) ONE step that made the pastry crust hard and tough. (1)\n    Too much water was added (or the dough was over-kneaded or over-handled), which developed the gluten.\n    Also accept: the fat was not rubbed in enough, the mixture was too warm, or too little fat was used.\n\n(c) Explain the steps Rhonda took to make a smooth, lump-free roux-based cream of pumpkin soup. (6)\n    Prepare and cook the pumpkin properly. She peeled, cut and boiled (or steamed) the pumpkin with onion,\n    garlic and herbs in stock until it was very soft, then puréed it in a blender or rubbed it through a sieve, so there\n    were no pieces or fibres left.\n    Make the roux correctly. She melted equal amounts of butter and flour in a pan and cooked them gently for 1–\n    2 minutes, stirring, without browning. Cooking the roux takes away the raw flour taste and helps the starch\n    thicken evenly.\n    Add the liquid gradually, stirring continuously, then simmer. She took the pan off the heat and added the\n    stock and milk a little at a time, stirring or whisking constantly, so the starch grains were evenly separated and\n    could not clump into lumps. She then returned it to the heat, brought it to the boil while stirring, and simmered\n    for a few minutes to cook the starch fully. Then she stirred in the pumpkin purée, seasoned it, added cream\n    just before serving, and strained it if necessary.","4":"(a)(i) Two nutrients from the bran of the wheat grain. (2)\n    Dietary fibre (cellulose)\n    B vitamins (thiamine and niacin), also accept iron and phosphorus.\n\n(a)(ii) Two preparation practices before cooking that could affect the nutritive value of coloured vegetables. (2)\n    Peeling thickly, or cutting into small pieces long before cooking, which exposes more surface to air so vitamin\n     C is lost by oxidation.\n    Soaking the vegetables in water, so water-soluble vitamins (B and C) and minerals leach out. (Also accept:\n     bruising with a blunt knife; washing after cutting.)\n\n(b)(i) Two functions of protein in the diet of preschoolers. (2)\n    Growth: building new body cells, muscles and organs during this period of rapid growth.\n    Repair of tissues, and making antibodies to fight infection (also accept enzymes and hormones).\n\n(b)(ii) How the multi-mix principle ensures enough protein for preschoolers. (2)\n    The multi-mix principle combines foods from several food groups in one meal. Mixing a staple (rice, cereal)\n    with a legume (peas, beans) gives complementary proteins. Each supplies the essential amino acids the other\n    lacks, so together they give high-quality protein. Adding a food from animals (milk, egg, fish) boosts the protein\n    further. For example, rice and peas with a little fish, or cornmeal porridge made with milk, ensure the child gets\n    enough good-quality protein.\n\n(b)(iii) FOUR guidelines mothers can follow so preschoolers eat nutritious foods. (4)\n    Serve small, colourful, attractive portions, cutting food into fun shapes.\n    Introduce new foods one at a time, with familiar foods, and do not force or bribe the child.\n    Give regular meals and healthy snacks (fruit, milk, crackers) instead of sweets, chips and sugary drinks.\n    Eat together as a family, set a good example, and involve the child in simple food preparation.\n\n(b)(iv) Explain how ONE physical and ONE financial factor may harm the nutrition of some elderly people. (4)\n\n\n    Physical factor: loss of teeth or poorly fitting dentures (or poor mobility). Elderly people who cannot chew\n    properly avoid foods such as meat, raw vegetables and fruit, and rely on soft, starchy foods such as porridge\n    and bread. Their diet then lacks protein, fibre, iron and vitamins, which can lead to undernutrition, anaemia\n    and constipation. (Arthritis or weakness may also stop them shopping and cooking.)\n    Financial factor: low fixed income (a pension). Many elderly people live on small pensions and must also pay\n    for medication and utilities. They cannot afford enough protein foods, fresh fruits and vegetables, especially\n    when food prices rise, so they buy cheap, filling, low-nutrient foods or skip meals, leading to malnutrition.","5":"(a)(i) One use of each item in the picture. (4)\n    1. Cook's (chef's) knife: chopping, slicing and dicing vegetables, meat and herbs.\n    2. Blender: puréeing soups, making fruit punches and smoothies, and blending sauces.\n    3. Muffin (patty) tin: baking muffins, cupcakes and small tarts.\n    4. Pastry brush: brushing glaze (egg wash or milk) onto pastry and bread, and greasing tins.\n\n(a)(ii) TWO other features to consider when selecting a refrigerator. (2)\n    Frost-free (automatic defrost) operation.\n    Energy-efficiency rating, adjustable shelves, the size of the freezer compartment, crisper drawers, and door\n     shelves. Also accept: size and capacity.\n\n(b)(i) Two items from a first aid kit for treating Sarah's foot. (2)\n    Tweezers (sterilized)\n    Antiseptic solution or wipes, and a sterile dressing or adhesive plaster (also accept cotton wool and a\n     bandage).\n\n(b)(ii) Describe the correct steps to treat Sarah's injury. (4)\n    1. Wash your hands. Sit Sarah down and raise her foot.\n    2. If the glass is small and on the surface, gently remove it with sterilized tweezers, pulling it out at the same\n    angle it went in. (If the glass is large or deeply embedded, do not remove it. Pad around it, bandage it, and get\n    medical help.)\n    3. Let the wound bleed a little to flush out dirt, then apply pressure with a clean pad to stop the bleeding.\n    4. Clean the area with antiseptic, cover with a sterile dressing or plaster, and advise her to seek medical\n    attention if it becomes red, swollen or painful, or about a tetanus injection.\n\n(c) FOUR hygiene and safety guidelines for your father's butcher shop. (4)\n    Keep meat refrigerated (below 5°C) or frozen (below −18°C) at all times, and do not leave it exposed in the\n     heat.\n    Wear clean protective clothing (apron, hair covering, gloves) and wash hands frequently, especially after\n     handling money.\n    Clean and sanitize chopping blocks, knives, saws and counters regularly, and use separate equipment for raw\n     and cooked meats.\n    Keep flies and pests out (screens, covered displays), dispose of waste and bones promptly in covered bins,\n     and buy meat only from inspected, licensed sources.","6":"(a) Two disadvantages or limitations of convenience foods. (2)\n    They are more expensive than fresh foods.\n    They are often high in salt, sugar, fat and additives, and lower in some nutrients and fibre. Also accept: the\n     portions may be small, and the packaging creates waste.\n\n(b) Explain \"extraction rate\" in milling wheat into flour. (1)\n    The extraction rate is the percentage of the whole wheat grain that ends up in the flour after milling. For\n    example, wholemeal flour has a 100% extraction rate (the whole grain is used), while white flour has about a\n    70–72% extraction rate, because the bran and germ are removed.\n\n(c)(i) THREE characteristics of fresh fish. (3)\n    Bright, clear, bulging eyes\n    Bright red or pink gills\n    Firm, elastic flesh with shiny scales that cling tightly, and a fresh, mild sea smell\n\n(c)(ii) Two methods of heat transfer when frying battered fish. (2)\n    Conduction\n    Convection\n\n(c)(iii) Describe how each method works when frying the fish. (4)\n    Conduction: the heat from the burner passes through the metal of the frying pan into the oil. Heat then passes\n    from the hot oil directly into the batter and fish where they touch, and moves from the outside of the fish to\n    the centre, molecule by molecule.\n    Convection: as the oil at the bottom of the pan heats, it becomes less dense and rises, while cooler oil sinks to\n    take its place. These convection currents circulate the hot oil around the fish, cooking all surfaces evenly. This is\n    especially true in deep-fat frying.\n\n(c)(iv) Describe TWO effects of heat on the flour-and-water batter during frying. (4)\n    The batter sets and becomes crisp. The heat causes the starch in the flour to gelatinize and the gluten protein\n    to coagulate, so the liquid batter becomes a firm, crisp coating. It seals the fish, keeping it moist and preventing\n    it absorbing too much fat.\n    The batter turns golden brown (dextrinization). The dry heat of the oil breaks down some of the starch on the\n    surface into dextrins, giving a golden-brown colour and a pleasant flavour. Water in the batter also turns into\n    steam and escapes, making it light and crisp.","7":"(a)(i) Explain \"à la carte\" menu. (2)\n    An à la carte menu is a list of dishes, each priced separately, from which customers choose the items they\n    want. Each dish is cooked to order, so the total bill depends on what is chosen.\n\n(a)(ii) Explain buffet service. (2)\n    Buffet service is a style of serving in which all the food is set out on a table, and guests help themselves (or are\n    served by staff at the table), then eat seated or standing. It is useful for serving large numbers of people\n    quickly, with a wide variety of dishes.\n\n\n(b)(i) Two factors when planning meals. (2)\n    Nutritional needs of the people being served\n    Money available (also accept time, occasion, likes and dislikes, foods in season)\n\n(b)(ii) Plan a THREE-course dinner menu for Aunt Mae's engagement party, using rice, chicken, carrots, bread\nand tomatoes. (3)\n    Starter: cream of tomato soup with garlic bread croutons\n    Main course: roast chicken with herb stuffing, savoury yellow rice, glazed carrots, and a tossed green salad\n    Dessert: bread and butter pudding with vanilla custard\n    Drink: fruit punch\n\n(b)(iii) Discuss TWO factors when planning the kitchen layout to improve efficiency. (4)\n    The work triangle. The sink, cooker and refrigerator should be placed close together, in the order of work\n    (store, prepare, cook, serve), with no traffic through the triangle. This cuts down the walking and saves time\n    and energy.\n    Enough work surfaces, storage and good lighting and ventilation. Continuous counter space beside the sink\n    and the cooker, with cupboards above and below, keeps equipment and ingredients close to where they are\n    used. Good lighting over work areas and an extractor fan or window make the kitchen safer and more\n    comfortable. Easy-to-clean surfaces (tiles, laminate) save cleaning time.\n\n(b)(iv) Explain how a pressure cooker works. (3)\n    A pressure cooker is a heavy pan with a lid that locks on and a rubber gasket (sealing ring) that makes it\n    airtight. As the water inside boils, the steam cannot escape, so the pressure builds up (to about 15 psi). The\n    higher pressure raises the boiling point of water to about 121°C, so the food cooks much faster than at 100°C,\n    in about a third of the normal time. A safety valve and weight control the pressure and let excess steam\n    escape. When cooking is finished, the pressure must be released (by cooling or by the valve) before the lid is\n    opened."},"2011":{"1":"(a)(i) Explain \"nutritional status\". (2)\n    Nutritional status is the condition of a person's health as it is affected by the food they eat and the body's use\n    of nutrients. A person may be well nourished, undernourished or overnourished.\n\n(a)(ii) Two food habits that may harm nutritional status. (2)\n    Skipping meals, especially breakfast.\n    Eating a lot of fast foods, fried foods and sugary drinks (also accept: eating few fruits and vegetables, snacking\n     on junk food, overeating, crash dieting).\n\n(a)(iii) One condition caused by each habit. (2)\n    Skipping meals: low blood sugar (tiredness, dizziness, poor concentration), or undernutrition and anaemia if it\n     is regular.\n    Too many fast and sugary foods: obesity (also accept tooth decay, type 2 diabetes, hypertension).\n\n(b) TWO guidelines for Ryan to reduce (i) fat and (ii) salt when preparing meals. (4)\n    (i) Fat: (1) bake, grill, steam or boil instead of frying; (2) trim visible fat from meat, remove the skin from\n     chicken, and use low-fat dairy products and less oil, butter and coconut milk.\n    (ii) Salt: (1) season with fresh herbs, spices, garlic, onion and lime juice instead of salt and stock cubes; (2) use\n     fewer salted and processed foods (salted meats, canned soups, snacks), and soak salted fish thoroughly\n     before cooking.\n\n(c)(i) Two signs that led to the diagnosis of vitamin A deficiency. (2)\n    Night blindness (difficulty seeing in dim light).\n    Dry, rough skin and dry, sore eyes (xerophthalmia), and frequent infections.\n\n(c)(ii) One food to correct the deficiency. (1)\n    Carrots (also accept liver, pumpkin, sweet potato, callaloo, mango, egg yolk, fortified margarine).\n\n(d)(i) One unsuitable dish for the convalescing grandmother. (1)\n    Fried chicken.\n\n(d)(ii) Two reasons. (2)\n    It is high in fat, which makes it heavy and hard to digest for someone recovering from illness, whose digestion\n     may be weak.\n    Frying makes the coating hard and crispy, which is difficult to chew for an elderly person. A convalescent\n     should have light, easily digested, steamed, baked or poached food. (The raw salad may also be hard to chew\n     and digest; lightly cooked vegetables would be better.)","2":"(a)(i) Define \"food additive\". (2)\n\n\n    A food additive is a substance added to food in small amounts during processing or preparation to improve its\n    keeping quality, flavour, colour, texture or nutritive value. Examples are salt, preservatives, colourings and\n    emulsifiers.\n\n(a)(ii) Two advantages of using additives. (2)\n    They preserve food and extend its shelf life, reducing waste and making foods available out of season.\n    They improve the flavour, colour, texture and nutritive value (for example fortification) of foods, making them\n     more attractive.\n\n(b)(i) Two guidelines for selecting mangoes for chutney. (2)\n    Choose firm, green (unripe) or half-ripe mangoes. They hold their shape and have the sharp, sour taste\n     needed for chutney.\n    Select sound fruit, free from bruises, rot and insect damage.\n\n(b)(ii) Explain how vinegar works as a preservative in mango chutney. (2)\n    Vinegar contains acetic acid, which lowers the pH (makes the chutney acidic, below about pH 4.5). Most\n    bacteria, yeasts and moulds cannot grow in such acidic conditions, so the chutney is preserved. The large\n    amount of sugar in chutney adds to the effect by drawing water out of micro-organisms by osmosis.\n\n(c)(i) A suitable material for the saucepan. (1)\n    Stainless steel (also accept enamel or heat-proof glass).\n\n(c)(ii) One reason for that material. (1)\n    Stainless steel does not react with the acid in vinegar and mangoes. Aluminium, copper or iron pans react with\n    the acid, causing a metallic taste, discolouration and loss of quality.\n\n(c)(iii) Describe the method of making the chutney. (2)\n    Wash, peel and chop or dice the green mangoes. Put them into the stainless steel pan with chopped onion,\n    garlic, hot pepper, ginger, raisins, spices (salt, cinnamon, allspice), sugar and vinegar. Bring to the boil, then\n    simmer gently, uncovered, stirring often, for about 45–60 minutes, until the mixture is thick, the mango is soft,\n    and no free liquid remains (a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a clear trail).\n\n(c)(iv) Two correct steps for bottling the chutney. (2)\n    Sterilize the jars and lids by boiling them, or heating them in the oven, and keep them hot.\n    Fill the hot jars with the hot chutney up to about 1 cm from the top, remove air bubbles, wipe the rims, and\n     seal immediately with vinegar-proof lids. Label with the name and date, and store in a cool, dark place.\n\n(c)(v) One reason for each step. (2)\n    Sterilizing kills micro-organisms on the jars that would otherwise spoil the chutney. Hot jars do not crack\n     when the hot chutney is poured in.\n    Filling and sealing while hot creates a vacuum and an airtight seal, which keeps out air and micro-organisms.\n     Vinegar-proof lids prevent corrosion. Labelling helps with stock rotation.","3":"(a)(i) Distinguish between a \"garnish\" and a \"decoration\". (2)\n\n\n    A garnish is an edible item used to improve the appearance of savoury dishes (for example a parsley sprig on\n    fish, or lemon wedges). A decoration is used to improve the appearance of sweet dishes (for example glacé\n    cherries, piped cream, or chocolate on cakes and desserts).\n\n(a)(ii) Two ways, other than garnishing, to improve the presentation of food. (2)\n    Serve food on suitable, clean, attractive dishes of the right size, and do not overcrowd the plate.\n    Combine foods with a variety of colours, shapes and textures, and arrange them neatly (also accept: serve hot\n     food hot and cold food cold; set an attractive table).\n\n(a)(iii) Two general guidelines for garnishing. (2)\n    Garnishes should be edible, fresh and simple, and should suit (complement) the dish in flavour and colour.\n    Add them just before serving so they stay fresh and crisp, and do not over-garnish.\n\n(b) An appropriate garnish for each dish. (2)\n    (i) Baked fish: lemon or lime wedges (butterflies) and a sprig of parsley.\n    (ii) Cream of pumpkin soup: a swirl of cream with croutons, or chopped chives or parsley.\n\n(c)(i) ONE factor responsible for each observation. (5)\n    1. The bananas did not discolour: they were dipped in, or tossed with, lemon or lime juice (or syrup) as soon\n     as they were cut, or prepared just before serving. The acid prevents enzymic browning.\n    2. The oil in the mayonnaise stayed stable: the egg yolk (lecithin, an emulsifier) was used and the oil was\n     added very slowly, drop by drop at first, with constant beating, forming a stable emulsion.\n    3. The batter stayed on the chicken: the chicken was dried well and coated in seasoned flour before dipping\n     into the batter, the batter had the right (thick coating) consistency, and the oil was hot enough to set the\n     batter quickly.\n    4. The coffee was very bitter: the coffee was boiled, or brewed for too long, or too much coffee was used.\n     Over-extraction releases bitter tannins.\n    5. The syrup crystallized: it was stirred after it began to boil, or the sugar was not fully dissolved before\n     boiling, or sugar crystals on the side of the pan fell back in (no acid or glucose added).\n\n(c)(ii) One way Rita achieved each characteristic of the white sauce. (3)\n    Free of lumps: she cooked the roux (fat and flour) for a minute, removed the pan from the heat, and added\n     the milk gradually while stirring continuously, then stirred while it came to the boil.\n    Non-starchy flavour: she cooked the roux well, and boiled the sauce for 2–3 minutes after it thickened, so the\n     starch was fully cooked.\n    Correct pouring consistency: she used the right proportions for a pouring sauce: 15 g (½ oz) each of fat and\n     flour to 250 ml (½ pint) of milk.","4":"(a) One function of each mineral. (2)\n    (i) Iodine: needed to make the thyroid hormone (thyroxine), which controls metabolism; it prevents goitre.\n    (ii) Phosphorus: works with calcium to build strong bones and teeth; also needed for energy release in cells.\n\n(b)(i) Two minerals, other than iodine and phosphorus, that Mrs Simon needs more of in pregnancy. (2)\n    Iron\n    Calcium (also accept zinc)\n\n\n(b)(ii) One benefit of each mineral. (2)\n    Iron: makes haemoglobin for the mother's increased blood volume and builds the baby's iron store,\n     preventing anaemia.\n    Calcium: builds the baby's bones and teeth and protects the mother's own bones.\n\n(b)(iii) A nutritionally adequate dinner menu for her adolescent daughter. (4)\n    Steamed fish (or stewed beef) in tomato sauce\n    Rice and red peas\n    Steamed callaloo and carrots\n    Tossed salad with cucumber and sweet pepper\n    Fruit salad with yogurt\n    Glass of milk, or fresh orange juice\n    (This gives protein for growth, iron for menstruation, calcium for bones, and vitamins A and C.)\n\n(c)(i) Outline the steps for making cheese muffins. (4)\n    1. Preheat the oven to 200°C (gas mark 6) and grease a muffin tin or line it with paper cases.\n    2. Sift the flour, baking powder, salt and a pinch of mustard into a bowl. Stir in most of the grated cheese.\n    3. In another bowl, beat the egg with the milk and melted butter or oil.\n    4. Pour the liquid into the dry ingredients and stir just enough to combine (the mixture should be lumpy; over-\n    mixing makes muffins tough).\n    5. Spoon into the tins until two-thirds full, sprinkle with the remaining cheese, and bake for 15–20 minutes,\n    until well risen and golden.\n    6. Cool on a wire rack.\n\n(c)(ii) Two nutrients in cheese muffins that supply energy. (2)\n    Carbohydrate (starch from the flour)\n    Fat (from the cheese, butter and egg); protein also supplies some energy.","5":"(a) Two desirable features of each item. (4)\n    (i) Kitchen knives: a sharp, strong stainless steel blade that holds its edge, and a comfortable, firm, non-slip\n     handle that is securely attached (riveted).\n    (ii) Cake mixers: several speeds and a choice of attachments (beaters, whisk, dough hook); a strong motor\n     with a stable base, and easy to clean, with parts that eject.\n\n(b) Two procedures for cleaning cake pans. (2)\n    Soak them in hot soapy water to loosen baked-on mixture, then wash with a soft sponge or brush. Do not use\n     harsh scourers on non-stick pans.\n    Rinse and dry thoroughly straight away (warming them in the cooling oven helps) to prevent rusting, then\n     store in a dry place.\n\n(c)(i) Two symptoms the boys are likely to have. (2)\n    Vomiting and nausea\n    Diarrhoea and stomach cramps, with fever\n\n\n(c)(ii) The bacteria likely to have been in the baked chicken. (1)\n    Salmonella (also accept Campylobacter or Clostridium perfringens).\n\n(c)(iii) THREE personal hygiene practices the person serving the chicken may NOT have followed. (3)\n    Washing hands with soap after using the toilet, and before handling the food.\n    Using clean tongs or gloves instead of bare hands to serve the chicken.\n    Covering cuts or sores with waterproof dressings, tying back and covering hair, and not coughing or sneezing\n     near food (also accept: wearing a clean apron).\n\n(c)(iv) Two essential steps to clean the greasy oven (not self-cleaning). (2)\n    Switch off and unplug or cool the oven. Remove the shelves and trays and soak them in hot soapy water.\n    Apply an oven cleaner (or a paste of baking soda and water), leave it to soften the grease, then wipe it off\n     with a damp cloth. Rinse well and dry. Wipe spills after each use to stop grease building up.\n\n(d) Two other factors that could have caused Patrice's cut. (2)\n    Using a blunt knife, which slips more easily and needs more force.\n    Not using a chopping board, or using a board that slipped (no damp cloth underneath), or being distracted\n     and working too fast, or having wet, greasy hands or a wet handle.","6":"(a) Two specific roles of the consumer organization in your country. (2)\n    Educating consumers about their rights and responsibilities, and about value for money.\n    Investigating consumer complaints and helping consumers get redress. It also monitors prices, labels and\n     weights, and checks products for safety.\n\n(b) Two ways to tell if supermarket eggs are fresh. (2)\n    Check the date on the carton, and look for clean, uncracked, rough (not shiny) shells.\n    At home, put the egg in a bowl of salted water: a fresh egg sinks and lies flat, while a stale egg stands up or\n     floats (because its air sac has grown larger).\n\n(c) Two forms of TVP sold in supermarkets. (2)\n    Soya chunks\n    Soya mince (granules); also accept vegetarian burgers or sausages.\n\n(d) FOUR reasons consumers may NOT benefit from bulk buying. (4)\n    Perishable foods may spoil before they can be used, so money is wasted.\n    There may not be enough storage space (cupboards, freezer) at home.\n    A large amount of money is needed at one time, which may strain a small budget.\n    People may use more, and waste more, when there is a lot in the house. The family may also get tired of the\n     same food, or prices may later fall or better offers appear.\n\n(e) The MAIN raising agent in each item. (2)\n    (i) Swiss roll: air (beaten into the eggs by whisking).\n    (ii) Pancakes: steam, plus air (also accept baking powder for thick American-style pancakes).\n\n(f)(i) The scientific principle behind a chemical raising agent in a fruit cake. (2)\n\n\n    A chemical raising agent such as baking powder contains bicarbonate of soda (an alkali) and an acid (cream of\n    tartar). When moistened and heated, the acid and alkali react to produce carbon dioxide gas. The gas bubbles\n    expand in the heat of the oven and push up the mixture, making the cake rise. The heat then sets the flour and\n    egg around the bubbles, giving a light texture.\n\n(f)(ii) The principles of the heat transfer methods during baking. (2)\n    Convection: hot air circulates in the oven, carrying heat around the cake tin. Conduction: heat passes through\n    the metal tin into the cake mixture and moves slowly from the outside to the centre. (Radiation from the hot\n    oven walls helps brown the surface.)","7":"(a)(i) Define \"cover\" or \"place setting\". (2)\n    A cover (place setting) is the space on a table set for one person, about 60 cm wide, with all the cutlery,\n    crockery, glassware and napkin that person needs for the meal, arranged correctly.\n\n(a)(ii) Two ways to enhance a tray for tray service. (2)\n    Use a clean, attractive tray cloth or napkin, and a small vase with a flower.\n    Use matching, attractive crockery, arrange items neatly so they are easy to reach, and serve small, attractively\n     garnished portions with hot food covered to keep it hot.\n\n(b)(i) Two points for proper storage of non-perishable foods in the food cupboard. (2)\n    The cupboard should be cool, dry, clean and well ventilated, away from the stove, so foods do not become\n     damp or stale.\n    Store foods in airtight, labelled containers. Put new stock behind old stock (first in, first out), and keep heavy\n     items on lower shelves.\n\n(b)(ii) Three reasons the layout gives maximum efficiency. (3)\n    The food cupboard, refrigerator, sink and cooker are arranged in the logical order of work (storage,\n     preparation and washing, cooking), forming a compact work triangle with short distances, which saves time\n     and energy.\n    There are continuous work surfaces with cupboards above and below, giving plenty of space to prepare food\n     and storage close to where items are used.\n    The sink is under the window, giving good natural light and ventilation, and the doors are placed so traffic\n     does not pass through the main work area, making it safer.\n\n(c)(i) Plan a lunch menu for a preschool child. (4)\n    Small wholewheat sandwich with egg (or chicken) and lettuce, cut into fun shapes\n    Carrot and cucumber sticks\n    Small banana or orange segments\n    Small box of milk, or pure fruit juice with no added sugar (or water)\n    A plain oatmeal cookie\n\n(c)(ii) How ONE item should be packaged. (1)\n    Wrap the sandwich in cling film or foil, or put it in a small, clean, airtight plastic lunch box, so it stays fresh and\n    moist and is protected from dust and flies. Pack it with an ice pack in an insulated lunch bag.\n\n\n(d) Quantities of flour and fat to make 48 biscuits (the recipe makes 24). (2)\n  48 ÷ 24 = 2, so double the quantities:\n  - Flour: 225 g × 2 = 450 g (16 oz or 1 lb)\n  - Fat: 100 g × 2 = 200 g (8 oz)"},"2012":{"1":"(a) Define \"over-nutrition\". (2)\n    Over-nutrition is a form of malnutrition caused by eating more food, or more of certain nutrients (especially\n    energy from fat and sugar), than the body needs over a long period. It leads to overweight, obesity and related\n    diseases.\n\n(b) Two signs of over-nutrition. (2)\n    Excess body weight (BMI over 25), or obesity, with a large waist measurement.\n    High blood pressure, high blood cholesterol or high blood sugar; also accept shortness of breath on exertion\n     and painful joints.\n\n(c) One condition linked to a deficiency of each mineral. (2)\n    (i) Sodium: muscle cramps (also accept dehydration or low blood pressure).\n    (ii) Calcium: rickets in children, osteoporosis in adults (also accept osteomalacia).\n\n(d)(i) Two possible reasons for Sally's change of diet (she omits meat but still eats animal foods). (2)\n    Health reasons: to reduce fat and cholesterol and lower the risk of heart disease and obesity.\n    Religious, ethical or moral beliefs, such as concern for animals, or a religion such as Seventh-day Adventist or\n     Hindu (also accept: cost, since meat is expensive; taste; environmental concerns).\n\n(d)(ii) Discuss TWO health benefits of such a diet. (4)\n    Lower risk of heart disease and high blood pressure. Without meat, the diet contains much less saturated fat\n    and cholesterol, while plant foods add fibre and potassium. This helps keep blood cholesterol and blood\n    pressure down. Because Sally still eats milk, cheese and eggs, she still gets enough protein, calcium and vitamin\n    B12.\n    Easier weight control and better digestion. A diet based on vegetables, fruits, legumes and wholegrains is high\n    in fibre and usually lower in calories. This helps prevent obesity and constipation, and lowers the risk of bowel\n    cancer and type 2 diabetes.\n\n(d)(iii) Plan an appropriate two-course lunch for Sally (a lacto-ovo vegetarian). (4)\n    Main course: macaroni and cheese pie, stewed red peas (cooked without meat), steamed callaloo and carrots,\n    and a tomato and cucumber salad\n    Dessert: banana custard (made with egg and milk)\n    Drink: carrot juice or sorrel","2":"(a) Define \"blanching\". (2)\n    Blanching is plunging food (usually vegetables or fruit) into boiling water, or steaming it, for a short time (1–3\n    minutes), then cooling it quickly in iced water. It is done before freezing to destroy enzymes, kill surface\n    bacteria and keep the colour.\n\n\n(b) Four benefits of preserving food. (4)\n    Prevents food spoiling, so less is wasted.\n    Makes seasonal foods available all year round.\n    Saves money: surplus or cheap seasonal food can be bought and stored.\n    Adds variety to the diet (jams, pickles, dried fruits), makes food easier to transport and store, and can create\n     income.\n\n(c)(i) A food packaged in a container lined with aluminium foil. (1)\n    Fruit juice in cartons (Tetra Pak), or UHT milk, or potato chips and snacks, or soup mixes.\n\n(c)(ii) One reason for the foil lining. (1)\n    Foil is a barrier to light, air (oxygen), moisture and micro-organisms, so it protects the flavour and nutrients and\n    extends the shelf life.\n\n(d)(i) and (ii) THREE guidelines for selecting the best mangoes for jam, and why each is important. (6)\n    1. Choose firm, just-ripe (or slightly under-ripe) mangoes. Importance: they contain more pectin and acid than\n    fully ripe fruit, so the jam sets well and has a good, tangy flavour.\n    2. Select fresh, sound fruit with no bruises, rot, mould or insect damage. Importance: damaged fruit contains\n    micro-organisms that make the jam ferment or go mouldy, and it spoils the colour, flavour and keeping quality.\n    3. Choose a variety with a good flavour and colour and little fibre (for example Julie or Bombay). Importance:\n    the jam will be smooth, not stringy, and have an attractive colour and a strong mango flavour.\n\n(d)(iii) Describe the plate (wrinkle) test or the flake test. (2)\n    Plate (wrinkle) test: chill a saucer in the freezer. Take the pan off the heat, put a teaspoon of jam on the cold\n    saucer and let it cool for a minute. Push the jam with your finger. If the surface wrinkles and holds its shape,\n    the jam is set and ready for bottling. If it is runny, boil for a few more minutes and test again.\n    Flake test: dip a clean wooden spoon into the jam, lift it out, let it cool slightly, then let the jam drip off the\n    side. If it falls in thick flakes or drops that join and hang off the edge of the spoon, the jam is set.","3":"(a) Three methods of cake-making. (3)\n    Creaming method\n    Rubbing-in method\n    Whisking method (also accept the melting method and the all-in-one method)\n\n(b) One function of each ingredient in cake-making. (3)\n    (i) Flour: forms the structure (framework) of the cake. Its gluten and starch set when heated.\n    (ii) Sugar: sweetens, helps trap air during creaming, gives a soft texture and a golden-brown crust.\n    (iii) Butter or margarine: adds flavour and richness, traps air when creamed, makes the cake tender and\n     moist, and helps it keep longer.\n\n(c) Two sweet dishes, other than cakes, where eggs are the main ingredient. (2)\n    Baked egg custard (or crème caramel)\n    Meringues (also accept soufflé or mousse)\n\n(d) Explain TWO principles of heat transfer when cakes are baked. (4)\n\n\n    Convection: the oven element or flame heats the air inside the oven. The hot air rises, cooler air sinks, and the\n    circulating currents carry heat all around the cake tin. (A fan oven speeds this up.)\n    Conduction: heat passes from the hot metal cake tin into the outside of the cake mixture, and then gradually,\n    molecule to molecule, from the outer layers to the centre. This is why the edges cook first, and why large cakes\n    need a lower temperature and a longer time. (Radiation from the oven walls and element also browns the top.)\n\n(e) FOUR steps in preparing the cornstarch base for a smooth ice cream. (4)\n    Blend the cornstarch with a little of the cold milk to a smooth paste, with no lumps.\n    Heat the rest of the milk (with sugar). Pour a little onto the paste, then return everything to the pan.\n    Bring to the boil, stirring continuously, and simmer for 2–3 minutes until it thickens and the starch is fully\n     cooked (gelatinized).\n    Remove from the heat, add flavouring (vanilla), cover the surface to prevent a skin forming, and cool\n     completely before freezing. Beat it or churn it during freezing to keep the ice crystals small, which keeps the\n     ice cream smooth.","4":"(a) Two functions of each nutrient. (6)\n    (i) Water: regulates body temperature (sweating); carries nutrients and removes wastes; aids digestion;\n     prevents constipation.\n    (ii) Protein: growth and repair of body tissues; makes enzymes, hormones and antibodies; supplies energy\n     when needed.\n    (iii) Carbohydrate: supplies energy for activity and warmth; fibre aids digestion and prevents constipation;\n     spares protein for growth and repair.\n\n(b) Three nutrients that make brown rice more nutritious than white rice. (3)\n    Dietary fibre\n    B vitamins: thiamine (B1), niacin and riboflavin\n    Minerals: iron, magnesium and zinc (also accept vitamin E). These are found in the bran and germ, which are\n     removed when white rice is polished.\n\n(c)(i) Which dish suits your elderly bedridden neighbour, and which does not? (2)\n    Suitable: steamed white fish.\n    Unsuitable: fried potato chips.\n\n(c)(ii) Reasons for your answer. (2)\n    Steamed white fish is low in fat, soft, moist and easy to chew and digest, and is a good source of protein for\n     repairing tissues and preventing bedsores. Steaming keeps its nutrients.\n    Fried chips are greasy and high in fat and calories, which makes them hard to digest for an inactive, bedridden\n     person. They can cause indigestion and weight gain, and they are often salty (bad for blood pressure).\n\n(d) THREE guidelines for Juliet (19, a busy secretary) to plan and prepare her meals. (3)\n    Plan meals for the week ahead and shop with a list. Cook in batches at the weekend and freeze portions for\n     weekday meals.\n    Pack a healthy lunch and snacks (sandwiches, fruit, yogurt) rather than relying on fast food, and never skip\n     breakfast.\n\n\n    Include foods from all the food groups, especially iron-rich foods (young women need extra iron), calcium,\n     and fruit and vegetables. Use quick cooking methods (stir-frying, steaming, pressure cooking) and some\n     healthy convenience foods.","5":"(a)(i) Three factors that can contribute to food contamination, for the checklist. (3)\n    Poor personal hygiene of food handlers (not washing hands, no hair covering, handling money and food).\n    Food not kept at the correct temperature (hot food not kept hot, cold food not refrigerated), or food left\n     uncovered.\n    Dirty premises or equipment, or pests (flies, rodents), or cross-contamination of raw and cooked foods.\n\n(a)(ii) Three desirable hygiene practices at the food outlets. (3)\n    Staff wear clean uniforms, aprons and hair nets, and use tongs or gloves to handle food.\n    Food is covered and displayed in clean, refrigerated or heated display cases.\n    Separate staff handle money and food (or handlers wash their hands after handling money); counters are\n     cleaned regularly and waste is disposed of in covered bins.\n\n(b)(i) Discuss TWO advantages of a gas cooker over an electric one. (4)\n    Instant, easily controlled heat. A gas flame heats up immediately and can be turned up or down instantly, and\n    you can see the size of the flame. This gives precise control (for example, reducing to a simmer quickly) and\n    saves time, because there is no waiting for an element to heat or cool.\n    Cheaper to run, and it works during power cuts. In many Caribbean countries, cooking gas costs less than\n    electricity for the same amount of cooking. A gas stove can still be used (lit with a match) during electricity\n    outages, which are common after storms.\n\n(b)(ii) FOUR factors, other than colour and warranty, to consider before buying a gas cooker. (4)\n    Size, and the space available in the kitchen.\n    The family's needs: the number of burners, the oven size, and whether a grill or broiler is included.\n    Safety features: flame-failure safety device, automatic ignition, stable construction.\n    Price and running costs, ease of cleaning (removable burners, enamel finish), a reputable brand, and\n     availability of spare parts and servicing.\n\n(c) ONE reason for each of Vivian's actions in treating her burn. (2)\n    Cooled the area with cold water: to reduce the heat in the skin, stop the burning from going deeper, relieve\n     the pain and reduce swelling.\n    Covered it with a clean cloth: to protect the burned skin from dirt and germs, preventing infection.","6":"(a) Two pieces of information in the UPC (barcode). (2)\n    A code for the manufacturer (company).\n    A code for the specific product (item and size), plus a check digit. The price is linked to the code in the store's\n     computer.\n\n(b) Define each term. (4)\n    (i) Comparative shopping: comparing the prices, quality, size and brands of the same product in different\n     stores, or of different brands in the same store, to get the best value for money.\n\n\n    (ii) Impulsive buying: buying items on the spur of the moment, without planning, usually because of attractive\n     displays, advertising or special offers, often things you did not need.\n\n(c)(i) Which carrot cake mix, Box A or Box B, is the better buy for nutrition? Give a reason. (2)\n    Box A. Its ingredients list contains real carrot (a source of vitamin A and fibre) and no added salt. Box B uses\n    only carrot extract (which is mainly flavouring, with few nutrients) and contains added salt, which is not good\n    for blood pressure.\n\n(c)(ii) TWO factors, other than income, that influence food buying in a supermarket. (2)\n    Advertising, packaging and in-store displays (special offers at eye level or at the checkout).\n    Family size and needs, health and dietary requirements, cultural or religious preferences, and the information\n     on labels (nutrition facts, dates).\n\n(d)(i) One effect of dry heat on the sugar and one of moist heat on the flour while the bread bakes. (2)\n    Dry heat on sugar: it caramelizes, browning the crust and giving flavour.\n    Moist heat on flour: the starch gelatinizes (the granules absorb water, swell and set), and the gluten\n     coagulates, setting the structure of the bread.\n\n(d)(ii) Using \"smoke point\" and \"flash point\", suggest how the oil caught fire. (4)\n    When oil is heated, it first reaches its smoke point: the temperature (about 200–230°C for most vegetable oils)\n    at which it begins to break down and give off a thin, bluish smoke. This is a warning that the oil is too hot.\n    Because the students were distracted, the oil kept heating beyond the smoke point until it reached its flash\n    point (about 300–320°C): the temperature at which the vapours given off by the oil can ignite, or burst into\n    flames when they meet a flame or spark. The oil therefore caught fire. (The fire should be put out by turning off\n    the heat and covering the pan with a lid or fire blanket, never with water.)","7":"(a) Two materials suitable for packing individual items for a picnic. (2)\n    Plastic containers with tight-fitting lids (also accept zip-lock bags)\n    Aluminium foil (also accept greaseproof paper, cling film, vacuum flasks)\n\n(b) The difference between a \"menu\" and a \"main course\". (4)\n    A menu is the list of all the dishes to be served at a meal, written in the order they will be served (or the list of\n    dishes available in a restaurant). The main course is just one part of the menu: the largest and most important\n    course, usually based on a protein dish (meat, fish, poultry or legumes) served with starchy foods and\n    vegetables.\n\n(c)(i) The order of preparation for cream of pumpkin soup, yeast bread and fruit punch. (3)\n    1. Loaf of yeast bread (first) · 2. Cream of pumpkin soup (second) · 3. Fruit punch (last)\n\n(c)(ii) Three reasons for that order. (3)\n    The yeast bread takes the longest. The dough needs time to rise (prove) twice and then bake, so it must be\n     started first. The soup can be made while the dough is proving.\n    The soup takes a moderate time (boiling the pumpkin, blending, adding cream) and should be served hot, so\n     it is made while the bread proves and bakes.\n    The fruit punch is quick to make and is best fresh and cold, to keep its vitamin C and flavour, so it is made last\n     and chilled just before serving.\n\n\n(d) FOUR guidelines for conserving fuel when using the oven. (4)\n   Cook several dishes at the same time, using all the shelves (whole-oven cooking).\n   Do not open the oven door unnecessarily (use the glass door and oven light to check), because heat escapes\n    each time.\n   Preheat only for as long as needed, and turn the oven off a few minutes before the food is done so that\n    residual heat finishes the cooking.\n   Plan baking to follow on from one dish to another while the oven is hot, keep the door seal in good condition,\n    and use the correct temperature."},"2013":{"1":"(a) Three signs of a lack of vitamin B1 (thiamine). (3)\n    Beri-beri: weakness and wasting of the muscles, with numbness or tingling in the legs (nerve damage).\n    Loss of appetite, tiredness and irritability.\n    Swelling (oedema) of the legs, and an enlarged heart or heart failure (wet beri-beri); also accept poor growth\n     in children, confusion and depression.\n\n(b) Three factors that may influence the eating habits of people in your community. (3)\n    Income (money available) and the cost of food.\n    Culture, religion and family traditions.\n    Advertising and the media, peer pressure, availability of foods in season, health status, and nutrition\n     knowledge.\n\n(c) Discuss THREE effects of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM). (6)\n    Poor growth and development. Protein is needed to build body tissues. Children with PEM become stunted\n    (short for their age) and underweight. Brain development can also be affected, leading to poor learning ability\n    and lower intelligence that may be permanent.\n    Weak immunity and frequent infections. Antibodies are made from protein, so malnourished children cannot\n    fight infections well. They suffer repeatedly from diarrhoea, measles and respiratory infections, which worsen\n    the malnutrition and can be fatal. PEM is a major cause of death in young children.\n    Physical signs of marasmus and kwashiorkor. In marasmus (a lack of both energy and protein), the child is\n    severely wasted, with \"skin and bones\", a wrinkled \"old man\" face and no fat. In kwashiorkor (mainly a lack of\n    protein), the child has oedema (swollen belly, legs and face), patchy, discoloured, thin hair, peeling skin, an\n    enlarged fatty liver, and is apathetic and miserable.\n\n(d) ONE reason to justify any TWO of the statements. (4)\n    1. Reduce salt: too much sodium raises blood pressure (hypertension), a major cause of stroke and heart and\n     kidney disease in the Caribbean.\n    2. Eat iron-rich foods with vitamin C: vitamin C changes non-haem (plant) iron into a form the body absorbs\n     much more easily, helping prevent anaemia.\n    3. Eggs contain cholesterol, so do not eat them daily: the yolk is high in dietary cholesterol, and eating a lot\n     of it may raise blood cholesterol in some people, increasing the risk of heart disease. Many people are\n     advised to limit eggs to a few per week.\n    4. Lean meat and fish are not the only way to reduce fat: fat can also be reduced by choosing low-fat\n     cooking methods (grilling, baking, steaming instead of frying), using low-fat dairy foods, trimming fat, and\n     eating more plant foods such as legumes and vegetables.","2":"(a) Two methods, other than freezing, to preserve green beans. (2)\n    Canning or bottling\n\n\n    Pickling (also accept drying or salting)\n\n(b) One preservative for preserving green beans at home. (1)\n    Vinegar (or salt).\n\n(c) One reason for using that preservative. (1)\n    Vinegar is acidic and lowers the pH, so bacteria and moulds cannot grow. (Salt draws water out of microbes by\n    osmosis.)\n\n(d) Define \"enzyme action\". (2)\n    Enzyme action is the activity of enzymes, which are proteins in plant and animal tissue that speed up chemical\n    changes. After harvesting they keep working, causing ripening, then over-ripening, softening, browning, and\n    the loss of flavour, colour and nutrients.\n\n(e) Two undesirable effects of enzyme action if the beans are not blanched. (2)\n    Loss of colour: the beans turn dull, olive-green or brown.\n    Loss of flavour, texture (they become tough or limp) and vitamin C during storage.\n\n(f) Two procedures before placing the blanched beans in the freezer. (2)\n    Cool them quickly in iced water, then drain and dry them thoroughly.\n    Pack them in airtight freezer bags or containers, remove the air, seal and label with the date.\n\n(g) One reason for each procedure. (2)\n    Rapid cooling stops the cooking process, so the beans stay crisp and green. Drying prevents large ice crystals\n     forming on the surface.\n    Airtight packing prevents freezer burn and oxidation. Labelling lets you use the oldest first, within the safe\n     storage time.\n\n(h) Scientific explanation of stages II and III of the bean cell during freezing. (4)\n    Stage II (cell ruptured): when the bean is frozen slowly, the water in and around the cells forms large, sharp ice\n    crystals. Water expands as it freezes, so these large crystals pierce and tear the cell walls and membranes,\n    rupturing the cell. (Quick freezing forms small crystals that cause less damage.)\n    Stage III (cell on thawing): when the bean thaws, the ice melts and the water, along with dissolved nutrients\n    (vitamins and minerals), leaks out through the broken cell walls (this is the \"drip\"). Because the cell has lost its\n    water and its walls are damaged, it can no longer stay firm. It collapses and shrivels, so the thawed bean is\n    limp, soft and soggy, and has lost some of its nutrients.","3":"(a) One herb and one spice. (2)\n    Herb: thyme (also accept parsley, basil, mint, chives).\n    Spice: nutmeg (also accept cinnamon, cloves, ginger, black pepper, allspice/pimento).\n\n(b) Two advantages of conservative cooking and two of steaming. (4)\n    Conservative cooking (a small amount of water, lid on, short time, cooking water used): (1) keeps more\n     water-soluble vitamins (B and C) and minerals; (2) keeps more flavour and colour, and saves fuel.\n\n\n    Steaming: (1) the food does not touch the water, so fewer nutrients are lost; (2) the food is light and easy to\n     digest, and no fat is needed (good for invalids and young children). Also accept: several foods can be cooked\n     at once in a tiered steamer, and there is little risk of burning.\n\n(c)(i) Outline how each technique is done. (3)\n    Sifting: pass the flour (with salt) through a sieve held high above the bowl, shaking or tapping it gently.\n    Rubbing-in: cut the cold fat into small pieces in the flour, then lift the mixture and rub the fat into the flour\n     lightly with the fingertips, letting it fall back into the bowl, until it looks like fine breadcrumbs.\n    Glazing: brush the top of the pastry lightly with beaten egg, milk, or egg wash (egg and milk) before baking (or\n     with water and sugar for sweet items).\n\n(c)(ii) Why each technique matters for short crust pastry. (3)\n    Sifting removes lumps and foreign matter, and adds air for a lighter pastry.\n    Rubbing-in coats the flour particles with fat, which limits gluten development, giving a short, crumbly, tender\n     texture. Using the fingertips keeps it cool and traps air.\n    Glazing gives a shiny, golden-brown, attractive finish and helps seal the pastry.\n\n(d) One way to prevent each problem. (4)\n    1. Baked custard curdled: bake it in a bain-marie (a tin of water) at a moderate temperature (about 160°C),\n     and remove it as soon as it is set. Overheating makes the egg proteins over-coagulate.\n    2. Egg whites did not peak: use a clean, dry, grease-free bowl and whisk. Make sure no yolk gets into the\n     whites, and use whites at room temperature. Any fat stops the foam forming.\n    3. Dark ring around the yolk: do not overcook the egg (about 10 minutes is enough). Then plunge it\n     immediately into cold water and peel it. Long cooking makes iron in the yolk react with sulphur from the\n     white to form black iron sulphide.\n    4. Cocoa tasted starchy: blend the cocoa to a smooth paste with a little cold liquid first, then add the hot milk\n     and boil it for 1–2 minutes, stirring, so the starch is fully cooked (gelatinized).","4":"(a) Classify carbohydrates into the three main groups. (3)\n    Monosaccharides (simple sugars): glucose, fructose, galactose.\n    Disaccharides (double sugars): sucrose, lactose, maltose.\n    Polysaccharides (complex carbohydrates): starch, cellulose (fibre), glycogen, pectin.\n\n(b) One function of carbohydrates. (1)\n    They supply the body with energy (4 kcal per gram) for activity and warmth. Also accept: fibre (cellulose) aids\n    digestion, and they spare protein for growth and repair.\n\n(c) A specific carbohydrate in sweet corn and in milk. (2)\n    (i) Sweet corn: starch (and some sucrose).\n    (ii) Milk: lactose.\n\n(d) Discuss ONE factor affecting the nutritive value of corn after harvesting or during preparation. (2)\n    Storage time and temperature after harvesting: soon after harvest, enzymes in the corn begin converting its\n    natural sugars into starch, and vitamins (especially vitamin C) are lost. The longer and warmer it is stored, the\n    more the sweetness and vitamins decline. Corn should be eaten fresh or refrigerated. (Also accept: boiling it in\n    a lot of water for a long time leaches out B vitamins and vitamin C, so it is better steamed or boiled briefly.)\n\n\n(e)(i) A suitable breakfast for your pregnant neighbour. (4)\n    Glass of fresh orange juice (vitamin C, to help iron absorption)\n    Wholewheat or fortified cereal (or oats porridge) made with low-fat milk (calcium, B vitamins, folate)\n    Scrambled egg with sautéed callaloo (protein, iron, folic acid)\n    Wholewheat toast with a little margarine\n    Sliced paw paw (fibre and vitamins)\n    Avoid strong coffee. Choose decaffeinated tea, or a glass of milk instead.\n\n(e)(ii) FOUR guidelines for developing positive eating habits in preschool children. (4)\n    Offer a variety of foods from all the food groups in small, attractive, colourful portions.\n    Introduce new foods gradually, alongside familiar foods, and do not force the child to eat.\n    Have regular meal and snack times. Offer healthy snacks (fruit, yogurt) instead of sweets and sugary drinks,\n     and do not use food as a reward.\n    Eat together as a family, set a good example, and let the children help prepare simple foods.","5":"(a) Complete the accidents and precautions table. (4)\n    (i) Burns, precaution: use dry oven gloves to handle hot dishes, and turn pan handles inwards.\n    (ii) Accident: falls (slips).\n    (iii) Cuts, precaution: use sharp knives correctly on a chopping board, cutting away from the body; store\n     knives safely, not loose in soapy water.\n    (iv) Accident: electric shock.\n\n(b) The function of each oven feature. (2)\n    (i) Thermostat: automatically controls and keeps the oven at the selected temperature, switching the heat on\n     and off as needed.\n    (ii) Glass oven door: lets you check the food while it cooks without opening the door, so no heat is lost and\n     cakes do not sink.\n\n(c) THREE causes of the students' illness, for each of the following. (9)\n    (i) Personal hygiene during preparation:\n    Food handlers did not wash their hands after using the toilet or handling raw meat.\n    A cook coughed or sneezed over the food, or had an uncovered cut or infected sore (Staphylococcus).\n    A handler who was ill (with diarrhoea or vomiting) still prepared food. Long dirty nails, or hair falling into the\n     food, also contaminate it.\n    (ii) Hygienic conditions of the place:\n    Dirty work surfaces, chopping boards and utensils.\n    Pests (flies, cockroaches, rats) in the preparation area.\n    Poor waste disposal (open bins), an unsafe water supply, or dirty, poorly ventilated surroundings.\n    (iii) Food-handling practices:\n    Cross-contamination: using the same board and knife for raw chicken and ready-to-eat salad.\n    Undercooking (especially chicken, eggs and meat), or poor reheating.\n    Leaving cooked food at room temperature for hours before serving (the danger zone), or thawing food\n     improperly.\n\n\n(d) A method of clearing the clogged sink. (1)\n    Use a plunger over the drain (with the overflow covered) and pump firmly to dislodge the blockage. Also\n    accept: pour boiling water, or baking soda then vinegar, down the drain; unscrew and clean the U-bend; use a\n    drain rod.","6":"(a) Two factors when buying (i) a fresh cut of beef and (ii) leaf lettuce. (4)\n    (i) Beef: bright red lean with creamy-white fat and some marbling, firm and moist (not slimy) with a fresh\n     smell; little gristle and bone; bought refrigerated from a clean outlet.\n    (ii) Leaf lettuce: crisp, fresh, bright green leaves with no wilting, browning, slime or insect damage, and no\n     rust spots at the cut end.\n\n(b) Define \"expiry date\". (2)\n    The expiry date is the date on the label after which the food should not be eaten, because it may no longer be\n    safe or of good quality.\n\n(c) How the label helps Mohan check that the punch contains milk and peanuts as the main ingredients. (2)\n    The ingredients list on the label shows all the ingredients in descending order of weight, so the ingredient\n    present in the largest amount is listed first. If milk and peanuts appear at the start of the list, they are the main\n    ingredients. The product name and nutrition facts (protein and fat content) also give clues.\n\n(d) Describe ultra-heat treatment (UHT) of the peanut punch. (2)\n    In UHT treatment the punch is heated very quickly to a very high temperature, about 135–150°C, for only 2–5\n    seconds, then cooled rapidly and filled under sterile conditions into sterilized, airtight cartons (aseptic\n    packaging). This kills all micro-organisms and spores, so the punch keeps for several months unopened without\n    refrigeration, with little change in flavour or nutrients.\n\n(e) Explain how yeast works as a raising agent in bread rolls. (4)\n    Yeast is a living single-celled fungus. When it is given food (sugar), warmth (about 25–35°C) and moisture, it\n    becomes active and ferments the sugar, producing carbon dioxide gas and alcohol. Kneading develops the\n    gluten in the flour into an elastic network. The carbon dioxide bubbles are trapped in this gluten, so the dough\n    rises (proves) and roughly doubles in size. In the hot oven the gas expands further and the alcohol evaporates,\n    giving a final rise (\"oven spring\"). The heat then kills the yeast and sets the gluten and starch, fixing the light,\n    open texture of the rolls.","7":"(a) Two guidelines for organizing a buffet service. (2)\n    Arrange the food on the table in a logical order (plates first, then main dishes, vegetables, salads, sauces, with\n     cutlery and napkins at the end), with serving spoons for each dish.\n    Keep hot foods hot (chafing dishes) and cold foods cold, label dishes, make sure there is enough food for\n     everyone, and leave enough space for guests to move around.\n\n(b) The position of the dinner fork and the soup spoon in a cover. (4)\n    Dinner fork: on the left of the dinner plate, prongs facing up, about 2.5 cm (1 inch) from the edge of the\n     table.\n\n\n    Soup spoon: on the far right, outside the dinner knife, because cutlery is placed in order of use from the\n     outside in.\n\n(c)(i) FOUR other ways caterers can save time. (4)\n    Plan menus and prepare a detailed time plan, dovetailing tasks.\n    Do pre-preparation in advance (marinating, chopping, baking cakes the day before) and freeze what can be\n     frozen.\n    Use labour-saving equipment (food processors, pressure cookers, mixers) and some convenience foods.\n    Organize the work area and staff: assign tasks, set up stations, and clean as you go.\n\n(c)(ii) How many pounds of cake for 1000 slices, if 1 lb gives 24 slices? (2)\n    1000 ÷ 24 = 41.67. Rounded to the nearest whole number, Lydia needs 42 pounds of cake. (41 lb gives only 984\n    slices, which is not enough.)\n\n(c)(iii) A TWO-course lunch menu that uses an indigenous food in one course. (4)\n    Main course: stewed chicken, rice and peas, roasted breadfruit (an indigenous food), and a tossed green salad\n    with carrot and cucumber\n    Dessert: cassava pone (another indigenous food) with a scoop of vanilla ice cream\n    Drink: chilled sorrel or passion fruit juice"},"2014":{"1":"(a) Three signs or symptoms of diabetes. (3)\n    Frequent urination\n    Excessive thirst\n    Constant hunger, unexplained weight loss, tiredness, blurred vision, or slow-healing sores\n\n(b) Three effects, other than diabetes, of high levels of fat in the diet. (3)\n    Obesity (overweight), because fat is very high in calories (9 kcal per gram).\n    High blood cholesterol and blocked arteries, leading to heart disease and stroke.\n    High blood pressure, gallstones, and an increased risk of some cancers (bowel, breast).\n\n(c)(i) Three guidelines to reduce sugar. (3)\n    Drink water or unsweetened juices instead of soft drinks and sweetened beverages.\n    Use less sugar in tea, porridge and baking, or use a sugar substitute; choose fresh fruit instead of cakes,\n     sweets and pastries for dessert.\n    Read food labels and avoid foods with sugar (sucrose, glucose, corn syrup) near the top of the ingredients list.\n\n(c)(ii) Three guidelines to reduce fat. (3)\n    Bake, grill, steam or boil foods instead of frying them.\n    Trim visible fat from meat and remove the skin from chicken. Choose lean meats and fish.\n    Use low-fat or skimmed milk and dairy products, and use less butter, margarine, mayonnaise and coconut\n     milk.\n\n(d) Discuss TWO health benefits, other than helping with diabetes, of a vegetarian diet. (4)\n    Healthier heart and blood pressure. A vegetarian diet is low in saturated fat and cholesterol (there is no meat\n    fat), and rich in fibre, potassium and antioxidants. This helps lower blood cholesterol and blood pressure,\n    reducing the risk of heart disease and stroke.\n    Weight control and better digestion. Plant foods are high in fibre and generally lower in calories, so they help\n    prevent obesity. Fibre also prevents constipation, piles and diverticular disease, and lowers the risk of bowel\n    cancer.","2":"(a) ONE additive used to do each of these. (4)\n    (i) Set jams and jellies: pectin.\n    (ii) Prevent oil in mayonnaise separating: an emulsifier, such as lecithin (from egg yolk).\n    (iii) Improve the nutritive value of margarine: vitamins A and D (fortification).\n    (iv) Tenderize meat: papain (from green pawpaw), bromelain, or a commercial meat tenderizer.\n\n(b) Two reasons why foods with certain additives should be used in moderation. (2)\n    Some people are allergic or sensitive to certain additives (for example MSG, tartrazine, sulphites), which can\n     cause headaches, asthma or rashes.\n\n\n    Some additives may be harmful in large amounts over a long time (some preservatives, nitrites in processed\n     meats linked with cancer), and many processed foods with additives are also high in salt, sugar and fat.\n\n(c) Discuss the scientific principle that explains the removal of excess water from cucumber during pickling. (4)\n    The principle is osmosis. Osmosis is the movement of water from a weaker (dilute) solution to a stronger\n    (concentrated) solution through a partially permeable membrane. When cucumber slices are sprinkled with\n    salt or soaked in brine, the solution outside the cells is more concentrated than the cell sap inside. Water\n    therefore moves out of the cucumber cells, through their membranes, into the salty solution. The cucumber\n    loses water, becoming firmer and crisper, and the salty conditions also stop micro-organisms from growing,\n    since they too lose water by osmosis. This removal of water lets the vinegar penetrate better and prevents the\n    pickle from being diluted.\n\n(d)(i) Explain TWO guidelines for selecting bananas for high-quality jam. (4)\n    Choose bananas that are just ripe (firm, yellow, perhaps with green tips), not over-ripe. Under-ripe or just-\n    ripe fruit contains more pectin and acid, which are needed for a good set. Over-ripe, soft bananas have lost\n    much of their pectin, so the jam may not set and may taste fermented.\n    Choose fresh, sound fruit without bruises, mould or damage. Damaged or mouldy fruit introduces micro-\n    organisms that cause the jam to ferment or go mouldy, and spoils its colour and flavour. Good-quality fruit\n    gives a jam with a good colour, flavour and keeping quality.\n\n(d)(ii) Describe the process that caused the bananas in the fruit salad to darken. (2)\n    Enzymic browning. When bananas are peeled and cut, the cells are damaged and release the enzyme\n    polyphenol oxidase. This enzyme reacts with oxygen in the air and with phenolic compounds in the fruit,\n    producing brown pigments (melanins), so the banana darkens. It can be prevented by covering the banana with\n    lemon or lime juice (acid) or syrup, or by preparing it just before serving.","3":"(a) Two icings or frostings for decorating cakes. (2)\n    Butter icing (buttercream)\n    Royal icing (also accept glacé icing, fondant or sugar paste, cream cheese frosting)\n\n(b) One function of each ingredient in cake-making. (2)\n    (i) Liquid (milk or water): binds the dry ingredients together into a batter, and produces steam in the oven,\n     which helps the cake rise.\n    (ii) Eggs: trap air when beaten, which helps the cake rise. The egg protein coagulates when heated and sets\n     the cake's structure. Eggs also add colour, flavour and food value.\n\n(c) Two reasons flour should be folded into a cake mixture. (2)\n    To keep in the air already beaten into the mixture, so the cake stays light.\n    To avoid over-mixing, which develops the gluten and makes the cake tough and heavy.\n\n(d) Explain ONE effect of too much sugar in the yeast dough. (2)\n    Too much sugar draws water out of the yeast cells by osmosis and slows down or kills the yeast. The dough\n    rises slowly or poorly, giving a small, heavy, dense loaf. The extra sugar also makes the crust brown too quickly,\n    or even burn, before the inside is cooked.\n\n(e) Two reasons to follow the rules for batter-making. (2)\n\n\n    To make sure the batter has the correct consistency, so the products are light, even-textured and the right\n     shape.\n    To develop the right amount of gluten and trap enough air, so the products rise properly and are not tough or\n     heavy (also accept: to give consistent results and avoid wasting ingredients).\n\n(f) Explain THREE steps in preparing a baked egg custard. (6)\n    Mix the custard gently. Warm the milk (do not boil it). Beat the eggs lightly with the sugar and vanilla, then\n    pour the warm milk onto the eggs, stirring. Strain the mixture into a greased ovenproof dish. Do not whisk\n    vigorously, as this creates froth and bubbles on the surface.\n    Bake in a water bath (bain-marie) at a moderate temperature. Stand the dish in a roasting tin containing hot\n    water and bake at about 160–170°C (gas mark 3). The water protects the custard from direct heat, so the egg\n    protein coagulates slowly and evenly, giving a smooth, firm set. Too much heat makes the protein over-\n    coagulate and squeeze out liquid, so the custard curdles or \"weeps\" and becomes full of holes.\n    Test for doneness and finish. Grate nutmeg over the top before baking. Bake for about 40–45 minutes, until\n    the custard is set but still slightly wobbly in the centre; a knife inserted near the centre should come out clean.\n    Remove from the water bath, cool, then serve warm or chilled.","4":"(a)(i) Define \"hydrogenation\". (2)\n    Hydrogenation is the process of adding hydrogen to liquid vegetable oils (unsaturated fats) under pressure,\n    with a nickel catalyst and heat. This changes some of the unsaturated fatty acids into saturated ones, turning\n    the liquid oil into a solid or semi-solid fat.\n\n(a)(ii) One food made by hydrogenation. (1)\n    Margarine (also accept vegetable shortening, such as Crisco).\n\n(b) Copy the table and give one meal-planning guideline for THREE conditions other than anaemia. (3)\n    2. Obesity: reduce high-calorie foods (fried foods, sugary foods), control portion sizes, and include plenty of\n     high-fibre vegetables and fruits.\n    3. Acne: limit greasy, fried foods and sugary snacks, drink plenty of water, and include fresh fruits and\n     vegetables (vitamins A and C, zinc).\n    4. Hypertension: reduce salt (sodium) and processed or salted foods. Season with herbs and spices instead.\n    5. Constipation: increase fibre (wholegrains, fruit, vegetables, legumes) and drink plenty of water.\n\n(c) Explain TWO effects of a diet lacking water over a long period. (4)\n    Dehydration and poor temperature control. Without enough water the body cannot sweat properly to cool\n    itself, especially in a hot climate. This leads to overheating, headaches, dizziness, tiredness and confusion.\n    Blood volume also drops, so the heart has to work harder.\n    Constipation and kidney problems. Water is needed to keep faeces soft. Without it they become hard and\n    difficult to pass, causing constipation. The kidneys also need water to flush out wastes. Too little water\n    concentrates the urine, which can lead to kidney stones and urinary tract infections.\n\n(d) Describe ONE effect of heat on each ingredient in a true sponge cake. (6)\n    Flour: the starch granules absorb moisture and swell (gelatinization), and then set. The gluten proteins\n    coagulate (set) at about 70°C, forming the firm framework of the cake that holds the air bubbles in place. On\n    the surface, starch breaks down into dextrins, giving the golden-brown crust (dextrinization).\n\n\n    Eggs: the air beaten into the eggs expands as it heats, making the cake rise. The egg proteins (albumin) then\n    coagulate at about 60–70°C, setting the foam into a light, spongy structure.\n    Sugar: the sugar dissolves and softens (tenderizes) the texture by slowing the setting of the proteins. On the\n    outside, the sugar caramelizes in the heat, helping to brown the crust and adding flavour.","5":"(a)(i) Two materials used to make saucepans. (2)\n    Stainless steel\n    Aluminium (also accept cast iron, copper, enamel, non-stick coated pans, heat-proof glass)\n\n(a)(ii) One advantage of each material. (2)\n    Stainless steel: strong, does not rust or react with acid foods, and is easy to clean.\n    Aluminium: light, cheap, and conducts heat very well, so food heats quickly.\n\n(b) The use of any TWO items in the first aid kit (Figure 1). (2)\n    The figure isn't visible in this copy. Common items:\n    Adhesive plasters: to cover small cuts and grazes and keep them clean.\n    Crêpe or roller bandage: to hold dressings in place or support a sprain.\n    Antiseptic solution or wipes: to clean wounds and kill germs. (Also: scissors to cut bandages and dressings;\n     tweezers to remove splinters; burn gel to cool and soothe minor burns; triangular bandage to make a sling.)\n\n(c)(i) Two ways the cherries in the freezer could have spoiled. (2)\n    The packaging was not airtight or moisture-proof, so air entered and caused freezer burn and oxidation (dry,\n     discoloured, shrivelled fruit).\n    The freezer temperature went up and down (power cuts, door left open), so the fruit partly thawed and\n     refroze, letting enzymes and micro-organisms act. Six months may also have been too long a storage time.\n\n(c)(ii) A suitable packaging material, with a reason. (2)\n    Heavy-duty polythene freezer bags with the air pressed out and sealed tightly, or rigid plastic freezer\n    containers with tight-fitting lids. Reason: they are airtight, moisture- and vapour-proof, and do not crack at low\n    temperatures, so they protect the fruit from freezer burn and off-odours.\n\n(c)(iii) FOUR guidelines for storing the cherries in the freezer. (4)\n    Freeze only fresh, firm, ripe, unblemished cherries. Wash, drain and dry them well first.\n    Pack them in suitable airtight freezer packaging, remove as much air as possible, and leave a little headspace\n     if they are packed in syrup.\n    Label each package with the contents and date, and use them within the recommended time (about 6–12\n     months), first in, first out.\n    Freeze quickly at −18°C or below, keep the freezer temperature steady, avoid opening the door often, and\n     defrost the freezer regularly.\n\n(d) Two food hygiene and safety factors Brian should use to judge the most hygienic shops. (2)\n    Cleanliness of the premises: clean floors, counters and display cases, no pests, and proper waste disposal.\n    Food handlers' hygiene and correct storage: staff with clean uniforms and covered hair who use tongs or\n     gloves; raw and cooked foods kept apart; food covered and stored at the correct temperatures (also accept a\n     valid food-handler's permit or health certificate).","6":"(a)(i) Two examples of TVP products. (2)\n    Soya chunks\n    Soya mince (also accept veggie burgers, soya sausages)\n\n(a)(ii) What the expiry date \"18 October 2014\" means to consumers. (1)\n    It is the last date by which the product should be eaten. After that date it may not be safe, or may have lost its\n    quality and nutritional value, so it should not be used.\n\n(a)(iii) Three other pieces of information on the label. (3)\n    Name and brand of the product\n    List of ingredients\n    Net weight; also accept nutrition facts, the manufacturer's name and address, and preparation or storage\n     instructions.\n\n(b)(i) Debate response: discuss FOUR disadvantages of convenience foods. (8)\n    They are more expensive. Convenience foods cost much more than the raw ingredients, because you pay for\n    the processing, packaging and advertising. A family that relies on them spends more of its food budget for less\n    food.\n    They are often high in salt, sugar, fat and additives. To improve flavour and shelf life, many convenience foods\n    contain large amounts of sodium, sugar, saturated fat, preservatives and colourings. Eating them regularly\n    increases the risk of hypertension, obesity, diabetes and heart disease, and some people react to the additives.\n    They may be lower in nutritional value. Processing destroys some vitamins (especially C and B), and many\n    convenience foods are low in fibre. Portions are often small, so they may not satisfy the appetite or supply a\n    balanced meal.\n    They limit creativity and skills, and create waste. Relying on ready-made foods means people lose cooking\n    skills and the flavour can be monotonous. The large amount of packaging (plastics, tins, foil) adds to\n    environmental pollution. Some convenience foods do not suit special diets or local tastes.\n\n(b)(ii) TWO groups of convenience foods for a quick main dish. (2)\n    Canned foods (for example canned tuna, corned beef, red beans or mackerel).\n    Frozen foods (for example frozen chicken pieces, fish fillets, mixed vegetables); also accept dried or\n     dehydrated foods such as instant rice, pasta or packet sauces.","7":"(a) Two factors when planning meals. (2)\n    Nutritional needs of the family\n    Money available (also accept time, likes and dislikes, foods in season, religion)\n\n(b) Match utensils A–D in the lunch cover to the names. (4)\n    The figure isn't visible in this copy. Use these standard placement rules to match the labels:\n    Dinner fork: on the left of the dinner plate.\n    Dessert fork: above the plate, handle pointing left.\n    Soup spoon: on the far right, outside the knife.\n    Dessert spoon: above the plate, above the dessert fork, handle pointing right.\n\n\n(c)(i) THREE hygiene practices by the cooks. (3)\n    Washing hands thoroughly before handling food, after the toilet, and after touching raw meat.\n    Wearing clean uniforms, aprons and hair coverings, and covering cuts with waterproof plasters.\n    Using separate boards and utensils for raw and cooked foods, cooking food thoroughly, and keeping work\n     surfaces clean and sanitized.\n\n(c)(ii) THREE hygiene practices by the waiters and waitresses. (3)\n    Holding plates by the rims, glasses by the base and cutlery by the handles. Never touching the food or the\n     eating surfaces.\n    Wearing clean uniforms, with hair neatly tied back, short clean nails and clean hands.\n    Covering food while carrying it, not coughing or sneezing near food, and wiping tables with clean, sanitized\n     cloths.\n\n(d) Explain how brisket becomes tender in a pressure cooker. (4)\n    Brisket is a tough cut because it contains a lot of connective tissue (collagen) from well-exercised muscles. In a\n    pressure cooker the steam is trapped, which raises the pressure inside to about 15 psi above normal. This\n    raises the boiling point of the water to about 121°C, higher than in an ordinary pot. The moist heat at this\n    higher temperature converts the tough, insoluble collagen into soft, soluble gelatin much faster than ordinary\n    stewing. The muscle fibres then separate easily and the meat becomes tender. What takes 2–3 hours of\n    stewing takes about 30–45 minutes in a pressure cooker, saving time and fuel."},"2015":{"1":"(a) The nutrient whose deficiency may lead to (i) goitre and (ii) night blindness. (2)\n    (i) Goitre: iodine.\n    (ii) Night blindness: vitamin A.\n\n(b) Four dietary guidelines for losing weight. (4)\n    Reduce fatty and fried foods. Grill, bake or steam instead.\n    Cut down on sugar, sweets, pastries and sugary drinks. Drink water instead.\n    Eat plenty of high-fibre foods: vegetables, fruit, wholegrains and legumes.\n    Eat smaller portions at regular times, do not skip breakfast, avoid late-night snacking (and exercise regularly).\n\n(c)(i) Discuss THREE effects of regularly eating fried chicken, macaroni cheese pie and sweet drinks. (6)\n    Weight gain and obesity. These foods are very high in calories from fat and sugar but low in fibre, so students\n    easily eat more energy than they use. The extra is stored as body fat, leading to overweight and obesity, which\n    affects self-esteem and fitness.\n    Higher risk of lifestyle diseases. The saturated fat in fried chicken and cheese raises blood cholesterol, and the\n    large amounts of sugar in carbonated drinks raise blood glucose. Over time this increases the risk of type 2\n    diabetes, high blood pressure and heart disease, even at a young age. The salt in these foods also adds to the\n    risk of hypertension.\n    Nutrient deficiencies, tooth decay and poor concentration. The meal lacks fruit and vegetables, so students\n    may lack vitamins A and C, minerals and fibre (leading to constipation and lowered immunity). The sugar and\n    acid in sweet drinks cause tooth decay. Sugar \"highs\" followed by crashes can lead to tiredness and poor\n    concentration in class.\n\n(c)(ii) A well-balanced canteen menu. (4)\n    Baked (or grilled) jerk chicken\n    Rice and peas (or a wholewheat roti)\n    Steamed mixed vegetables (callaloo, carrots, string beans) and a coleslaw\n    A slice of watermelon or a banana\n    Fresh fruit juice with no added sugar, or water","2":"(a)(i) Two reasons for preserving food. (2)\n    To prevent food from spoiling, so it lasts longer and is not wasted.\n    To make seasonal foods available all year round (also accept: adds variety, saves money, allows foods to be\n     transported and stored, makes surplus useful).\n\n(a)(ii) Two food additives used in preserves. (2)\n    Pectin (setting agent)\n    Citric acid, or sodium benzoate (preservative), or food colouring\n\n\n(a)(iii) Define \"sterilization\". (2)\n    Sterilization is the process of heating food, containers or equipment to a high temperature (above boiling\n    point, about 121°C, or by boiling for a long time) for a set period, in order to destroy ALL micro-organisms and\n    their spores, so the product keeps for a long time.\n\n(b) Explain the scientific principle that prevents microbial growth in each. (6)\n    (i) Guava jam: high sugar concentration (osmosis). The jam contains about 60–65% sugar. This strong sugar\n    solution draws water out of any micro-organism cells by osmosis, so they become dehydrated and cannot\n    grow. The high temperature during boiling also kills micro-organisms, and the sealed sterilized jar stops new\n    ones getting in.\n    (ii) Pickled cucumbers: acid (low pH). The vinegar (acetic acid) lowers the pH to below 4.5. Most bacteria,\n    including harmful ones, cannot survive or multiply in such acidic conditions, so the cucumbers are preserved.\n    (iii) Salted codfish: salt (osmosis and dehydration). Salt draws water out of both the fish tissue and any\n    microbial cells by osmosis. With little available water, bacteria and moulds cannot grow. The fish is also dried,\n    which removes more moisture.\n\n(c) ONE reason for each problem. (4)\n    (i) Crystals on the candied fruit: the syrup was too concentrated (too much sugar), or it was not concentrated\n     gradually enough, or it was stirred while boiling, so the sugar recrystallized. Too little acid or glucose can also\n     cause crystallization.\n    (ii) Sorrel jelly became dark: it was boiled for too long or too fast (the sugar caramelized), or it was cooked in\n     an iron or aluminium pan that reacted with the acid, or it was stored in a bright, warm place.","3":"(a)(i) Define \"boiling\". (1)\n    Boiling is a moist method of cooking food in liquid (usually water or stock) at 100°C, where large bubbles break\n    rapidly on the surface.\n\n(a)(ii) Two advantages of boiling. (2)\n    It is simple, safe and needs little attention or skill.\n    It uses no fat, so it is suitable for low-fat diets, and it tenderizes tough foods, making them easy to digest.\n\n(b) How each is used in food preparation. (3)\n    (i) Roux: a cooked mixture of equal amounts of fat and flour, used as the base for thickening sauces, soups\n     and gravies (for example white sauce).\n    (ii) Sauté: to fry food quickly in a small amount of hot fat, tossing it, to brown and soften it (for example\n     sautéing onions or vegetables).\n    (iii) Garnish: an edible decoration added to savoury dishes to improve their appearance and colour (for\n     example a parsley sprig, lemon wedges or tomato roses).\n\n(c) Discuss ONE effect of any THREE of the practices on flaky pastry. (6)\n    (i) Lemon juice added: the acid strengthens and softens the gluten, making the dough more elastic so it can be\n    rolled and folded many times without breaking. This gives well-defined, crisp, even flaky layers.\n    (ii) Oven temperature too low: the fat melts and runs out of the pastry before the starch can set and the\n    steam can push the layers apart. The pastry becomes greasy, heavy and hard, with poor rise and pale colour,\n    instead of light and flaky.\n\n\n    (iii) Dough stretched to cover the pies: stretching the dough stretches the gluten strands. In the oven they\n    contract (shrink) back to their original length, so the pastry lid shrinks, pulls away from the edges and may tear,\n    leaving the filling exposed.\n    (iv) Holes pricked with a fork: the holes let steam escape during baking, preventing the pastry from bubbling\n    up or bursting and the filling from boiling over. They also stop the pastry becoming soggy underneath.\n\n(d) TWO precautions so the pineapple jello sets, with a reason for each. (4)\n    Cook the fresh pineapple first (boil or stew it for a few minutes), or use canned pineapple. Reason: fresh\n    pineapple contains the enzyme bromelain, which breaks down the protein in gelatin and stops it setting. Heat\n    destroys (denatures) the enzyme.\n    Measure the gelatin and liquid accurately, and chill it long enough in the refrigerator. Reason: too much\n    liquid or too little gelatin gives a weak, runny jelly. Gelatin needs low temperatures and several hours to form a\n    firm gel. Also add the fruit only when the jelly is partly set, so it is evenly distributed.","4":"(a) Two functions of protein. (2)\n    Growth and repair of body tissues.\n    Production of enzymes, hormones and antibodies (also accept: a secondary source of energy).\n\n(b)(i) Name parts A and B in the egg diagram. (2)\n    The diagram isn't readable in this copy. The usual labels are: A: yolk; B: albumen (egg white). Other possible\n    labels are the shell, air sac, chalaza and shell membrane, so check the diagram.\n\n(b)(ii) Two nutrients, other than protein, in part A (the yolk). (2)\n    Fat (including cholesterol)\n    Iron, vitamin A, vitamin D or B vitamins (riboflavin)\n\n(c)(i) Explain TWO guidelines for complementary feeding of infants. (4)\n    Start at about 6 months, with one new food at a time. Breast milk alone meets the baby's needs for the first 6\n    months. After that the baby needs extra energy, iron and zinc. Introduce single foods (for example puréed\n    pumpkin, then mashed banana) one at a time, a few days apart, so any allergy or intolerance can be identified.\n    Make the food soft, smooth, plain and hygienic, and gradually increase the texture and variety. Begin with\n    smooth purées and porridges, then move on to mashed and finely chopped foods as the baby learns to chew.\n    Do not add salt, sugar or spices. Prepare the food with clean hands and utensils, using safe water, to prevent\n    diarrhoea.\n\n(c)(ii) Using the double-mix principle, a suitable meal for an infant. (2)\n    Mashed pumpkin and rice (staple + vegetable), or cornmeal porridge made with milk (staple + food from\n    animals), or mashed sweet potato with a little flaked fish.\n\n(d) FOUR advantages of continuing to breastfeed during complementary feeding. (4)\n    Breast milk continues to supply high-quality nutrients (protein, fat, vitamins), covering any gaps in the new\n     foods.\n    It contains antibodies that protect the baby against infections, diarrhoea and allergies.\n    It is clean, at the right temperature, always available, and free, which saves the family money.\n\n\n    It continues the emotional bond between mother and baby, gives comfort, and helps the mother's health (it\n     lowers her risk of breast and ovarian cancer and helps with spacing births).","5":"(a)(i) Three causes of burns in the kitchen. (3)\n    Touching hot pans, oven shelves or dishes without oven gloves.\n    Hot fat splashing, or a pan of oil catching fire.\n    Steam from kettles, pots or pressure cookers, or spilled boiling liquids (scalds); also accept loose sleeves\n     catching fire from a gas flame.\n\n(a)(ii) Three ways of preventing burns. (3)\n    Always use dry oven gloves or pot holders to handle hot items.\n    Turn pan handles inwards, away from the edge of the stove, and keep children away from the cooker.\n    Dry food before putting it into hot oil, lower it in gently, never leave frying unattended, and wear fitted\n     clothing with sleeves rolled up.\n\n(b) Explain ONE reason for each refrigerator guideline. (6)\n    (i) Fresh milk should be covered: milk easily absorbs strong odours from other foods (onions, fish), which spoil\n    its flavour. Covering also keeps out bacteria and stops the milk drying out.\n    (ii) Cooked food should not be stored hot: hot food raises the temperature inside the fridge, which can allow\n    bacteria in other foods to multiply. It also makes the motor work harder, wasting electricity, and causes\n    condensation and frost build-up. Cool food quickly, within about 2 hours, and then refrigerate it.\n    (iii) Food should not be packed tightly: cold air must circulate freely around the food to keep every part at a\n    safe, even temperature. Tight packing blocks the airflow, leaving warm spots where food spoils faster.\n\n(c) Explain what causes any TWO of the spoilages. (4)\n    (i) Milk turns sour: lactic acid bacteria present in milk multiply quickly at room temperature. They feed on the\n    milk sugar (lactose) and turn it into lactic acid, which gives a sour taste and makes the milk protein (casein)\n    curdle.\n    (ii) Bread goes mouldy: mould spores from the air land on the bread. Bread is moist and contains starch, which\n    the mould feeds on, so in warm, humid conditions the spores germinate and grow into fuzzy patches.\n    (iii) Oil becomes rancid: oxygen in the air reacts with the fat molecules (oxidation), helped by light and\n    warmth, producing off flavours and smells. Rancidity can also be caused by enzymes or moisture breaking\n    down the fat (hydrolytic rancidity).\n    (iv) Peeled vegetables discolour: this is enzymic browning. When the cells are cut, enzymes (polyphenol\n    oxidase) are released and react with oxygen in the air and with compounds in the vegetable, forming brown\n    pigments.","6":"(a) Two factors to consider when buying (i) cucumbers and (ii) fresh meat. (4)\n    (i) Cucumbers: firm, with bright green skin and no soft spots, yellowing or wrinkles; medium-sized and slender\n     (very large ones are seedy and bitter).\n    (ii) Fresh meat: bright red lean with creamy-white fat, firm and slightly moist, with a fresh smell (not slimy or\n     smelly); bought from a clean outlet where it is refrigerated, and the right cut for the cooking method.\n\n\n(b)(i) Define \"net weight\". (2)\n    Net weight is the weight of the food product alone, not including the weight of the can, packaging or container.\n\n(b)(ii) Explain THREE steps in making sweetened condensed milk. (6)\n    Standardizing and heating (pasteurizing) the milk. Fresh milk is tested and its fat content adjusted. It is then\n    heated to kill harmful bacteria and to stabilize the proteins, so the milk will not thicken or curdle during\n    storage.\n    Adding sugar and evaporating. About 40–45% sugar is dissolved in the milk, which is then heated under\n    reduced pressure (a vacuum) at a low temperature. This removes about 60% of the water without scorching\n    the milk. The high sugar concentration acts as a preservative by osmosis.\n    Cooling, seeding and canning. The thick milk is cooled quickly while being stirred, and seeded with tiny lactose\n    crystals so that only very small crystals form, giving a smooth texture rather than grittiness. It is then filled into\n    sterilized cans, which are sealed and labelled. Because the sugar preserves it, it does not need further\n    sterilizing.\n\n(c) FOUR reasons to keep shopping at the new supermarket rather than the small shop. (4)\n    Wider variety of foods and brands, so everything can be bought in one place.\n    Lower prices and special offers, because supermarkets buy in bulk, with unit pricing to compare value.\n    Better storage and hygiene: refrigeration and freezer sections, labelled goods with expiry dates, regular stock\n     rotation.\n    Convenience and services: parking, trolleys, air conditioning, longer opening hours, card payments, receipts\n     and refund policies.","7":"(a)(i) Define \"hors d'oeuvre\". (2)\n    An hors d'oeuvre is a small, light, savoury appetizer served at the beginning of a meal, or with drinks before it,\n    to stimulate the appetite.\n\n(a)(ii) One example. (1)\n    Stuffed eggs (devilled eggs), or saltfish fritters, melon balls, or prawn cocktail.\n\n(b) The components of a three-course menu. (3)\n    1. Starter (appetizer or soup) · 2. Main course · 3. Dessert (sweet)\n\n(c) Discuss THREE factors when planning meals for a luncheon for 100 people. (6)\n    Budget and cost per person. The department must work out the total money available and the cost per plate,\n    then choose dishes that are affordable in large quantities (for example chicken instead of shrimp, and local\n    foods in season), while still making a profit for the fund-raiser.\n    Equipment, space, staff and time available. Cooking for 100 people needs large pots, ovens, refrigeration and\n    serving dishes, plus enough helpers. Dishes that can be prepared in advance, cooked in bulk and held hot or\n    cold safely (baked chicken, rice dishes, salads, cakes) are best. The time plan must allow for preparation and\n    serving.\n    The guests and the occasion. Consider their ages, likely religious and dietary needs (vegetarian, no pork,\n    diabetic), and the formality of the event. Offer a balanced menu with a vegetarian option, attractive colours\n    and textures, and a suitable method of service (buffet or plated).\n\n\n(d) Explain how to adjust a 20-serving cake recipe (4 cups flour, 2 cups sugar) to make 100 servings. (4)\n  To go from 20 servings to 100 servings, divide the new number by the original: 100 ÷ 20 = 5. Multiply every\n  ingredient by 5.\n  - Flour: 4 cups × 5 = 20 cups\n  - Sugar: 2 cups × 5 = 10 cups\n  All the other ingredients (fat, eggs, liquid, raising agent) are multiplied by 5 too, so the proportions stay the\n  same. The mixture should be baked in several batches or tins of the usual size, because a single huge cake\n  would not cook through evenly. Baking times stay about the same for normal-sized tins."},"2016":{"1":"(a) Define \"undernutrition\". (2)\n    Undernutrition is a form of malnutrition caused by not eating enough food, or enough of one or more nutrients\n    (especially energy and protein), over a period of time. It leads to weight loss, poor growth and deficiency\n    diseases.\n\n(b) One sign or symptom of each disorder. (3)\n    (i) Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM): stunted growth and a \"wasted\" look (marasmus), or swollen belly and\n     legs (oedema) with patchy, discoloured hair (kwashiorkor).\n    (ii) Anorexia nervosa: extreme weight loss and thinness, with a strong fear of gaining weight (also accept:\n     stopped periods, fine body hair).\n    (iii) Dietary anaemia: pale skin and inner eyelids, with constant tiredness and breathlessness.\n\n(c) One function of dietary fibre. (1)\n    It adds bulk to the faeces and helps them move through the intestines, preventing constipation.\n\n(d) Plan a THREE-course meal for a vegan. (4)\n    Starter: pumpkin soup made with vegetable stock and coconut milk, with wholewheat croutons\n    Main course: curried chickpeas (channa) and potato, brown rice, steamed callaloo, and a tomato and\n    cucumber salad\n    Dessert: fresh fruit salad (mango, pineapple, paw paw) with toasted coconut\n    Drink: sorrel or fresh lime juice\n\n(e) Discuss THREE possible health benefits of a vegan diet. (6)\n    Lower risk of heart disease. A vegan diet contains no animal fats, so it is very low in saturated fat and has no\n    cholesterol. This helps keep blood cholesterol low and the arteries clear, reducing the risk of heart attack and\n    stroke.\n    Better weight control and a lower risk of type 2 diabetes. Plant foods are generally lower in calories and\n    higher in fibre, so they fill you up with fewer calories. Fibre also slows down the absorption of sugar, helping to\n    keep blood glucose steady. Vegans tend to have a lower BMI.\n    Better digestion and a lower risk of some cancers. The high fibre content prevents constipation and\n    diverticular disease, and is linked to a lower risk of bowel cancer. Fruits and vegetables are rich in vitamins (A,\n    C, E) and antioxidants that protect body cells, and a vegan diet is also usually lower in salt, which helps prevent\n    hypertension.","2":"(a)(i) Two tests to show jam has set. (2)\n    Wrinkle (cold plate or saucer) test\n    Flake test (also accept the thermometer test, at 105°C)\n\n(a)(ii) Describe ONE test. (2)\n\n\n    Wrinkle test: put a saucer in the freezer beforehand. Remove the jam from the heat, put a teaspoon of it on\n    the cold saucer and leave it to cool for a minute. Push the jam with your finger. If the surface wrinkles and the\n    jam does not run back together, it has reached setting point. If it is still runny, boil for a few more minutes and\n    test again.\n\n(a)(iii) Two guidelines, other than pectin, when selecting the mangoes. (2)\n    Choose firm, just-ripe mangoes (slightly under-ripe fruit has more acid and pectin, and gives a good flavour).\n    Select fruit that is fresh, sound and unblemished, with no bruises, rot or insect damage (also accept: a variety\n     with a good flavour and little fibre).\n\n(a)(iv) Two ways to increase the pectin content so the jam sets. (2)\n    Add a fruit high in pectin, such as green (unripe) apples, guava or citrus peel, or add commercial pectin.\n    Add acid (lime or lemon juice), which helps release the pectin from the fruit and helps it form a gel (also\n     accept: boil the fruit slowly before adding sugar to extract the pectin).\n\n(b) Explain why each procedure is important when freezing green beans. (8)\n    (i) Washing: removes soil, dust, insects and pesticide residues, and reduces the number of micro-organisms on\n    the beans before freezing, so the frozen product is clean and safe.\n    (ii) Blanching: dipping the beans in boiling water for 2–3 minutes destroys (inactivates) the enzymes that would\n    otherwise keep working even in the freezer, causing loss of colour, flavour, texture and vitamins. It also kills\n    surface bacteria and keeps the beans bright green.\n    (iii) Sealing the package: an airtight, moisture-proof seal keeps air out, preventing freezer burn (dry,\n    discoloured patches caused by moisture loss) and oxidation. It also stops the beans absorbing odours from\n    other foods and stops them drying out.\n    (iv) Rate of freezing: the beans should be frozen quickly. Quick freezing forms small ice crystals that do not\n    damage the cell walls, so the beans keep their crisp texture when thawed. Slow freezing forms large ice crystals\n    that rupture the cells, so the beans become limp and soggy and lose nutrients in the drip.","3":"(a) Three types of batters. (3)\n    Pouring batter (for pancakes and Yorkshire pudding)\n    Coating batter (for fish and chicken)\n    Fritter batter (for banana or saltfish fritters)\n\n(b) One function of each ingredient in yeast products. (3)\n    (i) Salt: adds flavour, and strengthens the gluten. It also controls the rate of yeast fermentation; too much kills\n     the yeast.\n    (ii) Sugar: food for the yeast, so it produces carbon dioxide faster. It also adds sweetness and helps the crust\n     brown.\n    (iii) Eggs: add richness, colour and food value, and help the dough rise and set.\n\n(c) Why Paul uses each technique in the creamed fruit cake (a different reason each). (4)\n    (i) Sieving: removes lumps and foreign particles from the flour, and introduces air.\n    (ii) Creaming: beating the fat and sugar together traps tiny air bubbles, making a light, fluffy mixture that gives\n     the cake volume.\n\n\n    (iii) Whisking (the eggs): beats air into the eggs and helps them blend evenly into the creamed mixture\n     without curdling.\n    (iv) Folding: gently combines the flour and fruit with a metal spoon, without knocking out the air already\n     trapped and without developing too much gluten.\n\n(d) One procedure to prevent each fault. (6)\n    (i) Fruit sank to the bottom: wash and thoroughly dry the fruit, then toss it in a little of the measured flour\n    before adding it. This coats the fruit so it does not sink. Also, do not make the mixture too wet or soft, and do\n    not use too much raising agent.\n    (ii) Cake sank in the middle: do not open the oven door during the first part of baking, because the sudden\n    drop in temperature makes the cake collapse before it sets. Measure the raising agent and liquid accurately,\n    and bake for the full time at the correct temperature.\n    (iii) Crisp, sugary crust: use caster sugar (fine sugar) instead of granulated sugar, measure the sugar accurately,\n    and cream the fat and sugar thoroughly so the sugar dissolves. Bake at the correct (not too high) temperature.","4":"(a) Using an example, define \"invisible fat\". (2)\n    Invisible fat is fat that is hidden within a food and cannot easily be seen or removed, for example the fat in egg\n    yolk, cheese, nuts, avocado, milk, cakes and pastries. (Visible fat can be seen, such as butter, oil or the fat on\n    meat.)\n\n(b)(i) Four factors that may influence the nutritional status of the elderly. (4)\n    Low income (a pension), so they can afford less nutritious food.\n    Poor teeth or dentures, making chewing difficult.\n    Reduced appetite, poorer sense of taste and smell, and loneliness or living alone.\n    Illness and medication, limited mobility to shop and cook, and a slower metabolism.\n\n(b)(ii) A preparation practice, with a reason, to keep the nutrients in dried beans, apple and lettuce. (6)\n    Dried beans: soak them overnight in cold water, then cook them in the same soaking water, or in a pressure\n    cooker. Soaking shortens the cooking time (less loss of B vitamins), and using the soaking water keeps the\n    water-soluble vitamins and minerals that leached out. Do not add bicarbonate of soda, because it destroys\n    thiamine (vitamin B1).\n    Apple: wash it and eat it with the skin on, or cut it just before serving and sprinkle with lemon juice. Much of\n    the fibre and vitamin C is in or just under the skin. Cutting it just before serving, and using lemon juice, prevents\n    vitamin C being lost and stops enzymic browning.\n    Lettuce: wash the leaves quickly under running water, do not soak them, and tear (rather than cut) them just\n    before serving. Dry them well and keep them chilled. Soaking dissolves vitamin C and B vitamins, and preparing\n    it early lets air destroy the vitamin C.\n\n(c) Explain why the doctor recommended more of any TWO nutrients for the pregnant woman. (4)\n    Calcium: the baby's bones and teeth are forming and need a great deal of calcium. If the mother's diet does\n    not supply enough, the calcium is taken from her own bones and teeth, weakening them and increasing her\n    later risk of osteoporosis.\n\n\n    Iron: her blood volume increases greatly during pregnancy, and the baby must build up its own store of iron for\n    the first months of life. Extra iron is needed to make haemoglobin and prevent iron-deficiency anaemia, which\n    causes tiredness and increases the risks during childbirth.\n    (Dietary fibre: pregnancy hormones slow the digestive tract, so constipation and haemorrhoids are common.\n    Fibre and water keep the bowels regular.)","5":"(a) Two factors when choosing a refrigerator. (2)\n    Size and capacity, to suit the family and the kitchen space.\n    Energy efficiency (energy rating), and the cost (also accept: frost-free features, guarantee, number of doors).\n\n(b) A different small electrical appliance for any TWO items. (2)\n    (i) Croutons: a toaster or toaster oven.\n    (ii) Fresh citrus fruit punch: an electric juicer (citrus press) or a blender.\n    (iii) Mayonnaise: a blender, food processor or electric hand mixer.\n    (iv) Bread: a bread maker (or a stand mixer with a dough hook).\n\n(c)(i) Two symptoms of food poisoning from the chowder. (2)\n    Vomiting and nausea\n    Diarrhoea and stomach cramps (also accept fever)\n\n(c)(ii) Explain THREE factors that may have contributed to contamination of the chowder. (6)\n    It was held in the temperature danger zone for 6 hours. The heat was turned off at 6 a.m. and the chowder\n    cooled slowly on the stove until noon. It spent hours between 5°C and 63°C, the range in which bacteria\n    multiply fastest. It should have been kept hot (above 63°C) or cooled quickly and refrigerated.\n    Milk and fish are high-risk foods. Both are moist, high in protein and low in acid, which makes them ideal food\n    for bacteria such as Salmonella, Staphylococcus and Clostridium perfringens. Some of these produce toxins or\n    spores that survive cooking.\n    Poor hygiene and the pot being uncovered. Workers may have handled the soup with unwashed hands or\n    utensils, and the uncovered pot could have been contaminated by flies, dust or droplets from coughing. It was\n    then not reheated thoroughly before serving.\n\n(c)(iii) FOUR hygiene and food-safety factors when buying the milk and fish. (4)\n    Check the expiry or \"use-by\" date on the milk, and buy pasteurized milk that is kept refrigerated in the store.\n    Make sure the milk carton or bottle is sealed and undamaged, not bulging, leaking or dented.\n    Buy fish that is fresh: bright, bulging eyes, red gills, firm flesh and a fresh sea smell. It should be displayed on\n     ice or refrigerated.\n    Buy from a clean, licensed outlet with clean staff (hair covered, clean hands and gloves), and take chilled\n     foods home quickly in a cooler.","6":"(a) One difference between white fish and oily fish. (2)\n    In white fish (for example snapper, cod, grouper), the oil is stored only in the liver, so the flesh is low in fat\n    (under 2%). In oily fish (for example salmon, sardines, mackerel, herring), the oil is spread throughout the flesh\n    (5–20% fat), giving darker flesh that is rich in omega-3 fatty acids and vitamins A and D.\n\n\n(b) Briefly describe TWO methods of heat transfer in cooking. (4)\n    Conduction: heat passes from molecule to molecule through a solid by direct contact, for example from the hot\n    stove through the metal base of a frying pan into the food, as in frying an egg.\n    Convection: in liquids and gases, the heated part becomes less dense and rises, while the cooler part sinks to\n    replace it. This sets up currents that carry heat around, for example in boiling water or the hot air circulating in\n    an oven. (Also accept radiation: heat rays travel in straight lines from a source such as a grill directly to the\n    food.)\n\n(c)(i) Discuss THREE shopping guidelines to help Mrs Otis provide nutritious meals despite high food costs. (6)\n    Plan meals and shop with a list and a budget. Planning the week's meals and writing a shopping list before\n    going to the market stops impulse buying of expensive snacks and convenience foods, so the money goes on\n    nutritious foods the family needs.\n    Buy local foods in season and cheaper protein alternatives. Fruits and vegetables in season are cheapest and\n    most nutritious. Local ground provisions (yam, breadfruit, green banana) are cheaper than imported starches.\n    Use peas, beans, eggs, chicken back and canned sardines instead of expensive cuts of beef. Combining legumes\n    with cereals (rice and peas) gives good-quality protein cheaply.\n    Compare prices and buy wisely. Compare unit prices and brands, buy store brands, take advantage of genuine\n    special offers, and buy non-perishables (rice, flour, dried peas) in bulk when this is cheaper. Buy only the\n    amount of perishables that can be used before they spoil, to avoid waste.\n\n(c)(ii) TWO types of information for the guava cheese label, with a reason. (4)\n    List of ingredients (guava, sugar, lime juice). Reason: customers know what is in the product, which matters\n    for people who must avoid certain foods (for example diabetics watching sugar), and it is required by law.\n    Net weight and the best-before date (also accept the producer's name and address). Reason: the net weight\n    lets customers judge value for money and compare prices. The date tells them how long the product will stay\n    at its best, so they do not eat spoiled food. The name and address allow them to contact Mr Otis or buy again.","7":"(a) One floor material for a modern kitchen, and why it suits. (2)\n    Non-slip ceramic tiles: they are hard-wearing, waterproof and easy to clean, and the non-slip surface reduces\n    falls. (Also accept vinyl: cushioned, waterproof, cheap and easy to clean.)\n\n(b) FOUR guidelines, other than pre-preparation, for writing a time plan. (4)\n    List the dishes in the order they will be prepared, starting with those that take longest to cook or must cool or\n     set (stews, jellies, cakes).\n    Include accurate times for each task, including oven pre-heating and cooking times.\n    Dovetail tasks, so jobs are done while other foods are cooking.\n    Include time for washing up, clearing as you go, garnishing, dishing up and serving, and include a list of\n     equipment and ingredients.\n\n(c) Write a plan for the main course of a lunch menu. (4)\n    Main course: baked herb chicken, rice and peas, glazed carrots, and a tossed green salad.\n    Time plan (for serving at 12:30 p.m.)\n    10:30 Collect equipment and ingredients. Season the chicken and leave it to marinate. Soak or check the peas.\n    10:45 Preheat the oven to 190°C. Put the peas to cook (pressure cooker).\n\n\n  11:00 Put the chicken in the oven (about 1 hour). Wash up.\n  11:15 Add the rice, coconut milk and seasonings to the peas, and simmer.\n  11:45 Peel and slice the carrots and steam them. Wash the salad vegetables and refrigerate them.\n  12:10 Glaze the carrots with butter and a little sugar. Toss the salad and add the dressing.\n  12:20 Check the chicken is cooked. Dish up all items into warm dishes and garnish with parsley and tomato.\n  12:30 Serve.\n\n(d) Explain how spoilage of each food can be minimized during storage. (6)\n  (i) Flour: store it in a clean, dry, airtight container in a cool cupboard. This keeps out moisture (which causes\n  mould and lumps) and insects such as weevils. Use old stock before new (first in, first out), and do not add new\n  flour on top of old.\n  (ii) Oranges: store them in a cool, airy place or in the refrigerator crisper, not in a sealed plastic bag. Check\n  them regularly and remove any bruised or mouldy fruit, since mould spreads quickly from one orange to the\n  others.\n  (iii) Meat: keep it refrigerated (below 5°C) and use it within 1–2 days, or wrap it airtight, label it and freeze it at\n  −18°C. Store raw meat covered on the bottom shelf so its juices cannot drip onto other foods. Cold slows the\n  growth of bacteria and enzyme activity."},"2017":{"1":"(a) Three types of diets that contribute to good health and wellbeing. (3)\n    A balanced diet (all nutrients in the right proportions).\n    A high-fibre diet (wholegrains, fruit and vegetables).\n    A low-fat, low-salt, low-sugar diet (also accept: a vegetarian diet, the DASH diet, a diabetic diet).\n\n(b) Explain THREE ways obesity affects the Caribbean. (6)\n    More lifestyle diseases and pressure on health services. Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes,\n    hypertension, heart disease, stroke and some cancers. As obesity rises, hospitals and clinics must treat more\n    patients, which uses up government health budgets and lengthens waiting times for everyone.\n    Lost productivity and economic cost. Obese workers are more likely to be ill, to miss work, and to die or retire\n    early because of complications. This reduces the size and output of the workforce and costs the economy\n    money in medical care, lost working days and disability benefits.\n    Effects on families and young people. Childhood obesity is rising in the Caribbean, so children are developing\n    diabetes and high blood pressure at younger ages. Families spend more of their income on medication and\n    treatment, and obese people may suffer low self-esteem, bullying and depression, which affects their quality of\n    life.\n\n(c)(i) Three vulnerable groups in the Caribbean. (3)\n    Infants and young children\n    Pregnant and breastfeeding women\n    The elderly (also accept: the poor, the chronically ill, people with disabilities)\n\n(c)(ii) Discuss TWO factors that can threaten the food security of ONE of these groups, leading to\nundernutrition. (4)\n    Group: the elderly.\n    Low, fixed income. Most elderly people live on small pensions. When food prices rise they cannot afford\n    enough protein foods, fruit and vegetables, so they eat less or buy only cheap starchy foods, which leads to\n    undernutrition.\n    Poor health and limited mobility. Arthritis, weak eyesight, poor teeth and chronic illness make it hard for them\n    to shop, carry groceries, cook or chew. Elderly people who live alone often skip meals because of loneliness or\n    a poor appetite, so they become undernourished.","2":"(a) Three functions of calcium in the diet. (3)\n    Builds and maintains strong bones and teeth (with vitamin D and phosphorus).\n    Needed for normal blood clotting.\n    Needed for muscle contraction (including the heartbeat) and nerve function.\n\n(b)(i) Three non-haem sources of iron. (3)\n    Non-haem iron is the iron found in plant foods (and in eggs). Examples:\n    Dark-green leafy vegetables: callaloo, spinach, pak choi.\n\n\n    Peas and beans (legumes): red kidney beans, lentils, chickpeas.\n    Wholegrain or iron-fortified cereals and bread, and dried fruits such as raisins and prunes.\n\n(b)(ii) ONE reason iron sources are especially important for strict vegetarians. (2)\n    Strict vegetarians eat no meat, fish or poultry, which are the best sources of haem iron (the form the body\n    absorbs most easily). They depend only on non-haem iron from plants, which is poorly absorbed, and the\n    absorption is further reduced by substances in plant foods (phytates and fibre). So they must eat plenty of iron-\n    rich plant foods, together with vitamin C, to avoid iron-deficiency anaemia.\n\n(c) Describe in detail how a person may become obese from eating too much protein. (4)\n    Protein supplies energy: 1 gram gives about 4 kcal. The body can only use a certain amount of protein each day\n    for growth and repair. It cannot store extra protein as protein. Instead, the liver breaks down the surplus amino\n    acids (deamination): the nitrogen part is removed and excreted as urea in urine, and the remaining carbon part\n    is either used for energy straight away or converted into glucose and then into fat, which is stored in the\n    adipose (fat) tissue under the skin and around the organs. If a person keeps eating more protein, and therefore\n    more calories, than they use, this fat store builds up and they gain weight, eventually becoming obese. Many\n    high-protein foods, such as fatty meats, cheese and fried chicken, are also high in fat, which adds even more\n    calories.\n\n(d) Explain TWO guidelines for conserving vitamin C during pre-preparation. (4)\n    Prepare fruits and vegetables just before cooking or serving, and do not soak them. Vitamin C dissolves in\n    water and is destroyed when exposed to air (oxidation). Cutting vegetables long in advance, or leaving them\n    soaking in water, allows the vitamin to leach out and be destroyed.\n    Use a sharp knife, cut into large pieces, and peel thinly (or not at all). Many vitamins lie just under the skin, so\n    thick peeling removes them. A blunt knife bruises the cells and releases enzymes that destroy vitamin C. Large\n    pieces expose less surface area to air and water, so less vitamin C is lost.","3":"(a) Two factors to consider when planning meals for the family. (2)\n    Nutritional needs of family members (age, sex, occupation, health).\n    Money available, and the time and skill available (also accept: likes and dislikes, religion, foods in season).\n\n(b) MAIN nutrient in each food group. (2)\n    (i) Food from animals: protein.\n    (ii) Staples: carbohydrate (starch).\n\n(c)(i) Define \"multi-mix principle\". (2)\n    The multi-mix principle is a method of planning meals by combining foods from several (usually four) of the\n    Caribbean food groups in one meal, so that the meal is nutritionally balanced. A two-mix uses staple + legume\n    or animal food; a three-mix adds vegetables; a four-mix includes staple, legume, animal food and vegetable or\n    fruit.\n\n(c)(ii) Use the multi-mix principle to plan a breakfast for a 5-year-old boy. (6)\n    Four-mix breakfast\n    Cornmeal porridge made with milk, sweetened lightly with condensed milk and nutmeg (staple + food from\n    animals)\n\n\n    Scrambled egg with grated cheese (food from animals)\n    Wholewheat toast with peanut butter (staple + legume/nut)\n    Slices of ripe banana or paw paw (fruit)\n    Glass of fresh orange juice\n    Why it suits a 5-year-old: small, soft and attractive portions; it provides energy for play and learning, protein for\n    growth, calcium for bones and teeth, and vitamin C. It is not spicy, and foods from at least four groups are\n    included.\n\n(c)(iii) Plan a lunch menu using the triple-mix (three-mix) principle. (4)\n    Rice (staple), stewed red beans (legume), and steamed callaloo with carrots (vegetable), served with a glass of\n    water or lime juice.\n    (Alternative: boiled green bananas and yam (staple), ackee and saltfish (food from animals), tossed salad\n    (vegetable).)","4":"(a) ONE agent responsible for the discolouration seen in cut fruits and vegetables. (1)\n    Enzymes (polyphenol oxidase), which react with oxygen in the air to cause enzymic browning.\n\n(b) Three methods of preserving food. (3)\n    Freezing\n    Drying (dehydration)\n    Pickling, or canning, jam-making, salting or smoking\n\n(c)(i) Two micro-organisms that can contaminate food. (2)\n    Bacteria (for example Salmonella)\n    Moulds (also accept yeasts or viruses)\n\n(c)(ii) FOUR factors, other than micro-organisms, that may have caused the fish dish to spoil. (4)\n    Enzymes in the fish itself kept breaking down the flesh (autolysis), making it soft.\n    The fish was not kept cold enough. It was left at room temperature or in a warm car, instead of being chilled\n     on ice or refrigerated.\n    The fish was stored too long before cooking, or was not fresh when bought.\n    The fish was not gutted and cleaned promptly, or the cooked dish was left uncovered and exposed to flies\n     (also accept: oxidation of the fish oils, causing rancidity; being thawed and refrozen).\n\n(c)(iii) Three signs or symptoms in people who eat the tainted fish. (3)\n    Nausea and vomiting\n    Diarrhoea and stomach cramps\n    Fever, headache, or (with some fish) tingling around the mouth, itching or flushing (scombroid poisoning)\n\n(d) Three effects of doubling the baking powder in a cake. (3)\n    The cake rises too quickly and then collapses (sinks) in the middle, because the structure cannot hold so much\n     gas before it sets.\n    It has a coarse, open, crumbly texture with large holes.\n    It has a bitter, soapy or metallic aftertaste, and may be darker or yellowish in colour from the excess alkali.","5":"(a)(i) Define \"work triangle\". (2)\n    The work triangle is the imaginary triangle formed by joining the three main work centres in a kitchen: the sink,\n    the stove (cooker) and the refrigerator. In a well-planned kitchen these are close together (the total of the\n    three sides is about 4–8 m) with no obstacles between them, which saves time and steps.\n\n(a)(ii) A kitchen layout that does NOT form a work triangle. (1)\n    The one-wall (single-line or straight) kitchen, where all three work centres sit in a single row.\n\n(a)(iii) Explain THREE factors to consider when planning the layout and design of your family's kitchen. (6)\n    Size and shape of the room, and the size of the family. The space available decides which layout is possible (L-\n    shape, U-shape, galley). A large family needs more counter space, storage and a bigger refrigerator, and room\n    for more than one person to work at a time.\n    The work triangle and the flow of work. The sink, stove and refrigerator should be placed close together in a\n    logical order (store, prepare, cook, serve), with no traffic passing through the triangle. This saves time and\n    energy and reduces accidents.\n    Safety, lighting and ventilation, and the budget. Plan for good natural and artificial lighting over work surfaces,\n    an extractor fan or window to remove heat, steam and smells, and safe positioning of the stove (away from\n    doors and windows with curtains). Electrical outlets should be away from the sink. Choose easy-to-clean,\n    durable surfaces. The cost of cabinets, appliances and plumbing changes must fit the money available.\n\n(b) Three features to consider when purchasing a food processor. (3)\n    Size and capacity of the bowl, to suit the amount of food the family usually prepares.\n    The range of attachments (slicing and grating discs, dough hook, whisk) and variable speeds or a pulse setting.\n    Safety features (interlocking lid, non-slip feet, overload cut-out), ease of cleaning (dishwasher-safe parts),\n     motor power, price, guarantee and after-sales service.\n\n(c) Explain TWO techniques Vishnu can use to conserve fuel while cooking curried chicken. (4)\n    Use a pressure cooker. The pressure cooker raises the boiling point of water to about 120°C, so the chicken\n    becomes tender in a fraction of the normal time (about 15 minutes instead of 45). Less cooking time means\n    much less gas is used.\n    Cover the pot, match the pan to the burner, and lower the flame once it boils. A tight-fitting lid traps heat and\n    steam, so the curry cooks faster. Using a pan that covers the burner means no heat is wasted around the sides.\n    Once the curry comes to the boil, turn the flame down to a simmer, which is all that is needed to keep it\n    cooking. (Also accept: marinate and cut the chicken into small, even pieces beforehand so it cooks quickly;\n    have all ingredients ready before lighting the stove.)","6":"(a) One guideline for (i) buying fresh beef and (ii) storing frozen beef. (2)\n    (i) Buy beef that is bright red with creamy-white fat, firm to the touch and with a fresh smell, from a clean,\n     refrigerated outlet.\n    (ii) Wrap it tightly in freezer bags or foil (airtight), label with the date, and store it at −18°C or below. Use\n     within the recommended time, and never refreeze it once thawed.\n\n(b)(i) One advantage and one disadvantage of boiling. (2)\n\n\n   Advantage: it is simple and safe, uses no fat, and makes tough foods tender and easy to digest.\n   Disadvantage: water-soluble vitamins (B and C) and minerals leach into the water and are lost, and the food\n    may become soggy or tasteless if overcooked.\n\n(b)(ii) Two dry methods of cooking. (2)\n   Baking (or roasting)\n   Grilling (also accept frying or toasting)\n\n(c)(i) FOUR likely reasons why Jason shops at Mabel's (a large chain). (4)\n   Lower prices and more special offers, because large chains buy in bulk.\n   A wider variety of brands and products, so he can do all his shopping in one place.\n   Better facilities: air conditioning, parking, longer opening hours, credit and debit card payments, loyalty cards.\n   Better quality control: proper refrigeration and stock rotation, plus clear return or refund policies.\n\n(c)(ii) Three courses of action Jason's friend could take to seek redress on the expired items. (3)\n   Return the expired items to Smart's Supermarket, with the receipt, and ask for a refund or replacement.\n   If the store refuses, write a formal complaint to the manager or owner.\n   Report the matter to the Consumer Affairs Division (or Bureau of Standards or public health inspector). As a\n    last resort, take it to the small claims court.\n\n(d) Three ways to make plain boiled rice more appealing. (3)\n   Add colour: stir in cooked green peas, diced carrots and sweet peppers, or a little turmeric (yellow rice).\n   Add flavour: cook in stock or coconut milk instead of water, with thyme, scallion, garlic or butter.\n   Garnish attractively: chopped parsley, a sprig of herbs, or mould the rice in a cup or ring mould before turning\n    it out onto the plate."},"2018":{"1":"(a) Three organizations involved in community nutrition in the Caribbean. (3)\n    CARPHA (Caribbean Public Health Agency), which absorbed the former CFNI (Caribbean Food and Nutrition\n     Institute).\n    PAHO/WHO (Pan American Health Organization).\n    FAO (Food and Agriculture Organization) or UNICEF; also accept national bodies such as the Ministry of Health\n     or the National Nutrition Council.\n\n(b) Explain TWO roles of community nutrition in the Caribbean. (4)\n    Educating the public about healthy eating. Community nutrition workers run campaigns, school programmes,\n    clinics and health fairs that teach people about balanced diets, the Caribbean food groups, breastfeeding and\n    reducing salt, sugar and fat. This helps prevent lifestyle diseases such as diabetes and hypertension, which are\n    very common in the region.\n    Identifying and helping vulnerable groups. They carry out nutrition surveys and monitor children's growth to\n    find who is malnourished (for example underweight children, anaemic pregnant women and the elderly poor).\n    They then provide feeding programmes, supplements (iron, folic acid, vitamin A) and food assistance, so\n    malnutrition is caught and treated early.\n\n(c) Discuss TWO factors that change people's dietary requirements, with TWO examples of each. (6)\n    Age and stage of life. Nutrient needs change as people grow and age. Example 1: infants and adolescents need\n    extra protein, calcium and energy for rapid growth (the teenage growth spurt). Example 2: elderly people need\n    less energy because they are less active and their metabolism slows, but still need plenty of calcium, vitamin D\n    and fibre to prevent osteoporosis and constipation.\n    Health status or physiological condition. Illness and special conditions change what the body needs. Example\n    1: a person with diabetes must limit sugar and refined carbohydrates and eat more fibre to control blood\n    glucose. Example 2: a pregnant woman needs more iron, folic acid, calcium and protein for the developing\n    baby.\n    (Also accept activity level or occupation: a labourer needs more energy than an office worker, and an athlete\n    needs more carbohydrate and protein. Also accept climate and gender.)\n\n(d)(i) Two high-intensity activities suitable for adolescents. (2)\n    Running or jogging (sprinting, track events).\n    Playing a fast sport such as football, basketball or netball (also accept: aerobics or dance classes, swimming\n     laps, skipping, cycling).\n\n(d)(ii) Minimum length of time to be effective. (1)\n    At least 60 minutes a day of moderate-to-vigorous activity for adolescents (WHO guideline). (Some textbooks\n    say a minimum of 30 minutes per session, at least 3 times a week.)","2":"(a)(i) Define \"deficiency disease\". (2)\n    A deficiency disease is an illness caused by a lack (shortage) of a particular nutrient in the diet over a long\n    period, for example scurvy from a lack of vitamin C, or anaemia from a lack of iron.\n\n\n(a)(ii) The deficiency disease from a lack of vitamin C. (1)\n    Scurvy.\n\n(a)(iii) Three signs of scurvy. (3)\n    Swollen, spongy gums that bleed easily, and loose teeth.\n    Wounds that heal slowly, and easy bruising (small red spots under the skin).\n    Tiredness, weakness, and aching joints and muscles.\n\n(b) Explain THREE reasons why water is important in the diet (water campaign). (6)\n    It regulates body temperature. When we are hot or exercising, the body releases water as sweat. As the sweat\n    evaporates from the skin it cools us down. Without enough water we can overheat, which is a serious risk in\n    the hot Caribbean climate.\n    It transports nutrients and removes waste. Water makes up most of the blood and carries nutrients and\n    oxygen to cells. It also carries waste products to the kidneys, which remove them in urine. Too little water\n    strains the kidneys and can lead to kidney stones.\n    It aids digestion and prevents constipation. Water forms saliva and digestive juices, helps dissolve nutrients so\n    they can be absorbed, and softens faeces so they pass easily. It also lubricates joints and protects organs.\n    Dehydration causes headaches, tiredness, poor concentration and constipation.\n\n(c) Describe the digestion of a piece of meat from the mouth to the stomach. (4)\n    In the mouth: the teeth cut, tear and grind the meat into smaller pieces (mechanical digestion), increasing its\n    surface area. Saliva moistens and softens it. Saliva contains the enzyme salivary amylase, but this acts on\n    starch, not protein, so little chemical digestion of meat happens here. The tongue rolls the food into a ball\n    (bolus) and pushes it to the back of the throat to be swallowed.\n    Down the oesophagus: the bolus travels down the gullet (oesophagus) by peristalsis, a wave of muscle\n    contractions, into the stomach.\n    In the stomach: the muscular stomach wall churns the meat with gastric juice. Hydrochloric acid kills bacteria\n    and provides the acid conditions needed by the enzyme pepsin, which begins breaking the meat's protein into\n    smaller chains (polypeptides). The meat becomes a creamy liquid called chyme. (Rennin also clots milk protein\n    in infants.)","3":"(a)(i) Define \"breakfast\". (2)\n    Breakfast is the first meal of the day, eaten in the morning. It \"breaks the fast\" of the night, refuelling the body\n    with energy and nutrients for the day's activities and helping concentration.\n\n(a)(ii) Two indigenous Caribbean foods for a breakfast menu. (2)\n    Cassava (for example cassava bread or bammy).\n    Breadfruit or green bananas, plantain, ackee, callaloo, sweet potato or yam (accept any local food).\n\n(b) The difference between a vegan and a lacto-vegetarian. (2)\n    A vegan eats only plant foods: no meat, fish, eggs, milk or any other animal product. A lacto-vegetarian also\n    avoids meat, fish and eggs, but does eat milk and dairy products such as cheese, butter and yogurt.\n\n(c) Using indigenous foods, plan a ONE-course menu for a manual worker. (4)\n    Stewed red peas with pig tail or salted beef (or a vegetarian version)\n\n\n    Boiled yam, sweet potato, green bananas and flour dumplings\n    Steamed callaloo with tomatoes\n    Ripe plantain (baked)\n    Carrot juice or a soursop drink\n    Why: a manual worker uses a lot of energy, so the meal is rich in starchy ground provisions and legumes for\n    energy and protein, with vegetables for vitamins and minerals, all from local foods.\n\n(d) Two reasons why each item is suitable or unsuitable for your aunt (hypertension and diabetes). (4)\n    Corned beef pie (pastry): unsuitable. (1) Corned beef is a processed meat that is very high in salt (sodium),\n     which raises blood pressure. (2) The pastry is made from refined white flour and is high in saturated fat, so it\n     raises blood sugar quickly, adds calories (weight gain) and increases the risk of heart disease.\n    Whole-wheat bread: suitable. (1) It is high in fibre, which slows the absorption of sugar, helping to control\n     her blood glucose levels. (2) Fibre also helps lower cholesterol and keeps her full, helping weight control, and\n     whole wheat supplies B vitamins and minerals. It should still be eaten in sensible portions and checked for\n     added salt.\n\n(e) One packaging material each for fried chicken and cupcakes (different materials). (2)\n    Fried chicken: aluminium foil, or a foil-lined paper box (it keeps the chicken warm and absorbs grease).\n    Cupcakes: a rigid plastic container with a lid (or a cardboard cake box), which protects the icing from being\n     squashed.","4":"(a)(i) Two food additives. (2)\n    Preservatives (for example sodium benzoate).\n    Colourings (also accept: flavourings such as MSG, emulsifiers, sweeteners, antioxidants).\n\n(a)(ii) Two functions of food additives. (2)\n    Preserve food and extend its shelf life by preventing spoilage.\n    Improve the colour, flavour or texture of food (also accept: improve nutritional value, for example by\n     fortification).\n\n(b) One difference between perishable and non-perishable foods. (2)\n    Perishable foods (fish, milk, meat) spoil quickly and must be refrigerated. Non-perishable foods (rice, canned\n    goods, flour) can be stored at room temperature for a long time without spoiling.\n\n(c)(i) Explain TWO methods of heat transfer, other than conduction, used to cook boiled rice, steamed fish and\ngrilled chicken. (4)\n    Convection (boiled rice and steamed fish). When the water is heated, the warm water at the bottom rises and\n    the cooler water sinks, setting up convection currents that carry heat evenly around the rice. In steaming, hot\n    steam rises and circulates around the fish, transferring heat to it.\n    Radiation (grilled chicken). Heat rays (infrared radiation) travel in straight lines from the red-hot grill element\n    or flame directly onto the surface of the chicken, without heating the air in between. This browns and cooks\n    the surface.\n\n(c)(ii) Two changes in the rice and two changes in the fish during cooking. (4)\n\n\n    Rice: (1) the starch gelatinizes: the grains absorb water, swell and become soft and tender (they roughly\n     double or triple in size). (2) The grains become translucent and slightly sticky, and some starch and B vitamins\n     pass into the cooking water.\n    Fish: (1) the protein coagulates, so the flesh changes from translucent to opaque (white) and becomes firm.\n     (2) The connective tissue (collagen) turns into gelatin, so the flakes separate easily. The fish also shrinks\n     slightly and loses some juices.\n\n(c)(iii) Two effects of prolonged heating on the frying oil. (2)\n    The oil breaks down and darkens, smokes at a lower temperature, and may reach its flash point and catch\n     fire.\n    It develops an unpleasant flavour and smell (it goes rancid, and harmful substances such as acrolein form),\n     and it becomes thicker and stickier, so food absorbs more oil and becomes greasy.","5":"(a) Four procedures to remove a blockage from a kitchen sink. (4)\n    Remove any visible food debris from the drain opening (wear gloves).\n    Pour boiling water, or a mixture of baking soda followed by vinegar, down the drain to break down grease.\n    Use a plunger to force out the blockage (cover the overflow first).\n    Unscrew and clean the U-bend (trap) over a bucket, or use a plumber's snake or wire, or a commercial drain\n     cleaner, following the instructions.\n\n(b)(i) Define \"first aid\". (2)\n    First aid is the immediate help given to an injured or suddenly ill person, before professional medical help\n    arrives, in order to preserve life, stop the condition getting worse and help recovery.\n\n(b)(ii) Explain THREE first-aid procedures for a person who has been electrocuted, while waiting for medical\nhelp. (6)\n    Make the area safe: break the electrical contact without touching the person. Switch off the power at the\n    main switch or pull out the plug. If you cannot, stand on something dry and insulating (a rubber mat or a pile of\n    newspapers) and push the person away from the source with a dry wooden broom handle or chair. Touching\n    them while they are still in contact with the current would electrocute you too.\n    Check breathing and pulse, and give CPR if needed. Call emergency services. If the person is not breathing\n    normally, begin CPR: 30 chest compressions followed by 2 rescue breaths, and continue until help arrives or\n    they start breathing.\n    Place in the recovery position, treat burns and treat for shock. If the person is breathing but unconscious, put\n    them in the recovery position so the airway stays open. Cool any burns at the entry and exit points with cool\n    water and cover them with a clean, dry dressing. Keep the person warm and still, loosen tight clothing, and do\n    not give food or drink.\n\n(c) Brunch for 20 family members. Explain TWO factors for the MOST efficient use of the kitchen. (4)\n    Plan and organize the work (time plan and dovetailing). Plan the menu and prepare a time plan that orders\n    the tasks so several jobs happen at once, for example baking muffins while the eggs and saltfish are being\n    prepared. Do as much preparation as possible the night before (chopping, marinating, setting the table). This\n    avoids rushing and crowding the kitchen on the day.\n    Arrange the work space and equipment (work triangle and labour-saving devices). Keep the sink, stove and\n    refrigerator clear and close together, and assemble all ingredients and utensils before starting. Use the oven\n\n\n    for several items at once and use labour-saving equipment (food processor, large pots, a rice cooker) to handle\n    large quantities. If family members help, assign each person a separate area and task, and clean as you go.","6":"(a) The difference between a need and a want. (2)\n    A need is something essential for survival and wellbeing, such as food, water, shelter and clothing. A want is\n    something we would like to have but can live without, such as a luxury item or designer brand.\n\n(b) Two reasons for cooking food. (2)\n    To kill harmful bacteria and make food safe to eat.\n    To make food easier to digest, and to improve its flavour, texture, colour and appearance (also accept: to add\n     variety, to preserve).\n\n(c) A cut of beef that is (i) tough and (ii) tender. (2)\n    (i) Tough: shin (also accept neck, brisket, flank, chuck).\n    (ii) Tender: fillet (also accept sirloin, rump, T-bone).\n\n(d)(i) Design a label for a food product with TWO nutritional facts and TWO pieces of general information. (4)\n    SUNSHINE PEANUT BUTTER (Smooth)\n    General information: Net Wt: 340 g · Ingredients: roasted peanuts, sugar, salt, vegetable oil · Best before:\n    12/2027 · Made by Sunshine Foods Ltd, Kingston, Jamaica\n    Nutrition facts (per 2 tbsp / 32 g serving): Energy: 190 kcal · Protein: 7 g · Total fat: 16 g · Sodium: 140 mg\n\n(d)(ii) Two ways the customer benefits from the barcode/UPC at checkout. (2)\n    Faster checkout and shorter queues, because items are scanned quickly.\n    Accurate pricing, with fewer cashier errors. The receipt is itemized, so the customer can check exactly what\n     they paid for each item.\n\n(e) Discuss TWO personal hygiene practices during food preparation that could affect the quality of the food. (4)\n    Washing hands thoroughly. Hands carry bacteria from the toilet, raw meat, money, phones and pets. If the\n    cook does not wash their hands with soap and warm water before and during food preparation, these bacteria\n    transfer to the food, causing food poisoning and making the food spoil faster.\n    Covering the hair, and not coughing, sneezing or touching the face over food. Loose hair can fall into food,\n    making it unappetizing and spreading bacteria. Coughing or sneezing sprays droplets containing germs (such as\n    Staphylococcus) onto the food. Tying back and covering the hair, wearing a clean apron, and covering cuts with\n    a waterproof plaster all keep food safe and of good quality."},"2019":{"1":"(a)(i) Define \"nutritional status\". (2)\n    Nutritional status is the condition of a person's health as it is affected by the intake and use of nutrients. It\n    shows whether the person is well nourished, undernourished or overnourished.\n\n(a)(ii) The FOUR types of assessment methods used to evaluate nutritional status. (4)\n    These are often remembered as the \"ABCD\" methods:\n    A – Anthropometric: body measurements such as height, weight, BMI, waist circumference and skinfold\n     thickness.\n    B – Biochemical: laboratory tests of blood or urine, such as haemoglobin for anaemia or blood glucose.\n    C – Clinical: a physical examination for visible signs such as pale eyelids, swollen ankles, poor hair or skin, or\n     goitre.\n    D – Dietary: finding out what a person eats, using 24-hour recall, food diaries or food frequency\n     questionnaires.\n\n(b) THREE reasons why effective management of diabetes mellitus is costly. (6)\n    Medication and testing supplies. Many diabetics need daily medication such as tablets (metformin) or insulin\n    injections for life, plus glucometers, test strips, lancets and needles to check their blood sugar several times a\n    day. These must be bought continuously.\n    Special diet and regular medical check-ups. A diabetic diet relies on fresh vegetables, wholegrains, lean\n    protein and sugar-free products, which are often more expensive than cheap refined starches and sugary\n    foods. The patient also needs regular visits to doctors, dietitians, eye specialists and foot clinics, and laboratory\n    tests such as HbA1c.\n    Treating complications and lost income. Poorly controlled diabetes leads to complications such as kidney\n    failure (needing dialysis), blindness, heart disease, nerve damage and foot ulcers that may lead to amputation.\n    These need costly hospital care, and the patient may lose working days or be unable to work, reducing family\n    income. The government also bears large costs through public health services.\n\n(c) TWO reasons pregnant women need more iron. (4)\n    The mother's blood volume increases by about 50% during pregnancy. Extra iron is needed to make the\n    additional haemoglobin that carries oxygen to her own body and to the placenta. Without enough iron she\n    becomes anaemic, tired and breathless, and is more at risk during childbirth because of blood loss.\n    The baby must build up its own iron store. The growing baby takes iron from the mother to make its blood\n    and to store in its liver, enough to last the first 4–6 months after birth, since breast milk is low in iron. Low iron\n    in pregnancy is linked with premature birth and low birth weight.","2":"(a) Two functions of food. (2)\n    Provides energy for activity and to keep the body warm.\n    Provides materials for growth and repair of body tissues (also accept: protection from disease, regulating\n     body processes, social function).\n\n(b) Discuss TWO effects of inadequate carbohydrate on Samantha's health. (6)\n\n\n    Low energy, tiredness and poor concentration. Carbohydrates are the body's main source of energy, and\n    glucose is the brain's preferred fuel. Without enough carbohydrate Samantha's blood sugar may fall, making\n    her feel weak, tired, dizzy, irritable and unable to concentrate. She may also find it hard to exercise, which\n    works against her weight-loss goal.\n    Protein is used for energy, and ketosis develops. When carbohydrate is short, the body breaks down fat and\n    protein (including muscle) for energy instead of using protein for growth and repair. This wastes muscle tissue.\n    Burning fat without carbohydrate also produces ketones, which can cause bad breath, headaches and nausea\n    (ketosis). In addition, cutting out foods such as wholegrains, fruit and ground provisions reduces her fibre\n    intake, which can lead to constipation and loss of B vitamins.\n\n(c) TWO ways calcium is important to (i) adolescent females and (ii) babies. (4)\n    (i) Adolescent females: (1) builds peak bone mass during the growth spurt, giving strong bones and helping\n     prevent osteoporosis later in life (women are at higher risk). (2) Needed for muscle contraction (including the\n     heart), nerve function, and normal blood clotting during menstruation.\n    (ii) Babies: (1) forms and hardens the rapidly growing bones and prevents rickets. (2) Needed for the\n     development of teeth, which are forming in the gums, and for healthy heart, muscle and nerve function.\n\n(d) Two functions of water in the body and two sources of water in the diet. (4)\n    Functions: (1) regulates body temperature through sweating. (2) Carries nutrients and oxygen around the\n     body and removes waste in urine (also accept: aids digestion, prevents constipation, lubricates joints).\n    Sources: (1) drinking water and other beverages (juices, milk, tea). (2) Foods with a high water content, such\n     as watermelon, cucumber, soups and lettuce.","3":"(a)(i) Define \"brunch\". (2)\n    Brunch is a meal that combines breakfast and lunch, eaten in the late morning (around 10 a.m. to 1 p.m.),\n    usually at weekends or on holidays. It includes both breakfast foods and lunch dishes.\n\n(a)(ii) Two differences between table d'hôte and à la carte. (4)\n    Price: table d'hôte has one fixed price for the whole set meal, while à la carte prices every dish separately, so\n    the bill depends on what you choose.\n    Choice and timing: table d'hôte offers a limited choice (a set number of courses with few options) and is\n    usually served at set times. À la carte offers a wide choice of dishes, which are cooked to order, so there may\n    be a longer wait.\n\n(b)(i) Plan a brunch menu to raise funds for the graduation ball. (4)\n    Graduation Fund-raising Brunch\n    Fresh fruit punch / Mint lemonade / Coffee or tea\n    Fresh fruit platter (pineapple, paw paw, watermelon)\n    Ackee and saltfish (or scrambled eggs with sweet peppers)\n    Baked chicken wings / Callaloo quiche\n    Fried dumplings, roast breadfruit and wholewheat bread rolls\n    Tossed garden salad\n    Banana bread and coconut drops\n\n\n(b)(ii) The class are mainly athletes. Complete the table with THREE factors (with explanation) to consider when\nplanning the menu. (6)\n    Factor 1: provide enough complex carbohydrates (wholegrains, ground provisions, breadfruit). Explanation:\n     athletes burn a lot of energy. Complex carbohydrates release energy slowly and refill the glycogen stores in\n     their muscles, giving them stamina.\n    Factor 2: include lean protein foods (eggs, fish, chicken, legumes). Explanation: protein is needed to repair\n     and build muscle tissue damaged during training, and to help recovery.\n    Factor 3: plenty of fluids, and fresh fruit and vegetables; low in salt, sugar and fried foods. Explanation:\n     athletes lose water and minerals through sweat, so drinks and water-rich fruits prevent dehydration. Fruit\n     and vegetables supply vitamins, minerals and antioxidants that help immunity, and limiting fried and sugary\n     foods supports the healthy lifestyle they follow.","4":"(a) Two perishable and two non-perishable foods. (4)\n    Perishable: fresh fish, and milk (also accept meat, eggs, soft fruits).\n    Non-perishable: rice, and canned corned beef (also accept dried peas, flour, sugar).\n\n(b) Define \"food infection\". (2)\n    A food infection is an illness caused by eating food containing large numbers of live harmful micro-organisms\n    (such as Salmonella), which then multiply inside the person's intestine and cause symptoms such as fever,\n    diarrhoea, vomiting and stomach pain. (This differs from food intoxication, which is caused by toxins the\n    bacteria have already produced in the food.)\n\n(c) One reason for each step in cheese production. (4)\n    Pasteurization of milk: heats the milk (about 72°C for 15 seconds) to kill harmful bacteria such as Listeria and\n     TB germs, so the cheese is safe to eat.\n    Addition of bacterial culture (starter): the bacteria change lactose into lactic acid, which sours the milk, helps\n     it curdle, and develops the cheese's flavour and texture.\n    Addition of rennet: rennet contains the enzyme rennin (chymosin), which coagulates the milk protein\n     (casein) into a firm curd that separates from the liquid whey.\n    Addition of salt to the curd: salt adds flavour, draws out more whey, and acts as a preservative by slowing\n     the growth of unwanted micro-organisms.\n\n(d) Two guidelines for each factor to prevent food contamination. (6)\n    (i) Temperature: (1) keep cold foods below 5°C and hot foods above 63°C, and avoid the \"danger zone\" (5–\n     63°C). (2) Cook food thoroughly (the centre of chicken should reach 75°C), and cool leftovers quickly and\n     refrigerate them within 2 hours.\n    (ii) Personal hygiene: (1) wash your hands with soap and water before handling food and after using the\n     toilet. (2) Tie back and cover your hair, wear a clean apron, keep nails short, and cover cuts with a waterproof\n     plaster.\n    (iii) Kitchen/food hygiene: (1) clean and sanitize work surfaces, boards and utensils, and use separate boards\n     for raw meat and cooked food. (2) Keep the kitchen free of pests, cover food, and dispose of waste in covered\n     bins emptied regularly.","5":"(a) Three steps to remove a blockage from a clogged sink. (3)\n    Remove standing water and any visible debris from the drain opening. Pour boiling water (or a mixture of\n     baking soda and vinegar) down the drain to dissolve grease.\n    Use a plunger: cover the overflow hole and pump firmly up and down to dislodge the blockage.\n    If the sink is still blocked, place a bucket under the U-bend (trap), unscrew it, clean out the debris, then refit it\n     and flush with hot water. Use a plumber's snake, or call a plumber, if necessary.\n\n(b) Three steps in first aid for Kazim's minor cut. (3)\n    Rinse the cut under clean running water to remove dirt.\n    Apply gentle pressure with a clean cloth or gauze until the bleeding stops, keeping the finger raised.\n    Apply an antiseptic and cover with a sterile plaster. A waterproof plaster is best if he returns to food\n     preparation.\n\n(c) One precaution for each activity. (6)\n    (i) Lighting the gas oven with matches: strike the match before turning on the gas, and open the oven door\n     first, so gas does not build up and explode. Keep your face away from the burner.\n    (ii) Slicing or cutting root vegetables: cut on a chopping board placed on a damp cloth so it does not slip. Use a\n     sharp knife, cut away from your body, and curl your fingers under (the \"claw\" grip).\n    (iii) Frying fish: dry the fish well before putting it into hot oil, so the oil does not spit, and lower it in gently,\n     away from you. Never leave the pan unattended, turn pan handles inward, and keep a lid or fire blanket\n     nearby in case of fire.\n\n(d) Two ways Ramona can achieve maximum efficiency when preparing meals. (4)\n    Plan ahead with a time plan, and dovetail tasks. Before starting, Ramona should write a work plan listing every\n    dish, the order of tasks and how long each takes. She can start the longest jobs first (marinating, stews, baking)\n    and do quick tasks such as chopping and salads while those cook. She can also do pre-preparation the day\n    before.\n    Organize the workspace and use labour-saving equipment. Collect all ingredients and tools before starting\n    (mise en place), keep the work area tidy, and clean as she goes. Using equipment such as a food processor,\n    pressure cooker or electric mixer cuts preparation and cooking times dramatically.","6":"(a) Define \"green consumer\". (2)\n    A green consumer is a shopper who is concerned about the environment and chooses products that cause the\n    least harm, such as goods with little or recyclable packaging, organic or local produce, and energy-saving\n    appliances. They also try to reduce, reuse and recycle.\n\n(b) Four characteristics of fresh fish. (4)\n    Eyes are bright, clear and bulging, not sunken or cloudy.\n    Gills are bright red or pink, not grey or brown.\n    Flesh is firm and springs back when pressed.\n    It has a fresh, mild sea smell, not a strong \"fishy\" or ammonia smell, and its scales are shiny and tightly\n     attached.\n\n\n(c)(i) Outline the preparation and service of a one-pot dish using THREE of the foods listed. (6)\n  Dish: chicken pelau (rice, chicken, vegetables)\n  Preparation:\n  1. Wash and cut 1 kg of chicken into pieces. Season with salt, black pepper, garlic, thyme, scallion and a little\n  green seasoning, and leave to marinate.\n  2. Heat 2 tablespoons of oil in a heavy pot, add 2 tablespoons of brown sugar and cook until it caramelizes to a\n  dark brown. Add the chicken and stir to coat and brown it.\n  3. Add 2 cups of washed rice, 1 cup of cooked pigeon peas, and diced carrots and sweet peppers. Stir well.\n  4. Add about 4 cups of water or stock and coconut milk, with a whole hot pepper for flavour. Bring to the boil,\n  then cover and simmer gently for about 25 minutes, until the rice is tender and the liquid is absorbed. Remove\n  the hot pepper.\n  Service: serve hot in a warmed dish, garnished with chopped parsley or chives, with a side of coleslaw or\n  tossed salad and slices of tomato and cucumber.\n\n(c)(ii) Explain TWO scientific principles used in steaming. (4)\n  Conduction and boiling: heat from the burner passes by conduction through the base of the pan to the water.\n  The water boils and turns into steam (water vapour) at 100°C.\n  Convection and condensation (latent heat): the hot steam rises and circulates around the food by convection.\n  When steam touches the cooler food it condenses back into water and gives up a large amount of latent heat,\n  which cooks the food. Since the food does not sit in the water, fewer water-soluble vitamins are lost and the\n  food stays moist and light."},"2021":{"1":"(a)(i) Define \"balanced diet\". (2)\n    A balanced diet is one that provides all the nutrients (carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, water\n    and fibre) in the right amounts and proportions to meet a person's needs for health, growth and activity. It\n    usually includes foods from all six Caribbean food groups each day.\n\n(a)(ii) Define \"dietary guidelines\". (2)\n    Dietary guidelines are simple, practical recommendations produced by health authorities (for example the\n    Ministry of Health or CARPHA) telling people what and how much to eat to stay healthy and reduce the risk of\n    diet-related diseases. Example: \"Eat a variety of foods from all the food groups every day.\"\n\n(a)(iii) Define \"nutritional status\". (2)\n    Nutritional status is the state of a person's health as it is affected by the food they eat and how well the body\n    uses the nutrients. It can be good (well nourished) or poor (undernourished or overnourished), and is\n    measured by things like weight-for-height, BMI and blood tests.\n\n(b) Explain TWO ways community nutrition can be used to address malnutrition. (4)\n    Nutrition education programmes. Community nutritionists and health centres run classes, health fairs and\n    media campaigns that teach families how to plan balanced meals on a budget, breastfeed, and prepare local\n    foods healthily. Better knowledge leads to better food choices, reducing both undernutrition and obesity.\n    Feeding and supplementation programmes. Schemes such as school feeding programmes, food banks, food\n    stamps and free vitamin or iron supplements at antenatal and child clinics give vulnerable groups (children,\n    pregnant women, the poor and the elderly) direct access to nutritious food. They also monitor children's\n    growth so problems are caught early. (Also accept: community gardens, which increase the supply of fresh\n    produce.)\n\n(c) Using a specific example for each, explain TWO dietary guidelines Caribbean people can use for a healthy\nlifestyle. (6)\n    Eat a variety of foods from the six Caribbean food groups every day. No single food has all the nutrients, so\n    choosing from staples, legumes, foods from animals, fruits, vegetables, and fats and oils helps make sure the\n    body gets everything it needs. Example: a lunch of rice and peas (staples and legumes), stewed chicken (foods\n    from animals), steamed callaloo and carrots (vegetables) and a slice of paw paw (fruit).\n    Reduce salt, sugar and fat. Too much of these increases the risk of hypertension, diabetes, obesity and heart\n    disease, which are common in the Caribbean. Example: season food with herbs such as thyme, garlic and\n    scallion instead of salt and stock cubes, choose water instead of sweet drinks, and bake or steam fish instead of\n    frying it.\n    (Also accept: eat more fruits and vegetables daily; be physically active; drink plenty of water.)","2":"(a)(i) Two sources of protein. (2)\n    Animal: meat, fish, eggs or milk.\n    Plant: peas and beans (legumes), nuts or soya.\n\n\n(a)(ii) Two functions of protein. (2)\n    Growth and repair of body cells and tissues.\n    Making enzymes, hormones and antibodies (also accept: supplying energy when carbohydrates and fats are\n     short).\n\n(a)(iii) Two elements found in protein. (2)\n    Nitrogen\n    Carbon (also accept hydrogen, oxygen, sulphur or phosphorus).\n\n(b) Explain TWO ways Susan can conserve vitamin C during pre-preparation and preparation of steamed\nvegetables. (4)\n    Pre-preparation: prepare the vegetables just before cooking, and do not soak them. Wash them whole, then\n    cut them into fairly large pieces with a sharp knife, immediately before steaming. Vitamin C dissolves in water\n    and is destroyed by air (oxidation), so leaving cut vegetables standing or soaking in water causes it to be lost.\n    Preparation: steam for the shortest time and serve at once. Put the vegetables into a steamer only after the\n    water is already boiling, cover with a tight-fitting lid, and cook until just tender-crisp. Do not add bicarbonate of\n    soda. Serve immediately, without keeping them hot for a long time. Vitamin C is destroyed by heat, long\n    cooking and alkali.\n\n(c)(i) Three signs or symptoms that made the doctor recommend Peter drink more water. (3)\n    Dark yellow urine, or passing very little urine.\n    Constipation.\n    Dry mouth, lips and skin, with headaches, dizziness, tiredness or confusion.\n\n(c)(ii) Three ways Peter can increase his mineral intake other than supplements. (3)\n    Eat more dark-green leafy vegetables (callaloo, spinach, pak choi) for iron, calcium and magnesium.\n    Include milk, cheese, yogurt and canned fish with bones (sardines) for calcium.\n    Eat more legumes, nuts, wholegrain cereals, lean meat and liver, and fresh fruit such as bananas for iron, zinc\n     and potassium (also accept: use iodized salt sparingly).","3":"(a)(i) Define table d'hôte. (2)\n    Table d'hôte is a set menu with a fixed number of courses and a limited choice of dishes, served at a set time\n    for a fixed total price.\n\n(a)(ii) Define main course. (2)\n    The main course is the most important and substantial course of a meal. It is usually built around a protein dish\n    (meat, fish, poultry or legumes) served with starchy foods and vegetables.\n\n(a)(iii) Define high tea. (2)\n    High tea is a substantial early-evening meal (around 5–6 p.m.) that combines afternoon tea and supper. It\n    includes a hot or cold savoury dish (such as fish cakes, meat pies or salads), bread and butter, cakes and\n    pastries, served with tea.\n\n(b) ONE benefit of each nutrient in an athlete's diet. (6)\n\n\n    (i) Proteins: athletes put great stress on their muscles in training. Protein repairs damaged muscle fibres and\n    builds new muscle tissue, which increases strength and helps recovery after exercise.\n    (ii) Carbohydrates: carbohydrates are the body's main and quickest source of energy. They are stored as\n    glycogen in the muscles and liver and used during exercise, which gives the athlete stamina and delays\n    tiredness.\n    (iii) Minerals: athletes lose minerals such as sodium and potassium in sweat, and these are needed for muscle\n    contraction and fluid balance (preventing cramps). Iron is needed to make haemoglobin, which carries oxygen\n    to working muscles, and calcium keeps bones strong.\n\n(c) Discuss the importance of vitamin B-12 in the diet of a vegan. (4)\n    Vitamin B12 is needed to make healthy red blood cells and to keep the nervous system (nerves and brain)\n    working properly. It is found almost only in animal foods such as meat, fish, eggs and milk, so a vegan, who eats\n    no animal products, is at high risk of deficiency. A lack of B12 causes pernicious (megaloblastic) anaemia, with\n    tiredness and weakness, and can lead to permanent nerve damage (numbness, tingling, memory problems).\n    Vegans should therefore eat B12-fortified foods (such as fortified soya milk, breakfast cereals and yeast\n    extract) or take a B12 supplement.","4":"(a) Four factors that contribute to food spoilage. (4)\n    Micro-organisms (bacteria, yeasts and moulds)\n    Enzymes in the food\n    Warm temperatures and moisture\n    Air (oxidation) and pests, or poor storage\n\n(b) Define \"food-borne illness\". (2)\n    A food-borne illness (food poisoning) is any sickness caused by eating food or drinking water contaminated\n    with harmful micro-organisms (such as Salmonella or E. coli), their toxins, or chemicals. Common symptoms\n    include vomiting, diarrhoea, stomach cramps and fever.\n\n(c) Explain how mould contaminates food. (4)\n    Moulds are tiny fungi that reproduce by releasing millions of spores into the air. When the spores land on food\n    that is moist, warm and slightly acidic or sugary (such as bread, fruit, cheese and jam), they germinate and\n    grow thread-like hyphae that spread through the food. You can see the mould as fuzzy green, white, blue or\n    black patches on the surface. The mould feeds on the food and breaks it down, giving a musty smell, a slimy\n    texture and off flavours. Some moulds also produce poisonous substances called mycotoxins (for example\n    aflatoxin on peanuts), which can make people ill.\n\n(d) Explain the importance of THREE steps when preparing green beans for freezing. (6)\n    Choose and clean: select young, fresh, firm beans, then wash them, top and tail them and cut them to size.\n    Only good-quality produce freezes well; freezing does not improve poor beans. Washing removes soil and\n    micro-organisms.\n    Blanch: plunge the beans into boiling water for about 2–3 minutes. Blanching destroys the enzymes that would\n    otherwise keep working in the freezer and cause loss of colour, flavour, texture and vitamins. It also kills\n    surface bacteria and keeps the beans bright green.\n\n\n  Cool quickly, drain, pack and label: plunge the beans straight into iced water to stop the cooking, then drain\n  and dry them well. Pack them in airtight freezer bags with the air squeezed out, label with the name and date,\n  and freeze quickly at −18°C. Rapid cooling keeps them crisp, removing water and air prevents ice crystals and\n  freezer burn, and labelling ensures they are used in date order.","5":"(a) Define \"first aid\". (2)\n  First aid is the immediate, temporary help given to a person who has been injured or has suddenly become ill,\n  before professional medical help arrives. It aims to preserve life, prevent the condition getting worse, and help\n  recovery.\n\n(b)(i) First aid for burns. (2)\n  Cool the burn immediately under cool (not ice-cold) running water for at least 10–20 minutes. Remove rings or\n  tight clothing near the area, unless they are stuck to the skin. Cover loosely with a clean, non-fluffy dressing or\n  cling film. Do not apply butter or ointments and do not burst blisters. Seek medical help for large or deep\n  burns.\n\n(b)(ii) First aid for heavy bleeding. (2)\n  Apply firm, direct pressure to the wound with a clean pad or cloth. Raise the injured part above the level of the\n  heart if possible. Keep the pressure on and add more pads on top if blood soaks through (do not remove the\n  first one). Bandage firmly in place, keep the person lying down and warm, and call for emergency help.\n\n(c) Discuss THREE ways energy can be conserved when using the refrigerator. (6)\n  Keep the door closed as much as possible. Decide what you need before opening the door and close it quickly.\n  Every time the door is open, cold air escapes and warm air enters, so the motor must work harder and use\n  more electricity to cool the fridge again.\n  Let hot food cool before putting it in, and do not overload the fridge. Hot food raises the temperature inside,\n  making the motor run longer. Overpacking blocks the circulation of cold air, so the fridge cannot cool\n  efficiently. Leave space between items.\n  Maintain and position the fridge correctly. Defrost regularly, since thick ice insulates the coils and wastes\n  energy (or use frost-free models). Keep the door seal clean and tight. Place the fridge away from the stove,\n  direct sunlight and walls, so air can circulate around the coils at the back. Set it at the correct temperature\n  (about 3–5°C), not colder than needed.\n\n(d) Which kitchen layout, A (one-wall) or B (L-shaped), suits the Brown family best? Explain. (4)\n  Layout B (the L-shaped kitchen) is most suitable. In an L-shaped kitchen the sink, stove and refrigerator form a\n  compact work triangle, which saves steps when Mrs Brown is baking for long periods. The two walls give much\n  more continuous counter space for rolling dough, mixing and cooling large batches of baked goods. The open\n  layout lets several family members work at the same time without getting in each other's way, and there is\n  room for a table in the open corner. Layout A (one wall) has all the work in a single line, with little counter\n  space and room for only one person, so it would be crowded for a large family running a baking business.","6":"(a)(i) Define \"enriched\". (1)\n\n\n    Enriched means nutrients lost during processing have been added back to the food, usually to about their\n    original level (for example, B vitamins and iron added back to white flour).\n\n(a)(ii) Define \"fortification\". (1)\n    Fortification is adding nutrients to a food that were not originally present in it, or were present only in small\n    amounts, to improve its nutritional value (for example, adding iodine to salt or vitamins A and D to margarine).\n\n(b) One example each of an enriched and a fortified food. (2)\n    Enriched: enriched white flour or white rice.\n    Fortified: iodized salt, fortified breakfast cereal, or vitamin A and D margarine.\n\n(c) Two advantages of the UPC in supermarkets. (2)\n    It speeds up checkout and reduces cashier errors, because prices are scanned automatically.\n    It helps with stock control: the computer keeps track of what has been sold, so the store knows when to\n     reorder.\n\n(d)(i) Which product, A or B, is most suitable for a diabetic? (1)\n    Product B.\n\n(d)(ii) Two reasons supporting that answer. (4)\n    Lower sugar. Product B has only 7 g of total sugar and 4 g of added sugar per serving (8% DV), compared with\n    12 g of sugar and 10 g of added sugar (20% DV) in Product A. Less sugar helps a diabetic keep their blood\n    glucose level under control.\n    Higher fibre and protein. Product B supplies 6 g of fibre (21% DV) and 9 g of protein, compared with 4 g of fibre\n    and 3 g of protein in A. Fibre and protein slow down the digestion and absorption of carbohydrate, so blood\n    sugar rises more slowly and steadily.\n\n(d)(iii) A group for whom Product A would be suitable. (1)\n    People with iron-deficiency anaemia (or pregnant women), because Product A is very high in iron (8 mg, 45%\n    DV) and also high in calcium.\n\n(e) One food in which each herb or spice is used. (4)\n    Ginger: ginger beer (or gingerbread, stir-fry).\n    Cloves: baked ham (or sorrel drink, black cake).\n    Cinnamon: cornmeal porridge (or sweet potato pudding).\n    Thyme: stewed chicken (or soups, rice and peas)."},"2022":{"1":"(a)(i) Define \"lifestyle disease\". (2)\n    A lifestyle disease is a non-communicable disease (one you cannot catch from another person) that is caused or\n    made worse by the way a person lives: an unhealthy diet, lack of exercise, smoking, too much alcohol and\n    stress.\n\n(a)(ii) Two lifestyle diseases affecting Caribbean people. (2)\n    Diabetes (type 2)\n    Hypertension (high blood pressure); also accept obesity, heart disease, stroke or some cancers.\n\n(b)(i) Two factors, other than activity level, pregnancy and lactation, that influence eating patterns in the\nCaribbean. (2)\n    Religion and culture or traditions (for example, Hindus do not eat beef).\n    Income or the cost of food (also accept: advertising and the media, peer pressure, availability of foods in\n     season, health conditions, age).\n\n(b)(ii) Explain how each factor changes dietary needs: level of activity, and pregnancy and lactation. (4)\n    Level of activity: the more active a person is, the more energy (calories) the body uses, so they need more\n    energy-giving foods (carbohydrates and some fat) and fluids. A labourer or athlete needs much more than an\n    office worker. Sedentary people need fewer calories, or they will gain weight.\n    Pregnancy and lactation: the mother must nourish the growing baby and later produce breast milk, so she\n    needs more energy, protein (to build the baby's tissues), iron and folic acid (for extra blood and to prevent\n    anaemia and birth defects), calcium (for the baby's bones and teeth) and fluids (especially when\n    breastfeeding).\n\n(c) Discuss TWO factors that may threaten the food security of elderly persons in a community. (6)\n    Low income. Many elderly people are retired and live on a small pension or none. They may not be able to\n    afford enough nutritious food, such as meat, fish, fresh fruit and vegetables, especially when food prices rise.\n    They may buy only cheap starchy foods or skip meals, leading to undernutrition and deficiency diseases.\n    Physical limitations and living alone. Poor mobility, weak eyesight, arthritis or other illness can make it hard\n    for an elderly person to walk to the shops, carry groceries or stand and cook. Those who live alone may lose\n    interest in cooking and eating. Missing teeth or poorly fitting dentures also make chewing hard, so they avoid\n    foods like meat and raw vegetables. All of this reduces the amount and variety of food they actually eat.","2":"(a) Two functions of food in the body. (2)\n    Supplies energy for activity and warmth.\n    Builds and repairs body tissue (also accept: protects against disease, regulates body processes).\n\n(b) Deficiency disease for each nutrient. (4)\n    (i) Vitamin A: night blindness (also accept xerophthalmia).\n\n\n    (ii) Vitamin C: scurvy.\n    (iii) Iron: anaemia (iron-deficiency anaemia).\n    (iv) Iodine: goitre.\n\n(c)(i) Three adjustments to Kendra's breakfast menu (she is an anaemic adolescent). (3)\n    Replace the coffee with a glass of fresh orange, guava or West Indian cherry juice.\n    Replace the chocolate milk with a non-dairy drink, or serve it at a different time of day.\n    Use wholewheat or iron-fortified bread instead of plain toasted bread, and keep the callaloo or spinach and\n     eggs (or add a little liver or kidney beans).\n\n(c)(ii) One reason for each adjustment. (3)\n    The tannins in coffee stop iron from being absorbed, while the vitamin C in fresh juice helps the body absorb\n     the iron in the callaloo and eggs.\n    Calcium in milk competes with iron and reduces iron absorption when they are eaten at the same meal.\n    Wholewheat or fortified bread supplies extra iron (and B vitamins) to help build haemoglobin, which an\n     adolescent with anaemia needs.\n\n(d) Plan a lunch menu suitable for Jeetu (stroke, hypertension, heart disease and diabetes). (4)\n    Lunch for Jeetu\n    Baked (or grilled) chicken breast without the skin, seasoned with herbs, garlic and lime instead of salt\n    Brown rice with red kidney beans (small portion)\n    Steamed callaloo and carrots\n    Tossed green salad with a lime and olive oil dressing\n    Fresh fruit (half an orange or a slice of paw paw)\n    Water, or unsweetened sorrel or lime juice\n    Why this suits him: it is low in salt and saturated fat (good for blood pressure and heart disease), low in sugar\n    and high in fibre (helps control blood sugar), with controlled starch portions and plenty of vegetables.","3":"(a) Three guidelines for planning meals for your sick grandmother. (3)\n    Serve light, easily digested food that is steamed, boiled, baked or poached, not fried or highly seasoned.\n    Give small, frequent, attractively served meals with plenty of fluids, as appetite is often poor during illness.\n    Include protein for repair, and fruit and vegetables for vitamins and minerals. Follow any special diet the\n     doctor has prescribed (low-salt, diabetic, and so on).\n\n(b) Three reasons why the time available must be considered when planning family meals. (3)\n    If time is short, the planner must choose quick cooking methods (stir-frying, grilling, pressure cooking) or\n     dishes that can be made in advance, so the meal is ready on time.\n    Working parents may have little time on weekdays, so convenience foods, one-pot meals or batch cooking at\n     weekends may be needed.\n    Some dishes, such as stews, roasts, yeast breads and marinated meats, take a long time, so they suit\n     weekends or days when more time is available.\n\n(c)(i) Three guidelines for packing a lunch for a field trip, with a reason for each. (6)\n\n\n    Choose foods that travel well and do not spoil easily, such as sandwiches, baked chicken and whole fruit.\n    Avoid mayonnaise-based salads and creamy foods. Reason: food may be without refrigeration for hours, and\n    warm temperatures let bacteria multiply and cause food poisoning.\n    Pack food in clean, airtight, leak-proof containers and use an insulated bag or ice pack. Reason: this keeps the\n    food fresh, stops it getting squashed or contaminated by dust and insects, keeps it cool, and prevents spills.\n    Include a balanced meal and a drink, plus a napkin or wet wipes. Reason: students need energy and nutrients\n    for a day of activity, water prevents dehydration, and wipes let them clean their hands before eating.\n\n(c)(ii) A suitable packed-meal menu. (4)\n    Wholewheat sandwich with tuna (or chicken), lettuce and tomato\n    Baked chicken drumstick\n    Carrot and cucumber sticks\n    A banana or an apple\n    Bottle of water, or a box of pure fruit juice (kept cold with an ice pack)","4":"(a) Two factors that contribute to food spoilage and contamination. (2)\n    Micro-organisms (bacteria, yeasts and moulds), which multiply in warm, moist conditions.\n    Poor hygiene and improper storage (also accept: enzymes, pests, cross-contamination).\n\n(b)(i) Two methods of food preservation. (2)\n    Freezing\n    Drying (dehydration); also accept pickling, canning, jam-making, salting or smoking.\n\n(b)(ii) A food preserved by each method. (2)\n    Freezing: meat, fish or green peas.\n    Drying: raisins, salted codfish, dried sorrel or herbs.\n\n(c)(i) Explain ONE chemical change when heat is applied to boiled cereal and to toasted bread. (6)\n    Boiled cereal (for example oats or cornmeal porridge): gelatinization of starch. When the cereal is heated in\n    water or milk, the starch granules absorb liquid, swell and finally burst at about 60–80°C. This releases starch\n    that thickens the liquid into a smooth, soft porridge. The cereal also becomes easier to digest, and its raw\n    flavour disappears.\n    Toasted bread: dextrinization (with the Maillard reaction). Dry heat breaks down the starch on the surface of\n    the bread into dextrins, which are shorter, slightly sweet sugar chains. These turn brown and give toast its\n    golden colour, crisp texture and toasted flavour. Proteins and sugars on the surface also react (the Maillard\n    reaction) to add to the browning and flavour.\n\n(c)(ii) The cake came out flat. Explain TWO ways you could have prevented this. (4)\n    Measure ingredients accurately, especially the raising agent. Too little baking powder, or old baking powder\n    that has lost its strength, means not enough gas is made to raise the cake. Too much liquid or sugar can also\n    make the mixture too heavy to rise. Weigh carefully and check that the baking powder is in date.\n    Trap enough air and bake at the correct temperature without opening the door. Cream the fat and sugar until\n    light and fluffy, and fold in the flour gently so the air bubbles are not knocked out. Put the cake into a fully\n\n\n    preheated oven at the right temperature, and do not open the oven door early. A sudden drop in temperature\n    makes the cake sink before its structure has set.","5":"(a) Three principles when using small kitchen equipment. (3)\n    Read and follow the manufacturer's instructions before use.\n    Handle electrical equipment safely: dry hands, no water near plugs, unplug before cleaning or changing\n     blades.\n    Clean the equipment properly after every use and store it in a safe, dry place (also accept: use it only for its\n     intended purpose).\n\n(b) Three principles to conserve fuel, time or energy in the kitchen. (3)\n    Plan meals and dovetail tasks, so everything is prepared before you turn on the stove.\n    Use lids, match pot size to burner size, and lower the flame once food boils. Use a pressure cooker for foods\n     that take a long time.\n    Cook several dishes in the oven at the same time and use residual heat (also accept: tiered steamer, thaw\n     frozen food before cooking).\n\n(c) Choose ONE small electrical device and explain the role of FIVE special features. (5)\n    Device: electric blender\n    Variable speed settings (low, medium, high): let you choose the right speed for the job, slow for mixing drinks\n    and fast for puréeing, which gives better results.\n    Pulse button: gives short bursts of power for chopping or crushing ice, so you can control the texture and avoid\n    overheating the motor.\n    Removable stainless steel blades: sharp blades cut and mix food quickly, and they can be removed for safe,\n    thorough cleaning.\n    Goblet (jug) with measuring marks and a tight-fitting lid: you can measure ingredients straight into the jug,\n    and the lid stops liquid splashing out during blending.\n    Safety interlock or overload cut-out: the motor will not run unless the jug is properly locked on, and it switches\n    off if the motor overheats, preventing accidents and damage. (Also accept non-slip suction feet, cord storage,\n    dishwasher-safe parts.)","6":"(a) Define bulk shopping, consumer and credit. (3)\n    (i) Bulk shopping: buying goods in large quantities at one time, usually at a lower price per unit.\n    (ii) Consumer: a person who buys and uses goods and services.\n    (iii) Credit: buying goods or services now and paying for them later, often in instalments and usually with\n     interest added.\n\n(b) Three rights or responsibilities of the consumer. (3)\n    Right to safety: to be protected from goods that are harmful to health.\n    Right to be informed: to have accurate information (labels, prices) to make choices.\n    Right to redress: to complain and receive a refund or replacement for faulty goods. (Responsibilities: read\n     labels, keep receipts, report unfair practices.)\n\n\n(c) Explain THREE ways exposure to external cuisine negatively affects the habits of Caribbean people. (6)\n    More fast food and processed food. Through tourism, television and international fast-food chains, many\n    Caribbean people now eat burgers, fried chicken, pizza and sugary drinks often. These foods are high in fat, salt\n    and sugar and low in fibre, which contributes to obesity, diabetes and hypertension.\n    Less use of local foods and traditional cooking. Imported foods are seen as more \"modern\" or fashionable, so\n    people use fewer local fruits, vegetables, ground provisions and traditional dishes. This lowers the nutritional\n    quality of the diet, hurts local farmers and increases the region's dependence on food imports (lower food\n    security).\n    Changes in meal patterns. People copy foreign habits such as eating on the go, skipping breakfast and snacking\n    in front of the TV instead of having family meals. This leads to poor food choices, overeating and less family\n    time.\n\n(d) Four reasons why safe kitchen hygiene and safe food handling are important in the restaurant industry. (4)\n    They prevent food poisoning and the spread of disease among customers.\n    They protect the restaurant's reputation, so customers keep coming back.\n    They are required by public health laws. Breaking them can bring fines, closure or loss of licence.\n    They reduce food spoilage and waste, which saves money, and they create a safe workplace for staff."},"2024":{"1":"(a)(i) Define \"diet\". (2)\n    A diet is all the food and drink a person usually eats and drinks each day. It can also mean a special eating plan\n    followed for health or medical reasons, such as a low-salt diet.\n\n(a)(ii) One type of diet used by Caribbean people. (1)\n    Vegetarian diet, for example the Rastafarian \"Ital\" diet (also accept: Hindu vegetarian diet, diabetic diet, low-\n    salt diet).\n\n(b)(i) Three reasons to reduce fats and oils, salt and processed foods. (6)\n    Fats and oils: too much fat, especially saturated fat, raises blood cholesterol. Fatty deposits build up in the\n     arteries, leading to heart disease and stroke.\n    Fats and processed foods: these are high in calories (energy-dense), so eating a lot leads to weight gain and\n     obesity, which is a risk factor for diabetes.\n    Salt and processed foods: too much sodium (salt, MSG, preservatives in processed foods) raises blood\n     pressure (hypertension) and strains the heart and kidneys. Processed foods are also often low in fibre and\n     nutrients.\n\n(b)(ii) Three reasons to eat a variety of locally grown fruits and vegetables daily. (6)\n    They are rich in vitamins (A and C) and minerals (iron, potassium), which protect the body against disease and\n     help keep the immune system strong. Eating a variety supplies a wider range of nutrients.\n    They are high in dietary fibre, which prevents constipation, helps control blood sugar and cholesterol, and\n     makes you feel full, which helps with weight control. They are also low in fat and calories.\n    Locally grown produce is fresher (it travels less, so it loses fewer vitamins) and is usually cheaper. Buying it\n     supports local farmers and improves the country's food security.\n\n(c)(i) Three vulnerable groups other than the poor. (3)\n    Infants and young children\n    Pregnant and breastfeeding women\n    The elderly (also accept: adolescents, the sick or convalescents, people with disabilities)\n\n(c)(ii) Explain TWO factors that may threaten the food security of poverty-stricken people. (4)\n    Low or no income and rising food prices. Poor families cannot afford enough food or a variety of nutritious\n    food. When the price of imported food goes up (much Caribbean food is imported), they buy cheaper, less\n    nutritious foods such as white flour and sugar, or skip meals.\n    Natural disasters and climate change. Hurricanes, floods and droughts destroy crops and livestock and cut off\n    roads. Food becomes scarce and more expensive, and the poor are hit hardest because they have no savings to\n    buy food or rebuild gardens. (Also accept: unemployment, lack of land to grow food, poor education, illness of\n    the breadwinner.)","2":"(a) Complete Table 1. (4)\n    (i) Nutrient: fat (saturated fat or cholesterol) leads to heart disease.\n\n\n    (ii) Protein: a deficiency causes kwashiorkor (or marasmus, protein-energy malnutrition).\n    (iii) Nutrient: vitamin C (ascorbic acid). A lack causes scurvy.\n    (iv) Water: too little causes dehydration (also accept constipation).\n\n(b) One food rich in each nutrient. (2)\n    (i) Calcium: milk (or cheese, yogurt, sardines with bones, dark-green leafy vegetables).\n    (ii) Protein: meat, fish, eggs or peas and beans.\n\n(c)(i) Why protein is essential at TWO named stages of life. (4)\n    Infancy and childhood: the body is growing very quickly, so protein is needed to build new cells, muscles,\n    organs and bones. A lack can cause stunted growth and kwashiorkor.\n    Pregnancy: extra protein is needed to build the baby's tissues, the placenta, extra blood and the mother's\n    growing body tissues (uterus and breasts). (Also accept adolescence for the growth spurt, or convalescence to\n    repair damaged tissues.)\n\n(c)(ii) Why calcium is essential at TWO named stages of life. (4)\n    Childhood and adolescence: bones and teeth are growing and hardening, and peak bone mass is built during\n    the teenage years. Calcium (with vitamin D) is needed to make strong bones and teeth and to prevent rickets.\n    Old age (and women after menopause): bones gradually lose calcium and become thin and weak. Enough\n    calcium slows this bone loss and helps prevent osteoporosis and fractures. (Also accept pregnancy and\n    breastfeeding for the baby's bones and teeth.)\n\n(d) TWO dietary practices that may stop your anaemic friend absorbing enough iron. (2)\n    She drinks tea or coffee with her meals or supplements. The tannins block iron absorption.\n    She takes her iron with milk or calcium-rich foods, or eats few vitamin C foods. Calcium reduces iron\n     absorption, and without vitamin C less plant iron is absorbed. (Also accept: eating mostly plant sources of\n     iron, or a high-fibre/bran diet with phytates.)","3":"(a)(i) Define lacto-ovo vegetarian and vegan. (4)\n    Lacto-ovo vegetarian: a person who does not eat meat, poultry or fish, but does eat dairy products (milk,\n    cheese) and eggs, along with plant foods.\n    Vegan: a strict vegetarian who eats only plant foods. A vegan eats no meat, fish, poultry, eggs, milk or any\n    other animal product (often not even honey or gelatine).\n\n(a)(ii) Two main dishes suitable for a vegan. (2)\n    Stewed peas (red beans) with rice and steamed callaloo, made without meat.\n    Curried chickpeas (channa) and potato with roti, or a vegetable and tofu stir-fry.\n\n(b) For each factor, one reason with an example why it matters when planning meals. (6)\n    (i) Traditions and beliefs: families have customs about which foods are eaten, or not eaten, on certain days or\n    occasions, and a meal that ignores them may be rejected. Example: a family that traditionally has fish on Good\n    Friday, or ham and sorrel at Christmas, expects those foods.\n    (ii) Health of family members: some members may have diet-related conditions that need special food.\n    Example: a diabetic grandfather needs meals low in sugar and refined starch, so the planner should use\n    wholegrains, vegetables and sugar-free desserts.\n\n\n    (iii) Religion: some religions forbid certain foods, so including them would offend or exclude family members.\n    Example: Muslims and Jews do not eat pork, Hindus do not eat beef, and Seventh-day Adventists avoid pork\n    and shellfish.\n\n(c) FOUR strategies in meal planning and preparation to develop positive eating habits in children. (4)\n    Make meals colourful and attractive: cut fruit and vegetables into fun shapes and serve small portions.\n    Involve children in planning, shopping and simple preparation, such as washing vegetables or making a salad,\n     so they are keen to taste what they made.\n    Introduce new foods one at a time, alongside foods they already like. Offer them several times without\n     forcing.\n    Set a good example and eat together as a family at regular times. Limit sugary snacks and drinks, offering fruit\n     and water instead.","4":"(a) Three causes of food contamination and spoilage. (3)\n    Micro-organisms: bacteria, yeasts and moulds.\n    Enzymes naturally present in the food (they cause over-ripening and browning).\n    Pests (flies, rodents, cockroaches) and poor hygiene (also accept: moisture, warm temperatures, oxidation,\n     chemical or physical contaminants).\n\n(b)(i) One difference between perishable and non-perishable foods. (1)\n    Perishable foods spoil quickly (within days) unless refrigerated or frozen. Non-perishable foods keep for a long\n    time, months or years, at room temperature.\n\n(b)(ii) One example of each. (2)\n    Perishable: fresh fish (or milk, meat, ripe fruit).\n    Non-perishable: dried rice (or canned beans, flour, sugar).\n\n(c) Two advantages and two disadvantages of preserving mangoes using the sun. (4)\n    Advantage 1: it is cheap. Sunlight is free, and no special equipment or fuel is needed.\n    Advantage 2: dried mango is light and takes up little space, keeps for a long time without refrigeration, and\n     makes a healthy snack.\n    Disadvantage 1: it is slow and depends on the weather. Rain or cloudy days stop the drying and the fruit can\n     go mouldy.\n    Disadvantage 2: the fruit is exposed to dust, insects and birds, so it can become contaminated. Sunlight also\n     destroys some vitamins (vitamin C, carotene), and the colour may darken.\n\n(d)(i) Explain the heat transfer processes during the boiling of potatoes. (4)\n    Conduction: heat from the burner passes through the metal base of the saucepan to the water touching it.\n    Heat also moves by conduction from the surface of each potato to its centre, which is why large pieces take\n    longer to cook.\n    Convection: water at the bottom of the pot heats up, becomes less dense and rises, while cooler water sinks to\n    take its place. These convection currents circulate the heat through all the water so the potatoes cook evenly.\n    Radiation (minor): some heat radiates from the flame or element to the sides of the pot.\n\n(d)(ii) Two food additives to enhance boiled potatoes. (2)\n\n\n    Salt (a flavour enhancer).\n    Herbs or flavourings such as parsley, garlic or chives, and butter or margarine (also accept black pepper or\n     MSG).","5":"(a) Define \"Universal Product Code (UPC)\". (2)\n    The UPC is the barcode printed on a product's package: a pattern of black bars with a 12-digit number. It is\n    scanned at the checkout to identify the product and its manufacturer, look up its price, and keep track of stock.\n\n(b) Four pieces of information found on a food label. (4)\n    Name and brand of the product\n    List of ingredients\n    Net weight or volume\n    Expiry or best-before date (also accept: nutrition facts, manufacturer's name and address, storage\n     instructions, country of origin)\n\n(c)(i) THREE factors the supermarket should consider when planning its kitchen, with a reason for each. (6)\n    Size of the space and layout (work triangle and flow of work): the kitchen must be large enough for the\n    equipment and staff, and laid out so food moves in one direction from storage to preparation to cooking to\n    serving. This saves time and stops raw and cooked food meeting (cross-contamination).\n    Ventilation, lighting and safety: a large commercial kitchen produces heat, steam, smoke and grease. Extractor\n    hoods, good lighting, fire extinguishers and non-slip floors protect workers from heat stress, fires and falls, and\n    are required by law.\n    Health regulations, hygiene and easy-to-clean surfaces: the restaurant will serve the public, so it must meet\n    public health standards: stainless steel surfaces, tiled walls, enough sinks (including hand-wash basins),\n    drainage and pest control. This prevents food poisoning and lets the business get its licence. (Also accept:\n    budget, storage and refrigeration, water and electricity supply.)\n\n(c)(ii) FOUR measures to keep the microwave oven properly maintained. (4)\n    Clean the inside after each use with a damp cloth and mild detergent, and wipe up spills immediately.\n    Use only microwave-safe containers. Never put metal, foil or metal-trimmed dishes inside.\n    Never run the microwave empty, and do not slam the door. Check that the door seal and hinges are clean and\n     working.\n    Train staff to follow the manufacturer's instructions, and have it serviced regularly by a qualified technician.","6":"(a) Three rules of personal hygiene and three rules of kitchen hygiene. (6)\n    Personal 1: wash your hands thoroughly with soap and warm water before handling food, and after using the\n     toilet, handling raw meat or touching the bin.\n    Personal 2: tie back or cover your hair, keep nails short and clean, and do not wear jewellery when preparing\n     food.\n    Personal 3: wear a clean apron, do not cough or sneeze over food, and cover cuts with a waterproof plaster.\n    Kitchen 1: clean work surfaces and equipment before and after use with hot soapy water or sanitizer.\n    Kitchen 2: use separate chopping boards and knives for raw meat and for cooked or ready-to-eat foods.\n    Kitchen 3: empty and clean bins regularly, keep them covered, and keep pests out of the kitchen.\n\n\n(b) Outline THREE reasons why whisking is the most suitable method for Dave's swiss roll (20 minutes). (6)\n  It is quick. The whisking method uses only eggs, sugar and flour, with no fat to cream or rub in, so the mixture\n  can be made in about 10 minutes and the thin sponge bakes in 7–10 minutes. This fits easily into Dave's 20\n  minutes.\n  It gives the light, flexible sponge a swiss roll needs. Whisking eggs and sugar traps a lot of air, which expands\n  in the oven and raises the cake without needing much baking powder. Because the sponge contains no fat, it\n  stays soft and springy and can be rolled up while warm without cracking.\n  It needs simple equipment and few ingredients. Only a whisk or hand mixer and three cheap ingredients are\n  needed, so it is economical for a class fair. The fatless sponge is also lower in fat and spreads well with jam.\n\n(c) Explain TWO ways the Caribbean has been impacted by world cuisine. (4)\n  New ingredients and dishes from the people who settled here. Africans brought foods such as okra, ackee and\n  callaloo and cooking styles like coo-coo. East Indians introduced curry, roti, dhal and chutneys. The Chinese\n  brought stir-frying, chow mein and fried rice. These have become everyday Caribbean foods.\n  Globalization and fast food. Through tourism, television and international chains (KFC, McDonald's, pizza),\n  Caribbean people now eat more processed, fried and imported foods. This adds variety and convenience, but it\n  has also led to higher rates of obesity, diabetes and hypertension, and to less use of local foods."},"2026":{"1":"(a) Define the term \"vulnerable group\". (2)\n    A vulnerable group is a group of people who are more likely than others to suffer from malnutrition or diet-\n    related problems, because their bodies have higher nutrient needs (growth, pregnancy) or because they have\n    less ability to get or use food (age, illness, poverty).\n\n(b) One nutritional problem for each group. (4)\n    (i) Infants: protein-energy malnutrition (marasmus or kwashiorkor), especially when weaned too early or onto\n     watery, low-energy foods.\n    (ii) Pregnant women: iron-deficiency anaemia (also accept folic acid deficiency, which can cause neural tube\n     defects in the baby).\n    (iii) The elderly: osteoporosis, from too little calcium and vitamin D (also accept constipation, from low fibre\n     and low fluid).\n    (iv) Poverty-stricken individuals: undernutrition, or deficiency diseases such as anaemia, because they cannot\n     afford enough varied food.\n\n(c)(i) Identify Disease A and Disease B. (2)\n    Disease A: diabetes (diabetes mellitus).\n    Disease B: hypertension (high blood pressure), which can lead to heart disease.\n\n(c)(ii) Two ways of controlling the symptoms of obesity. (2)\n    Cut down on energy-dense foods (fried foods, sugary drinks, pastries) and eat more fruit, vegetables and\n     wholegrains, in smaller portions.\n    Exercise regularly, for example 30 minutes of brisk walking most days, so the body uses up more energy.\n\n(d) Discuss TWO factors that can threaten the food security of an 80-year-old who is disabled and lives alone.\n(6)\n    Physical access to food (disability and mobility). Because he is disabled and lives alone, he may not be able to\n    walk or drive to the market or supermarket. He may also find it hard to carry groceries home. If he cannot get\n    to the shops often, he may run out of fresh foods and rely on whatever is in the cupboard, such as crackers,\n    tinned foods and sweet drinks. Over time this leads to a diet lacking in fresh fruit, vegetables and protein,\n    which can cause deficiencies (for example vitamin C deficiency or anaemia) and weight loss.\n    Ability to prepare food and economic access (income and age). At 80 he is probably retired and living on a\n    small pension, so he may not be able to afford enough nutritious food, especially protein foods, which are\n    expensive. His disability may also make cooking difficult or unsafe: standing at the stove, lifting pots, cutting\n    food. He may skip meals or live on snacks. Old age also brings a smaller appetite, dental problems that make\n    chewing hard, and loneliness, which reduces interest in eating. Together these increase his risk of\n    undernutrition, constipation and osteoporosis.\n    (Other acceptable factors: lack of family or community support, chronic illness, medications that affect\n    appetite, and natural disasters cutting off supply.)","2":"(a) Three functions of food. (3)\n\n\n    Provides energy for body activities and warmth.\n    Builds and repairs body tissues (growth and repair).\n    Protects the body and regulates body processes (vitamins and minerals help fight disease and keep the body\n     working).\n    (Also accept social and psychological functions: it brings people together and gives comfort and enjoyment.)\n\n(b) The three macronutrients. (3)\n    Carbohydrates\n    Proteins\n    Fats (lipids)\n\n(c)(i) Discuss ONE reason why the menu may NOT suit Gabrielle, who has iron-deficiency anaemia. (3)\n    The menu is low in iron. Fish (especially white fish), potatoes, carrots, cauliflower, cheese, cream and the\n    crème caramel all supply very little iron. There are no rich iron sources such as liver, red meat, dark-green leafy\n    vegetables (callaloo, spinach) or legumes. In addition, the coffee in the dessert contains tannins and the cheese\n    sauce contains calcium, and both reduce how much iron the body absorbs. So the meal would not help correct\n    her anaemia.\n\n(c)(ii) Two non-starchy vegetable dishes to increase iron intake, with a reason. (3)\n    Steamed callaloo (or spinach) with onions and tomatoes. Dark-green leafy vegetables are rich in iron, and the\n     tomato adds vitamin C to help absorption.\n    Sautéed pak choi (bok choy) with sweet peppers, or a stir-fried broccoli and string-bean dish. These dark-\n     green vegetables supply iron, and sweet peppers are very high in vitamin C.\n\n(c)(iii) Explain TWO precautions when preparing the dish to increase iron absorption. (4)\n    Include a source of vitamin C in the dish or serve it with the meal, for example tomatoes, sweet peppers, a\n    squeeze of lime, or a glass of orange or West Indian cherry juice. Vitamin C changes plant (non-haem) iron into\n    a form the body absorbs much more easily.\n    Do not serve the dish with tea, coffee or milk-based sauces, and do not overcook it. Tannins in tea and coffee\n    and the calcium in dairy foods block iron absorption, so keep these away from the iron-rich dish. Cook the\n    vegetables quickly in very little water (steam or sauté), and do not soak them. This keeps their vitamin C, which\n    helps the iron to be absorbed.","3":"(a) Four factors to consider when planning meals. (4)\n    Nutritional needs of the family members (age, sex, occupation, health).\n    Money available (the food budget).\n    Time and skill available for preparation.\n    Foods in season and available locally (also accept: likes and dislikes, religion and culture, equipment available,\n     occasion).\n\n(b) Two Caribbean food groups. (2)\n    Staples (cereals and starchy fruits, roots and tubers).\n    Legumes and nuts (also accept: foods from animals, fruits, vegetables, fats and oils).\n\n(c)(i) Plan a suitable two-course meal for a graduation dinner. (6)\n\n\n    Main course: Baked chicken breast in a herb and lime sauce, rice and peas, glazed carrots and string beans,\n    tossed green salad, and sorrel or fruit punch to drink.\n    Dessert: Chocolate mousse topped with whipped cream and a strawberry (or pineapple upside-down cake with\n    ice cream).\n    Why this suits the occasion: it is attractive and festive, it covers several food groups (animal protein from the\n    chicken, staples and legumes in the rice and peas, vegetables, and fruit in the drink), it has a variety of colours,\n    textures and flavours, and the dessert gives a special finish suitable for a celebration.\n\n(c)(ii) Four individual table appointments for the menu above. (4)\n    Dinner plate (for the main course).\n    Dinner knife and dinner fork.\n    Dessert spoon and fork (placed above the plate).\n    Glass or tumbler for the drink, and a napkin (also accept: side plate, salad plate, butter knife).","4":"(a) Define \"food contamination\". (2)\n    Food contamination is when harmful substances get into food and make it unsafe to eat. These can be micro-\n    organisms (bacteria, moulds, viruses), chemicals (cleaning agents, pesticides) or physical objects (hair, glass,\n    insects).\n\n(b) Four factors that contribute to food contamination. (4)\n    Poor personal hygiene of food handlers (not washing hands, coughing over food).\n    Cross-contamination, for example using the same board for raw meat and cooked food.\n    Storing food at the wrong temperature (leaving cooked food at room temperature).\n    Pests (flies, rats, cockroaches) and dirty equipment or work surfaces (also accept: unsafe water, poor waste\n     disposal, undercooking).\n\n(c)(i) The importance of each food additive used in baking. (6)\n    Anti-caking agents: stop powdered ingredients such as flour, icing sugar and baking powder from absorbing\n     moisture and forming lumps, so they stay free-flowing.\n    Antioxidants: stop fats and oils in baked goods from going rancid (oxidizing), so the products keep longer and\n     do not develop off flavours.\n    Artificial sweeteners: give a sweet taste with few or no calories. They are useful in baked goods for diabetics\n     and people trying to lose weight.\n    Emulsifiers: help fat and water mix evenly and stay mixed, giving cakes a smooth batter, a fine even crumb\n     and a longer shelf life.\n    Bulking agents: add volume or bulk without adding many calories. They are often used with sweeteners to\n     replace the bulk that sugar normally gives.\n    Raising agents: produce carbon dioxide gas, which makes doughs and batters rise, giving a light, spongy\n     texture (baking powder, bicarbonate of soda, yeast).\n\n(c)(ii) Two observations to show dextrinization and caramelization occurred. (4)\n    Dextrinization: the surface of the bread, cake or pastry turns golden brown. The crust becomes crisp, with a\n     slightly sweet, toasted flavour.\n    Caramelization: sugar on the surface (for example on a sugar-topped bun) turns a rich amber or dark brown.\n     It gives a distinctive caramel or burnt-sugar smell and taste, and a shiny, hard, crunchy coating.","5":"(a) Four factors when planning the layout of a kitchen. (4)\n    The work triangle: sink, stove and refrigerator should be close together with a clear path between them.\n    Size and shape of the room, and how much money is available.\n    Lighting and ventilation (windows, extractor fan).\n    Safety (stove away from doors and curtains), plus enough storage and counter space (also accept: placement\n     of plumbing and electrical outlets, easy-to-clean surfaces).\n\n(b) Two special features when purchasing a pot or pan. (2)\n    Non-stick coating.\n    Heat-resistant handles and a tight-fitting lid (also accept: heavy, thick base for even heating; ovenproof;\n     whistling or pressure feature; pouring lip; measuring marks inside).\n\n(c) Explain TWO ways Brian can conserve fuel while preparing the menu. (4)\n    Use the oven fully (oven-cooking). The roasted chicken and the green fig and cheese pie both need the oven,\n    so bake them at the same time on different shelves, instead of heating the oven twice. Do not keep opening\n    the oven door, as heat escapes each time. Turn the oven off a few minutes before the end and let the leftover\n    heat finish cooking.\n    Use a tiered steamer, or steam over another pot. Boil the green figs in one pot and place a steamer basket\n    with the vegetable medley on top, so one burner cooks two items. Cover pots with lids, use pans that match\n    the size of the burner, and turn the flame down once the water boils. (Also accept: use a pressure cooker to\n    cook the figs quickly, and prepare everything before lighting the stove.)\n\n(d)(i) Identify the accident most likely in each figure. (3)\n    Figure 1: the child pulls the iron's cord and the hot iron falls on him, causing a burn (and a blow to the head).\n    Figure 2: a fall from the step-ladder (stool) while reaching for something on a high shelf, which can cause a\n     fracture, bruising or head injury.\n    Figure 3: a cut to the hand or finger from the knife while chopping.\n\n(d)(ii) Three steps to treat the accident in Figure 3 (a cut). (3)\n    Apply firm pressure on the cut with a clean cloth or gauze, and raise the hand above heart level to stop the\n     bleeding.\n    Once bleeding slows, rinse the cut under clean running water to remove dirt, and apply an antiseptic.\n    Cover with a sterile plaster or dressing. For a deep cut, or one that will not stop bleeding, get medical help.","6":"(a) Four types of information on a food label. (4)\n    Name of the product and brand name.\n    List of ingredients (in descending order of weight).\n    Net weight or volume.\n    Best-before or expiry date (also accept: nutrition facts, name and address of the manufacturer, country of\n     origin, storage instructions, allergy warnings, batch number).\n\n(b) Define \"green consumer\". (2)\n\n\n  A green consumer is someone who thinks about the environment when shopping. They choose products that\n  cause the least harm to the environment, such as items with little or recyclable packaging, organic or locally\n  grown foods, and energy-efficient appliances, and they avoid waste.\n\n(c)(i) Explain THREE benefits of buying food and materials for a party from a supermarket rather than a door-to-\ndoor seller. (6)\n  Wider variety and one-stop shopping. A supermarket stocks a wide range of brands, sizes and types of food,\n  decorations, paper goods and drinks all in one place. You can buy everything for the party in one trip and\n  choose exactly what suits your budget and menu, which saves time and transport costs.\n  Better quality and safety control. Supermarket goods are labelled with expiry dates, ingredients and storage\n  instructions, and are usually stored at the correct temperatures (refrigerators and freezers). Food bought door-\n  to-door may have been kept in the heat with no labels, so there is a greater risk of buying spoiled or\n  contaminated food.\n  Consumer protection and value for money. Supermarkets give receipts, have return or exchange policies, and\n  are regulated by bodies such as the Bureau of Standards and the Consumer Affairs Division. If something is\n  wrong you can get a refund. They also offer special offers, bulk-buy discounts and unit pricing, so you can\n  compare prices. A door-to-door seller may be hard to trace if there is a problem.\n\n(c)(ii) Explain TWO ways product labels can influence the purchase of food and materials for a party. (4)\n  Nutrition, ingredient and allergy information. Reading the label lets the host choose foods that suit the guests,\n  for example low-sugar drinks for diabetic guests, or avoiding products containing nuts for someone with an\n  allergy. Labels showing \"low fat\" or \"no added sugar\" may lead the buyer to choose a healthier option.\n  Quantity, price and date information. The net weight and number of servings on the label help the host work\n  out how many packets are needed for the number of guests, and compare value between brands (unit price).\n  The expiry or best-before date makes sure the food will still be fresh on the day of the party. Attractive\n  packaging and brand names can also tempt shoppers to buy."}});
export const fnhPaper2UploadedWorkedQuestion=(year,number)=>FNH_P2_UPLOADED_WORKED_REFERENCE[String(year)]?.[String(number)]||null;
